zones[947] = { "Ten thousand years ago the land of this world lay together in one continent, ancient Kalimdor. The Highborne drew recklessly on the magic of the Well of Eternity, and the ripples of it reached the Twisting Nether. The Burning Legion took notice. Sargeras tempted Queen Azshara with power beyond measure, if only the Legion were summoned, and the Highborne opened a great gate in the depths of the Well.\n\nMalfurion Stormrage and the night elf resistance saw that the Well had become a doorway for Sargeras himself. Malfurion drew on the Dragon Soul and broke the ritual with a storm. Sargeras and most of his demons were flung back into the Twisting Nether. Then the Well collapsed, and the earth split beneath the survivors. Most of the old continent sank. Ruined continents rose from the wreck, and the drowned heart of the land became the Great Sea. The Maelstrom still turns where the Well once stood.", "Azeroth" }
zones[1411] = { "Cracked and red, Durotar is a land of crags and canyons, dry and hot, sparse in water and green things. It lies on the eastern coast of Kalimdor. The orcs find in it a harsh beauty that recalls Draenor. After the Third War, Warchief Thrall led his people here to build a new homeland, and he named it for his murdered father, Durotan.\n\nPeace did not last. Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore, a hero of the Second War and no friend to orcs, brought a great fleet from Kul Tiras against Durotar, determined to crush the Horde before it could take root. His daughter, Jaina Proudmoore, chose otherwise. At Theramore she turned against her own father to fight beside Thrall, and Daelin died in the battle before the two could reconcile. In gratitude, Thrall let Jaina's soldiers withdraw to Theramore unharmed.\n\nThe Darkspear trolls hold the southern coast as exiles. Their own witch doctor, Zalazane, drove them from their home in the Echo Isles, and they have not been able to take the isles back.", "Durotar_(Classic)" }
zones[1412] = { "The tauren wandered for hundreds of years with no land of their own. Then they met Thrall and his orcs when they landed on Kalimdor, and Cairne Bloodhoof saw kindred spirits with honor and power. With Thrall's escort Cairne led his people into Mulgore, and with the orcs' help the Bloodhoof tribe drove back the centaur and claimed the grasslands.\n\nIt is a sheltered valley, a great pasture of green grass and few trees, walled in by mountains on every side with a single pass leading out. In the middle, the cliffs of Thunder Bluff rise above the fields and throw long shadows across the plain. On that mesa Cairne built a refuge where tauren of every tribe are welcome, and the scattered tribes have united under his rule.\n\nTents and hide huts stand beside windmills that drive the grain mills. The centaur still raid the plains, but the mesa strongholds have held against them. Every tauren who comes of age must walk into the wilds of Mulgore and prove themselves in the Great Hunt.", "Mulgore_(Classic)" }
zones[1413] = { "The dust here was not always dust. This land was forest once, tended by the night elves, and several of their large cities stood here. After the Great Sundering, war and disaster turned the green valley into a sun-scorched plain. It is hot and mostly flat, with scattered hills and a few oases where the water gathers.\n\nFor countless generations the tauren roamed these plains, following the kodo and the thunder lizards. Centaur hunted them for sport. The tauren fought back when they had to, but they had no love of war and would rather strike their tents and move on. A year of peace was always followed by a year of war. Then Thrall's orcs and the Darkspear trolls came ashore and marched beside the Bloodhoof tauren against the centaur.\n\nNow the Horde holds most of it. Orc farms and watchtowers dot the grass, and the Gold Road runs through the land from north to south. Quilboar and centaur raid from every side, and Burning Blade cultists work in the north.", "Barrens_(Classic)" }
zones[1414] = { "When the Highborne were gone, the night elves who stayed on the broken continent chose to shut themselves away. A veil of mist settled over the land and hid it from passing sailors. Among humans, Kalimdor lived on only as an old legend of a country that had broken and sunk beneath the waves. The high elves of Quel'Thalas knew better, since night elves visited them over the long years.\n\nEven when the Third War began, most humans still thought Kalimdor a story. The Kirin Tor's own ambassador at King Terenas' court was among the doubters. A few listened to Medivh's warning and sailed west, among them Thrall's new Horde and the expedition of Jaina Proudmoore. They found a whole continent, with ice and forest in the north and desert in the south. The Horde holds the central and eastern lands now, and the night elves keep to the northwest.", "Kalimdor" }
zones[1415] = { "These lands were once part of ancient Kalimdor, until the Great Sundering split them away as three continents. Azeroth lies in the south and Lordaeron in the north. Khaz Modan sits between them, the ancestral home of the dwarves and gnomes. Its name means Mountain of Khaz, for the titan Khaz'goroth.\n\nIn the Second War the orcs overran Khaz Modan. Every dwarven mountain keep fell except Ironforge, which held. The Bleeding Hollow and Dragonmaw clans occupied the rest, and wildlife grew scarce as despoiled badlands spread. Dwarves who could flee did so, and the others hid underground and resisted.\n\nLordaeron is named for Lordain, a general of the Empire of Arathor who sacrificed himself in the Troll Wars. Humans have long held its northern, western and southern lands, and trolls the east. High elves keep their woods in the far north, and the Forsaken hold the Undercity where Lordaeron's people once lived.", "Eastern_Kingdoms" }
zones[1416] = { "Rain falls on these peaks more often than not, and the sky stays gray under heavy cloud. Forest covers the slopes below snow on the highest ground. At the foot of the mountains, humans and high elves broke the Amani Empire in the final battle of the Troll Wars.\n\nTwo powers once stood here. Alterac was a city-state among the peaks that came to rule much of the northlands. Dalaran, the city of the Kirin Tor, stood by Lordamere Lake. Alterac fell for its treason in the Second War. Dalaran was destroyed in the Third War, and its wizards have opened the dome they raised over it while they rebuild.\n\nCrushridge ogres hold the ruins of Alterac. The Syndicate, rogues gathered by Aliden Perenolde, a prince of the fallen royal house, hold Strahnbrad and the farmland, and the two clash over the rest. Deep in the mountains, the Frostwolf orcs and the dwarves of the Stormpike Guard contest a valley of their own.", "Alterac_Mountains_(Classic)" }
zones[1417] = { "King Thoradin built Strom, the capital of Arathor, in this dry and craggy country. Near it he raised a great wall to shield his people from the Amani trolls. The empire fell, and the Imperial Guard that stayed behind founded Stromgarde in the old city. Thoradin's Wall still runs along the northern edge, collapsed and gaping wide.\n\nStromgarde escaped the Scourge's plague, then fell to the Syndicate and the Boulderfist ogres. The remnant of its army fled the city for a small canyon to the north, now called Refuge Pointe. Prince Galen Trollbane leads the human defenders, and they, the Syndicate and the ogres still fight over the ruined city.\n\nIn the east the Horde holds Hammerfall, a camp for captured orcs that it took over and renamed for the fallen warchief Orgrim Doomhammer.", "Arathi_Highlands_(Classic)" }
zones[1418] = { "The Badlands are cracked dry earth, strewn with boulders and cut by winding canyons, shut in by mountains on every side. Only the hardiest creatures survive here, coyotes and buzzards among them. In the far east, a great canyon is home to black dragon spawn.\n\nDwarf prospectors from Ironforge come to dig in the north, where the ruins of Uldaman were unearthed. The walls hold the first clues to where the dwarves came from. The Stonevault troggs, woken after thousands of years of sleep, slaughtered the explorers and now defend the city as their own. A large Dark Iron fortress stands just south of the ruins, and its dwarves war with the Dustbelcher ogres and the drakes.\n\nIn the northwest is Kargath, the only Horde outpost in Khaz Modan. Warchief Thrall sent its orcs to keep watch on their renegade Blackrock kin.", "Badlands_(Classic)" }
zones[1419] = { "The rocks of the Blasted Lands were once tan. Searing heat and wild energies turned them crimson when the Dark Portal was destroyed, and the land has stayed a desert ever since. Before that it was part of the Black Morass, a dark fen of marshland. Days are hot and nights bitterly cold. Mountains block any moisture from the north, no river runs here, and when storms roll in off the eastern sea the rain races off the packed earth and back to the water.\n\nLightning strikes the ground without end. Basilisks and scorpions have settled the waste, and ogres roam it. A remnant of the Burning Legion under Lord Kazzak has taken refuge here.\n\nA single road enters the land from the north, and the grim mages of Nethergarde Keep guard it.", "Blasted_Lands_(Classic)" }
zones[1420] = { "The sky over Tirisfal Glades hangs gloomy and green. The wooded hills here, on Lordaeron's northern coast, are soaked in the curse of the Lich King's plague, and the trees and brush cling to their last shreds of life.\n\nUnder King Terenas this was a land of quiet farms and small towns, where families lived happily for generations. It was one of the first places to fall when Prince Arthas Menethil ransacked Lordaeron. The people died, rose as slaves of the Scourge, and mass graves were dug to hold the rest. Afterward Lady Sylvanas Windrunner and her free-willed Forsaken took the glades as their own.\n\nThe Forsaken have enemies here. The Scarlet Crusade keeps several outposts, including the Scarlet Monastery, a stronghold of its faith. Scourge minions still foul the derelict farmsteads and mills. In the western hills, Forsaken paladins of Tyr's Watch hold Bandarion Keep and keep faith with the Light.", "Tirisfal_Glades_(Classic)" }
zones[1421] = { "The pines here are tall and silver-barked, and they stand over grassy knolls. Before the Third War the Alliance kept this wood green and whole. Then the Scourge came through, burned the crops and killed the people, and with Lordaeron fallen there was no one left to tend it. The trees went pale and sick, and ghostly mists hang between them. The forest is quiet now, except for a howling that comes and goes. Dilapidated farmsteads and abandoned mines dot the land, home to the darker things of the woods.\n\nThe Forsaken hold the north and fight a constant guerrilla war with the Rot Hide gnolls. Their claim is disputed by the wizards of Dalaran and by Scourge agents who hold some of the ruined fields. A few humans cling to the south, guarded by wizards sent from Dalaran, who also come to study the plague and the worgen curse. In the southwest, the people of Pyrewood Village turn into worgen under the moon, cursed by Archmage Arugal, who keeps to the dark towers of Shadowfang Keep.", "Silverpine_Forest_(Classic)" }
zones[1422] = { "Before the plague, this was forested farmland east of Lordaeron's capital. Hearthglen was the seat of the regional government, and Andorhal gathered the grain of the northern provinces and sent it out across the kingdom.\n\nKel'Thuzad and the Cult of the Damned knew that grain was the quickest road for the plague. They infected it, and they set four massive cauldrons in the fields of the four largest farms. As Lordaeron collapsed, the forests began to die. The pines drop sickly brown needles, and their trunks swell with growths like tumors. A smoky gray mist hangs over the ground.\n\nThe land still seems to fight back, with what little it has left. The Scarlet Crusade holds Hearthglen and strikes at the Scourge from there. The one calm place is Uther's Tomb, a monument to Uther the Lightbringer.", "Western_Plaguelands_(Classic)" }
zones[1423] = { "Before the plague, these lands were farms and small villages, all of them looking to Stratholme, the largest city in Lordaeron. Prince Arthas Menethil came to Stratholme hunting the source of the plague and purged the city. From there he went north, returned as one of the Lich King's death knights, and killed his own father to end the kingdom of Lordaeron.\n\nThe Scourge rules here now. Stratholme is their southern capital, under the lich Kel'Thuzad, though the Scarlet Crusade holds its western districts and fights on in vain, turning outsiders away. The ground is brown and sick, the trees have swollen into giant mushrooms, and the air hangs orange with mist. Necromancers and Damned cultists prowl the roads. Amani forest trolls strike from the north. Only at Light's Hope Chapel does a small Argent Dawn camp welcome anyone willing to fight.", "Eastern_Plaguelands_(Classic)" }
zones[1424] = { "Most of Lordaeron has been twisted by the Scourge, but the Hillsbrad Foothills still look as the kingdom once did. The hills are green, the meadows fertile and the soil rich. Pine trees dot the farmland, and roads cross the pastures from town to town. A river flows south from Darrowmere Lake.\n\nFor all that, this is the most blood-soaked battlefield in Azeroth. The first battle between the Alliance and the Horde in the Second War was fought here. Orc raiders struck the coastal towns, and forest trolls fought beside them against human and high elf defenders. In the Third War Thrall assembled the orcish clans, then slipped into Southshore by night and stole its ships to carry the new Horde across the sea to Kalimdor.\n\nThe Scourge wiped out the town of Tarren Mill. The Forsaken took it as their southernmost holding and now press the humans of Southshore and Hillsbrad Fields, who stand with the Alliance. Syndicate outlaws, murlocs and naga trouble both sides.", "Hillsbrad_Foothills_(Classic)" }
zones[1425] = { "The Wildhammer dwarves hold the west of the Hinterlands, where they tame wild gryphons. The bond grew so strong that they raised an enormous monument in the likeness of a gryphon in their city. The forest trolls who held this land before them, remnants of the Amani Empire, call the dwarves squatters on ancestral ground and raid the aeries.\n\nThe Wildhammers knew almost nothing of the Horde when the orcs marched into the Hinterlands in the Second War. Thane Kurdran Wildhammer's gryphon riders met the invaders from the sky, hurling storm-charged hammers from their mounts and giving the columns no rest. Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer meant the attack to draw the Alliance after him while he struck Quel'Thalas. Anduin Lothar saw it, sent half his army to the elves, and fought beside Kurdran himself. Together they drove the orcs and their forest troll allies out. Kurdran pledged his kingdom to the Alliance.\n\nOf the three troll tribes here, only the Revantusk have taken the Horde's side.", "Hinterlands_(Classic)" }
zones[1426] = { "Snow lies deep across Dun Morogh, and its mountains are the highest in the Eastern Kingdoms. Their glaciers feed the rivers that water the green fields of Khaz Modan, and their peaks give the dwarves ore without end. Mountains close the land in on every side, and dwarven troops watch the few passes in.\n\nIce trolls of the Frostmane tribe once ruled here, until dwarven armies broke their empire and drove them back into the hills. Dwarves and gnomes became close friends. The dwarves helped the gnomes raise Gnomeregan, and the gnomes shared their knowledge of machines. When the orcs invaded Khaz Modan in the Second War, both peoples joined the Alliance, and between dwarven strength and gnomish invention the orcs never took either capital.\n\nNow Gnomeregan has fallen, and its survivors shelter in Ironforge. Troggs are turning up all over the region. The Frostmane see that Ironforge's armies are busy with the troggs and the Alliance's wars, and they raid the dwarves more often.", "Dun_Morogh_(Classic)" }
zones[1427] = { "More than two hundred years ago the ground here was torn open. Before that the land was part of the Redridge Mountains. Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan of the Dark Iron dwarves needed something to turn the War of the Three Hammers his way. He and seven of his most trusted dwarves called on the ancient powers sleeping beneath the world, and what answered was the Firelord Ragnaros, more terrible than any nightmare they had imagined. Blackrock Mountain burst up out of the ridge, the Dark Iron capital of Thaurissan fell to rubble, and the valley to the north became a blackened rift, the Searing Gorge.\n\nThe Dark Irons wanted land for their works, and the gorge had few people and few soldiers, so a large Dark Iron force overran the dwarven garrisons and put slaves to work digging out the middle of the valley. War golems and elementals walk the gorge beside them.\n\nThe Thorium Brotherhood, Dark Iron dwarves who broke from the clan, holds its own town in the north of the gorge.", "Searing_Gorge_(Classic)" }
zones[1428] = { "The sky over the Burning Steppes glows red, lit by eruptions from Blackrock Mountain. Rivers of lava cross the craggy foothills, the earth is charred, and the air stays hot and dry. The road here, the only land passage from Stormwind to Khaz Modan and Lordaeron, is well traveled and dangerous, and Stormwind has all but abandoned it.\n\nThe land was once part of the Redridge Mountains. In the War of the Three Hammers the Dark Iron Sorcerer-Thane, Thaurissan, tried to summon elemental servants and called up Ragnaros the Firelord instead. The mountains melted, and a volcano rose where he came through. The surviving Dark Irons became his puppets.\n\nOrcs settled here after the Second War. The Blackrock orcs keep one of the few lasting orc settlements in Azeroth and do not honor Thrall's truce with the Alliance. Firegut ogres roam the countryside. Black dragons under Nefarian hold the upper levels of Blackrock Spire, and Dark Iron dwarves hold its depths, where rumor says Ragnaros still broods.", "Burning_Steppes_(Classic)" }
zones[1429] = { "In the First War, thousands of orcs marched through Elwynn Forest and closed around Stormwind, leaving the sea as the only way out. Anduin Lothar led the Knights of Stormwind in a charge through the trees and caught the orcish rearguard by surprise. The orcs fled, but the relief did not last. Fire swept much of the forest, and in the second siege Stormwind fell. Lothar led the survivors onto boats bound for Lordaeron.\n\nThe trees have grown back since, hardwoods and lush glades between rushing rivers and lakes, and frequent rain keeps the farmland rich. Loggers, farmers, fishers and miners work it, and Stormwind could not last long without what they send. Here and there an orcish catapult still lies broken and rotting.\n\nThe stonemasons spent years rebuilding Stormwind and were never paid. When Guildmaster Edwin VanCleef demanded what was owed, the nobles ordered the guild disbanded. Riots followed, and out of them came the Defias Brotherhood. Its bandits keep holdings across the forest.", "Elwynn_Forest_(Classic)" }
zones[1430] = { "The Ivory Tower of Karazhan stands at the heart of the pass, which cuts through the mountains of central Azeroth and joins Duskwood to the Swamp of Sorrows. A score of years ago it was counted part of the Redridge Mountains. It was the home of the dark wizard Medivh, who opened the Dark Portal for the orcs and was hunted down and killed in the First War. The tower has stood abandoned since, and its evil magic still leaks into the land.\n\nNothing grows here. The trees are petrified and leafless, and a foul wind keens through the jagged canyon that gives the pass its name. Ogres, demons and undead roam the interior, along with a few human gypsies. Carrion birds are the only wildlife.\n\nNobles from Darkshire came to look into the blight, and none who entered the tower came out again. Travelers say a new power has claimed it, and that the ogres and demons answer to it. The Dark Riders, a mysterious band seen in Duskwood, are known to come from this pass.", "Deadwind_Pass_(Classic)" }
zones[1431] = { "Duskwood was once the southern part of Elwynn Forest, a fiefdom of Stormwind called Brightwood. Two sizable towns stood among its farms. Now a black fog hangs beneath the trees, the trunks have grown gnarled, and the night does not lift.\n\nWorgen came with the fog. The dead walk out of the graveyards, and ogres have pushed in from Deadwind Pass. Stormwind is far away and long ago abandoned the forest, but the people who remain cling to their holdings. Their militia, the Night Watch, patrols the roads and guards Darkshire against skeletal warriors, ghouls, worgen and Defias men.\n\nAt its heart lies an ancient volcano crater, long dead and now full of life, with a twilight gentler than the dark outside. Elsewhere lie abandoned farmsteads and Raven Hill Cemetery, which holds the graves of everyone killed by beasts and undead since the darkness fell.", "Duskwood_(Classic)" }
zones[1432] = { "Gray mountains rise on every side of Loch Modan, their hills lightly wooded with pine and birch. The Stonewrought Dam holds the loch in place, a work of dwarven engineering that has no equal on Azeroth.\n\nQuiet as it looks, the loch has seen war. In the Second War the orcs of the Horde overran this land and made a stand against the defenders of the Alliance. The dwarves of Ironforge held out, and when the war ended they reclaimed the country. Broken catapults and ballistae still lie on the bottom of the loch and on its islands, plain evidence of that fighting.\n\nPeace has not returned. The dwarven digs broke open caves under the ground, and the troggs they unearthed have settled in the mountains. They build crude camps and caves from which to strike at passing travelers. Hostile kobolds, wild beasts and the ogres of the northeast add to the danger.", "Loch_Modan_(Classic)" }
zones[1433] = { "Elms rise over fast rivers in the Redridge Mountains, and the land climbs steadily toward the peaks. The range lies east of Elwynn Forest and south of the Burning Steppes. These hills lie under Stormwind's protection, though since the Second War they have been independent in name. The people are calm and content. They send Stormwind timber from the woods, fish from the lake and crops from the fields, and Redridge is one of the last peaceful regions in Azeroth.\n\nThe peace is imperfect. A force of Blackrock orcs came down from the Burning Steppes and holds Stonewatch Keep. Gnolls and more Blackrock orcs press on the town of Lakeshire from the surrounding hills. The road from the crossroads at Three Corners toward the keep is held by dragon whelps, gnolls, murlocs and wild beasts.\n\nThe surface of Lake Everstill looks calm. Murlocs live beneath it, and people still talk of a sea monster in the deep water.", "Redridge_Mountains_(Classic)" }
zones[1434] = { "For ages the jungle trolls of the Gurubashi ruled this land, and their stone cities lay scattered across it. The empire turned cruel. It sacrificed trolls of other tribes to Hakkar the Soulflayer, a blood god, and a long civil war split the tribes apart for good. The Darkspear went their own way, and those who stayed in the vale have fought one another ever since.\n\nThe cities fell and the jungle closed over them. The Bloodscalp hold the northwest and the Skullsplitters the east, each tribe living among the ruins. Zul'Gurub, once the capital, sits in a valley in the northeast. Naga hunt along the coast, and the strangle-thorns that name the vale tangle every path inland.\n\nOutsiders keep coming. Goblins run a harbor on the southern shore, and pirates make it their port. In the north, Colonel Conrad Kurzen's expedition has raised a compound among the trees, where his men are at war with one another. The trolls watch all of it, and their wish to purge the land of foreigners grows.", "Stranglethorn_Vale_(Classic)" }
zones[1435] = { "Once the northern half of a vast marshland called the Black Morass, the Swamp of Sorrows saw the Dark Portal open in the southern half. The orcs came through and raised two settlements and an outpost in the mire. When the portal collapsed in the Second War, the southern half became the Blasted Lands, and the northern half is now the Swamp of Sorrows.\n\nThis is a stagnant land of sucking bogs and weeping trees. Seawater seeps into the fen, so the pools are brackish and full of crocolisks, and salt crusts the fallen logs and rocks. Bog beasts wade through the interior, and jaguars hunt along the banks, and the one road rises only inches above the water.\n\nSome draenei came through the Dark Portal in search of a new life and settled here. Homesickness drove most of them mad, and they attack all on sight. Far from Stormwind, the Horde has planted a colony of its own. Green dragons patrol the marsh at the command of Ysera the Dreamer, guarding a ruined troll temple beneath its largest bog.", "Swamp_of_Sorrows_(Classic)" }
zones[1436] = { "The wind crosses Westfall's fields with nothing to stop it. This was the breadbasket of Stormwind, rolling pasture and farmland, and now the grass is yellow and thin and carrion birds fill the fields.\n\nThe Stonemasons Guild rebuilt Stormwind after the Second War and was never paid for it. Its guildmaster, Edwin VanCleef, led the masons out into the countryside, and there they became the Defias Brotherhood. The farmers were still shaken from the orc raids and put up little resistance. The Brotherhood drove many of them off and hired Riverpaw gnolls to hold the land. Its men burned the fields and salted the earth, and once goblins joined its ranks, the Brotherhood armed itself with harvest golems that hunt anyone who walks the fallow rows alone.\n\nStormwind, short of gold and soldiers, leaves the region to itself. Gryan Stoutmantle founded the People's Militia, a volunteer force that keeps a last stronghold here against the Brotherhood.", "Westfall_(Classic)" }
zones[1437] = { "Mist hangs low over the Wetlands, and the sky is a flat gray from one end of the country to the other. Small rivers, lakes and ponds run through the ground, mountains close it in on three sides, and the ocean lies open to the west. Dwarven strongholds once stood all across this land. War has left them as rubble, and murlocs, wild beasts and renegade orcs hold it now.\n\nIn the Second War the Dragonmaw clan took the fortress of Grim Batol in the mountains to the east. The orcs and their dragons ate the wildlife thin, and the plants suffered along with it.\n\nThe Dragonmaw lost their hold when the red dragons they had enslaved broke free and burned their bases and catapults. The survivors fled south into the mountains, where they still live. Menethil Harbor, on a small island in Baradin Bay, is the Alliance's only foothold in the Wetlands.", "Wetlands_(Classic)" }
zones[1438] = { "After the Third War, Malfurion Stormrage vanished into the Emerald Dream, and the archdruid Fandral Staghelm took over the leadership of the night elf druids. He told the Circle of Ancients that the time had come to grow a new World Tree and win back the immortality the night elves had lost. Their joined powers raised an immense tree out of the Veiled Sea, and they named it Teldrassil, crown of the earth.\n\nThe night elves made their home in its boughs, where a twilit forest of mighty trees, streams and lakes covers the great limbs, with Darnassus on the western side. But Nozdormu called the wish arrogant and refused to bless the tree. Its growth is flawed, and the immortality never came.\n\nNow a sinister presence has settled over the land. Furbolgs and grell have gone mad, and satyrs have appeared among the trees. The great turtles and threshers of the surrounding sea flee to beach themselves and die on the shores of Darkshore. The source of the corruption is still a mystery.", "Teldrassil_(Classic)" }
zones[1439] = { "The name comes from the mist. A gray haze lies over this coast most days, and rain and wind scour its rocky beaches.\n\nTen thousand years ago the land lay farther inland, and the night elves raised great cities here, Ameth'Aran and Bashal'Aran among them. They fell when the first Well of Eternity was shattered, and the Great Sundering cut a new coastline through the region. Their ruins still stand on the cliffs, and stories say that murlocs and naga are slowly taking them over.\n\nThe few night elves who lived here left during the Third War. After it, a small number came back to revive the port, and they cleared away the ruins they could not restore. Fandral Staghelm also led druids onto this shore, and from it they planted Teldrassil.\n\nThe mountains to the east keep out most of the taint of Felwood, but the rivers carry it down anyway. It turns the land gray and poisons the wildlife, and the furbolgs it reaches fall into a rage that does not leave them.", "Darkshore_(Classic)" }
zones[1440] = { "Rain falls almost without pause in Ashenvale, but the trees are so old and thick that only trickles reach the ground. The night elves hunted these woods after the Great Sundering and never built a city in them. The Sentinels and the ancients, living trees that act as wardens, guard the forest and strike at anyone who cuts a live tree.\n\nThe Horde came in the Third War. Goblins under Neeloc Greedyfingers began felling the trees, and the Warsong clan followed. Cenarius came to restore the cut trees, and Grommash Hellscream, tricked by the masters of the Burning Legion, killed the demigod. Then Archimonde's infernals fell burning from the sky, and undead and demons marched through the forest toward Mount Hyjal.\n\nNow the night elves hold Astranaar and their other refuges in the west. The Horde holds the east from Splintertree Post and cuts timber at the Warsong lumber camps. Demons of the Legion still linger in the forest, and satyrs have taken old elven ruins for their own.", "Ashenvale_(Classic)" }
zones[1441] = { "It is thought that a river once cut this canyon, leaving tall columns of sandstone standing in it, with green forest along its banks. Then the Sundering tore the world apart. The river changed course, the trees died, and the soil turned to orange dust. The lake in the east dried into a vast salt flat that lies below the level of the sea. Despite the barren ground, the sunrise seen from Freewind Post or from the mesas around Darkcloud Pinnacle can be spectacular.\n\nDozens of the stone columns still rise from the canyon floor like a forest. Bridges cross the tops of many of them, so a traveler can get over the canyon without meeting the centaur war bands that patrol below.\n\nThe canyons have long been tauren ground. To keep clear of the centaur tribes that nearly wiped them out, the tauren live on the tips of the high mesas. Now, with the help of the Horde, they are fighting back against the centaur and against the fanatical Grimtotem tribe.", "Thousand_Needles_(Classic)" }
zones[1442] = { "The Stonetalon Mountains keep their holiest ground on the largest peak in the range. Night elves tend a barrow and a glade at the top and commune there with the spirits of nature. The tauren hold the peak sacred too, because the immortal Oracle once lived here and watched the strands of fate as the Earth Mother wove them.\n\nElune blessed a Fountain of Health in these mountains in ancient times. During the Third War, harpies captured the wyverns that nested nearby and fouled the fountain's water. Thrall came against them with Cairne Bloodhoof and his tauren. They broke a band of centaur to win a Glyph of Purification and cleansed the fountain with it.\n\nLower down, the land has been stripped. Goblins of the Venture Company have cut almost every tree in Windshear Crag, filled the air with soot, and spoiled the water with oil and waste, to the fury of the orcs and tauren. The mountain spirits cried out against it, loud enough that the shaman as far off as Orgrimmar heard.", "Stonetalon_Mountains_(Classic)" }
zones[1443] = { "Desolace is a gray, rocky waste littered with bones, under black skies and constant wind. The skulls of ancient kodo stare from half-buried graves, and the husks of long-dead trees are all that is left of a green past. Only the western coast is still lush.\n\nLegend says the centaur came from a union between a son of the demigod Cenarius, Zaetar, and a princess of the earth elementals, Theradras, and that the first khans murdered their father for shame of their misshapen form. Their race was born in the western canyons, from Maraudon. Once the night elves stopped holding them in check, the centaur turned on the tauren and at last drove them out of Desolace.\n\nNow four tribes of centaur, each under its own khan, fight one another as fiercely as they fight outsiders. The Horde and the Alliance have both set up camps here, and the Burning Blade cult has opened portals to bring demons into the land.", "Desolace_(Classic)" }
zones[1444] = { "Where the land around it is barren, Feralas is a jungle of huge, ancient trees. The night elves once spread their power through this forest, and their ruins lie everywhere, overgrown by roots and vines. The greatest is the city now called Dire Maul.\n\nThe Gordunni ogres have made themselves masters of the ruins and of much of the forest, and they have defiled what they hold and left their taint on the land. Raiding parties burn villages and ambush travelers in the southern woods. The Horde fears the clan's growth.\n\nGrimtotem tauren live scattered through the woods, hunted by Horde agents on Belgrom's orders. Naga crowd the islands off the western coast, and silithid hives have lately been found in the forest. One of the four Great Trees that open onto the Emerald Dream grows here, guarded by the green dragons.", "Feralas_(Classic)" }
zones[1445] = { "Dustwallow Marsh was once a high plain of tall grass, grazed by great herds. The Sundering smashed it down into a valley, and water bled up from underground and has kept it wet ever since. Crocolisks, raptors and spiders wait in every pool and bush. In the south, black dragons nest in such numbers that the land is called the Wyrmbog.\n\nWild beasts had the marsh to themselves until the Third War. After the battle at Mount Hyjal, Lady Jaina Proudmoore led her people to the eastern shore and raised Theramore on a rocky island. Her father, Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore, brought the Kul Tiran fleet against the Horde, and the marsh became a battleground.\n\nNeither the Horde nor the Alliance holds much of it now. A few towers and checkpoints stand along the road to the Barrens, and Theramore's garrison is thinning as soldiers desert, for reasons nobody can explain. The only Horde foothold is Brackenwall Village in the northwest, where the Stonemaul ogres live under Overlord Mok'Morokk.", "Dustwallow_Marsh_(Classic)" }
zones[1446] = { "Nozdormu, the dragon of time, is said to have made this land his home since before anyone counted years. The heat and the miles of empty sand suited him. Tanaris has been an endless sea of sand for aeons.\n\nThe land is rolling dunes under a cloudless sky, with sandstorms that come up fast. The south is rougher, with mountains over cave networks that run deep and are barely explored. Hyenas, scorpids, basilisks and silithid are common out in the sand, and drakes sometimes ride the hot air overhead. The silithid have been building hives in the remote regions for a long time. The titans left a holding here too, the ancient city of Uldum.\n\nThe goblins of the Steamwheedle Cartel built Gadgetzan and Steamwheedle Port in this desert. It is as close to their homeland as they can get on the continents.", "Tanaris_(Classic)" }
zones[1447] = { "Autumn never leaves Azshara. The trees along the coast burn orange and red, and past them great cliffs drop to ivory beaches and a shallow sea broken by spires of jagged rock.\n\nThis coast was once part of Zin-Azshari, the night elf capital. When the demons were cast out of the world, the Great Sundering tore the land apart and the sea poured in. Most of the city drowned. A few ragged buildings still perch on the cliffs above ruins among the coral. The night elves who lived through it named the broken coast for Azshara, their queen, driven mad by demonic influence, and they call it cursed.\n\nIn the Third War, Illidan Stormrage came through with his naga and satyrs and wrecked the elven harbors so that no one could follow him. Maiev Shadowsong and her Watchers followed his trail and had to put down the suffering creatures he left behind. Some night elves say demons still lurk beneath the water. Others say the queen survived the drowning and was changed into a hideous creature of the sea.", "Azshara_(Classic)" }
zones[1448] = { "Felwood was once the northern woods of Ashenvale, tended by the night elves and watched over by Cenarius. Then the Burning Legion pressed toward Mount Hyjal. Archimonde ordered the forest corrupted to weaken the night elves who defended it, and the dreadlord Tichondrius carried out the order with the Skull of Gul'dan, a warlock relic. Illidan Stormrage braved the forest, defeated Tichondrius and took the skull. His brother Malfurion banished him for using demonic power, but the taint stayed in the land.\n\nNothing grows in the meadows now. The ancients that once guarded the woods roam blindly, their twisted limbs bleeding fel poison, and they turn their worst fury on anything that looks like an elf. Plant eaters refuse the cursed vegetation, so the beasts feed on each other and are mad with rage. Furbolgs ruined by the corruption wander the trees, and satyrs gather at tainted moonwells. The night elves are ashamed that they failed this forest, and they have not stopped trying to heal it.", "Felwood_(Classic)" }
zones[1449] = { "Three deserts press in around Un'Goro Crater and cut it off almost entirely from the rest of the world. Even so, a thick jungle of exotic plants and stranger beasts fills it. Peaceful earth elementals walk among raging devilsaurs. A volcano rises at the center, where fire elementals prowl, and strange crystals dot the ground. Few know their purpose, though some of the scientists camped here study them.\n\nPylons stand on the ridges above, and close examination has not explained them. The Qiraji call this crater the God Lands, and it is ancient past all living memory.", "Un'Goro_Crater_(Classic)" }
zones[1450] = { "Moonglade lies cradled in a ring of mountains, a hidden vale of hills and old forest that corruption has never touched. Countless mounds rise among the trees, and beneath them lie the sanctums of druids past.\n\nCenarius once dwelled here. In the War of the Ancients, as the Burning Legion's numbers swelled, Malfurion Stormrage saw that the night elves could not win alone. He brought Tyrande Whisperwind and his brother Illidan into the sacred quiet of the valley to beg Cenarius for aid. The demigod answered. After the Great Sundering, the keepers of the grove and the dryads who had sheltered here went out into the world.\n\nSome years after the Third War, Moonglade was opened to all the druid races, and Keeper Remulos became its guardian. Tauren and night elves live side by side, and the Moonglade Wardens allow no quarrels between them. The mad demigod Omen sleeps in the depths of the central lake and stirs each year around the Lunar Festival.", "Moonglade" }
zones[1451] = { "Sand and broken rock cover the southwestern corner of Kalimdor, and the only things that rise from the waste are the huge hives of the silithid.\n\nA part of the old Aqiri empire lies here, the land called Ahn'Qiraj. It stands uninhabited now, and its central temple is sealed. At the end of the War of the Shifting Sands the night elves and the bronze dragons shut the Qiraji behind the Scarab Wall, and the desert has held that prison for thousands of years. The Qiraji's servants, the silithid, were not sealed in. They have multiplied and spread across southern Kalimdor.\n\nThe Cenarion Circle keeps a constant watch from Cenarion Hold. The bronze dragonflight, Nozdormu's brood, has returned to the desert for the first time in ten thousand years.", "Silithus_(Classic)" }
zones[1452] = { "Above Moonglade the mountain climbs through a thick bank of white cloud, and beyond it lies Winterspring. The air is crisp and clean up here. Snow covers the ground, and when it is not falling the sun often shines.\n\nThe trees were bent into odd shapes during the Battle of Mount Hyjal, and roots of the World Tree stand exposed, towering overhead like great oak arches. Thousands of years ago, after the Great Sundering, night elves came to this valley to begin again. An older quarrel runs through it, between malevolent blue dragons and the white frostsaber cats that prowl the slopes.\n\nConflict still shakes the valley. The blue dragonflight's forces, among them General Colbatann, clash with the night elves, who want them gone from the slopes of Mount Hyjal. Demons have taken over the southern parts of the land completely. Goblins hold the trading town of Everlook.", "Winterspring_(Classic)" }
zones[1453] = { "Orcs of the Horde burned Stormwind in the First War. After the Second War its people came home to the ruins, and the Stonemasons' Guild rebuilt the city in white stone. The work cost more than the crown could pay. The nobles had run up a huge debt, spreading the army through Elwynn and into Stranglethorn, and the guild's fees went unpaid.\n\nWhen Guildmaster Edwin VanCleef demanded them, the House of Nobles ordered the guild disbanded. Riots followed, and in them Queen Tiffin Wrynn was killed by accident. King Varian Wrynn vowed to punish those responsible and dealt with the rioters harshly. The stonemasons fled the city and hid in Westfall, where they became the Defias Brotherhood.\n\nKing Varian has since vanished under suspicious circumstances. His young son, Prince Anduin, was given the crown so that order could be kept, and Highlord Bolvar Fordragon serves as regent and commands the kingdom's forces.", "Stormwind_City" }
zones[1454] = { "Soon after the Battle of Mount Hyjal, Warchief Thrall led his people to a bare stretch of Kalimdor's coast. He named the land Durotar for his late father, Durotan, and the fortress he raised there he named Orgrimmar, for his friend and mentor Orgrim Doomhammer. It was built fast and built to hold. Stout walls, massive gates and tall towers guard it, and the mountains at its back were carved into parts of the fortress itself.\n\nThe curse on the orcs had ended, and the Horde was no longer a juggernaut bent on war. Thrall's people wanted to survive and prosper. Goblins, tauren and Darkspear trolls helped raise the city, and so did the Mok'Nathal Rexxar. Centaur, harpies and quilboar gave the builders trouble, but the city thrived.\n\nOrcs and Darkspear trolls live here now, with tauren and the Forsaken the Horde has lately accepted. Old shaman pass their knowledge to the Horde's newest leaders, and razor-winds sweep the valleys hard enough to tear the roofs from poorly built houses.", "Orgrimmar" }
zones[1455] = { "The first dwarves walked out of Uldaman and went west to the mountains of Dun Morogh. They dug into the tallest peak, raised an altar to Khaz'goroth, the titan they called their father, and built the Great Forge beneath it. A city grew around the fire and took its name from the forge.\n\nIronforge is a cavern cut into the mountain, its halls laid out in rings around the Great Forge at its heart, with smaller levels deeper underground. Falls of molten rock run down the walls, shops are built into the stone, and hearths roar in every inn. The feel of the place is bustling, rowdy and industrial, yet cozy.\n\nWhen the Horde swept over Khaz Modan in the Second War, one dwarven stronghold after another fell, and the survivors of each fled to Ironforge. Kilrogg Deadeye and his Bleeding Hollow clan fought the dwarves for months and could not take the city. King Magni Bronzebeard rules here now, and the gnomes driven out of Gnomeregan have been given a quarter of their own, Tinker Town.", "Ironforge" }
zones[1456] = { "For countless generations the tauren wandered, and the centaur hunted them across the plains. Then Warchief Thrall and the Horde came to Kalimdor. With their help Cairne Bloodhoof and his tribe drove the centaur back and took the grasslands of Mulgore. For the first time in hundreds of years the tauren had land of their own.\n\nCairne built his refuge on a row of windswept mesas, high above the grass. Elevators of rope and wood, an invention unique to the tauren, lift people from the foot of the bluffs, and long bridges of the same make cross the gaps between the mesas. Tents, longhouses and painted totems stand on every summit.\n\nTauren of every tribe are welcome here, and the scattered tribes have come together under Cairne's rule. A few disagree with him about the direction the new nation should take. All of them agree that no one is wiser than he is.", "Thunder_Bluff" }
zones[1457] = { "Immortality ended for the night elves after the Third War, and many could not bear aging, disease and frailty. A number of druids set out to win it back by planting a great tree that would join their spirits to the eternal world again. With Malfurion Stormrage missing, Fandral Staghelm, who led them, became Archdruid. The tree, Teldrassil, grew above the clouds off the stormy coast of northern Kalimdor. In its twilight boughs the city of Darnassus took root, built to honor the survival of the night elves.\n\nThe city stands around a lake and is open to the sky. Graceful bridges cross the water and keep the buildings far apart, so nothing feels crowded. Lodges of worked wood and tended groves line the shores, and fallen leaves cover the paths. The Temple of the Moon rises above the trees like a beacon. High Priestess Tyrande Whisperwind rules from it, and the Sentinels keep watch over the city. It remains a tranquil refuge, and a monument to a people learning to live with mortality.", "Darnassus" }
zones[1458] = { "The Undercity was old before the Forsaken claimed it. Far beneath the palace of Lordaeron lay the ancient crypts and catacombs of its royal dead, along with the city's dungeons and sewers, and the poorest of Lordaeron's people lived there among the prisoners and the dead.\n\nArthas Menethil meant the Undercity to be the Scourge's seat of power in Lordaeron, but the Lich King called him away before it was built. As the Lich King's grip loosened, Lady Sylvanas Windrunner led a group of free-willed undead out of the Scourge. She lured Arthas into a trap, though he escaped. When the fighting over Lordaeron was done, she named her followers the Forsaken and claimed the ruined capital as their own.\n\nThe Forsaken spent years finishing the maze of catacombs the Scourge had begun. Rivers of poisonous sludge run through every avenue, and the fumes are almost unbearable for the living members of the Horde. For Sylvanas and her cursed people it is a refuge in a world that fears and hunts their kind.", "Undercity" }

areas[1411] = {
  ["bladefist bay"] = { "Rubble from the sea washes in on every tide, and the water has been called the Great Sea's midden heap. The bay lies off the northeastern shore of Durotar, around Skull Rock, and the coast of Azshara can be seen across the water to the north. It carries the name of Kargath Bladefist, a chieftain of the Shattered Hand clan.", "Bladefist_Bay" },
  ["burning blade coven"] = { "Dusty red mountains form the northern border of the Valley of Trials, and a cave there leads into abandoned caverns. The orc warlock Yarrog Baneshadow leads the Burning Blade Coven, and some say members of the Burning Blade, a cult of demon worshippers, wait within. Imps and other weak demons spill out of the cave mouth into the valley, and stronger ones are said to lurk deeper in.", "Burning_Blade_Coven" },
  ["darkspear strand"] = { "The Darkspear trolls came ashore here when they were driven from the Echo Isles. A few steps inland, Vol'jin founded a fishing village and named it Sen'jin Village for his father. The survivors gathered there to begin again.", "Darkspear_Strand" },
  ["deadeye shore"] = { "Deadeye Shore takes its name from Kilrogg Deadeye, the orc hero who lost an eye and once led the Bleeding Hollow clan. A great patch of sand torn from the desert here is shaped like a skull missing an eye, as if the land bore the wound his name recalls.\n\nLittle disturbs the shore now but raptors, scorpids and boars. The orc fisherman Byrok was once attacked here by the Burning Blade.", "Deadeye_Shore" },
  ["den"] = { "Zureetha Fargaze sends promising young orcs and trolls out into the world from this cave in the cliff, near the middle of the Valley of Trials. Recruiters and those who teach the young live and work here with the basic comforts of a camp, and grunts guard the way in.", "The_Den" },
  ["drygulch ravine"] = { "A river runs through Drygulch Ravine, which lies sunk below the land around it. Dustwind harpies nest on its cliffs and have taken to banditry. Caravans lose their supplies to them, and the harpies hoard the plunder up on the high cliffs, where the strongest guard the eggs. The only way in is a small cave from Razorwind Canyon, or a fall from above.", "Drygulch_Ravine" },
  ["dustwind cave"] = { "New recruits to the Burning Blade cult come to a small cave atop the cliffs of Razorwind Canyon, northeast of Razor Hill, to train under its cultists.", "Dustwind_Cave" },
  ["echo isles"] = { "Raptors and lynxes prowl these tropical islands off the southeastern coast of Durotar, which were the home of the Darkspear trolls. At the height of the Gurubashi Empire, jungle trolls ruled lands that reached from Stranglethorn Vale to these isles.\n\nThe Darkspear trolls had barely settled when Zalazane, a witch doctor of their own, betrayed them. He had learned the secrets of undeath, and he turned zombies and other minions on his own people. Vol'jin and the survivors fled to the mainland shore. Zalazane holds the isles still. He has buried tainted fetishes among his huts on the main isle, and they twist the flow of magic through Durotar and corrupt the wildlife of the land. His undead trolls cross to the mainland to hex more of the tribe and drag them under his sway.", "Echo_Isles" },
  ["hidden path"] = { "A secret route climbs through the mountains that border the Valley of Trials, and shaman who are just beginning their path follow it to Spirit Rock. From the highest point the Barrens spread out below.", "Hidden_Path" },
  ["jaggedswine farm"] = { "One of several farms near the front gates of Orgrimmar, this farm keeps a large pen of swine beside a pond and a storage house.", "Jaggedswine_Farm" },
  ["kolkar crag"] = { "Warlord Kolkanis leads the Kolkar centaurs who hold this craggy gorge in southern Durotar. He plots an attack on the orc towns of the land, from the Valley of Trials and Sen'jin Village to Razor Hill, and he means to carry it as far as the gates of Orgrimmar.", "Kolkar_Crag" },
  ["razor hill"] = { "The forces of Kul Tiras arranged a summit with Thrall on this hill, shortly after the Third War. It was an ambush. Rexxar suspected as much and insisted on going in Thrall's place, and he defeated the assassins who were waiting.\n\nOrcs now keep a town at the crossroads here. Orgnil Soulscar watches over it, tracking the movements of the Burning Blade in the area.", "Razor_Hill" },
  ["razor hill barracks"] = { "The barracks is the largest building in Razor Hill. Its size marks the town as one of the main settlements of Durotar, after Orgrimmar.", "Razor_Hill_Barracks" },
  ["razormane grounds"] = { "Bones stripped of their flesh litter the Razormane Grounds, and brambles burst out of the red mountains above. This land once belonged to the Razormane quilboar, and Warchief Thrall's orcs drove many of them away. Now the quilboar press back in from the Barrens, and the orcs fight to keep them out of their homeland. For every quilboar cut down, two more seem to come out of the thickets. Geolord Mottle leads the encampment, and Captain Flat Tusk commands its Battleguard.", "Razormane_Grounds" },
  ["razorwind canyon"] = { "The Dustwind harpies nest in Razorwind Canyon, which lies in two halves on either side of the road between Razor Hill and Orgrimmar. The bird women are many and aggressive. A traveler who is not ready for a fight can be quickly subdued or killed. A cave in the eastern half opens into Drygulch Ravine.", "Razorwind_Canyon" },
  ["rocktusk farm"] = { "The pigs of Rocktusk Farm feed the Horde. The farm lies just down the western road outside the gates of Orgrimmar, and its owners feel fairly safe there.", "Rocktusk_Farm" },
  ["scuttle coast"] = { "Ships from Kul Tiras lie smashed on the rocks of the Scuttle Coast, on the east side of Durotar looking out to the Great Sea. They were wrecked in the battles between the orcs and Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore's fleet. The orcs still go through the stores in the hulls and pull out bits of gnome technology.", "Scuttle_Coast" },
  ["senjin village"] = { "Vol'jin led the Darkspear trolls to this stretch of the southern coast after the witch doctor Zalazane, one of their own, betrayed them and drove them from the Echo Isles. He named the fishing village after his father Sen'jin, who fell saving his people from murlocs in the Third War. Three very small huts, two small ones and one big hut stand beside a dock.", "Sen'jin_Village" },
  ["shrine of the dormant flame"] = { "The Shrine of the Dormant Flame is most holy to all shaman of the Horde. A secret path spirals up the tallest mountain of the region, from near the Southfury River northwest of the Valley of Trials, and the summit looks out over the whole land. A brazier waits there, unlit. A shaman proves mastery of fire by defeating a fire elemental and lighting the brazier with its ember. Telf Joolam, the keeper of the shrine, guides those who come to try.", "Shrine_of_the_Dormant_Flame" },
  ["skull rock"] = { "The orc warlock Gazz'uz leads a band of the Burning Blade from inside Skull Rock, a cave in northeastern Durotar. It is one of several places in the region where the cult has held out.", "Skull_Rock" },
  ["southfury river"] = { "Beginning in a lake on Mount Hyjal, the Southfury drops through a set of great waterfalls into the lands below. It runs wide and swift, and it may be the most prominent river in Kalimdor. It divides Ashenvale from Azshara, and farther south it parts the Barrens from Durotar. Three bridges cross it, one from Azshara into Ashenvale, one into Orgrimmar and one from Durotar into the Barrens.", "Southfury_River" },
  ["spirit rock"] = { "Spirit Rock stands in the hills high above the Valley of Trials, reached by the Hidden Path, and it is sacred to the shaman of the Horde. Aspiring shaman drink a sapta near the stone to commune with the earth.", "Spirit_Rock" },
  ["thunder ridge"] = { "In a gorge of northwestern Durotar, southwest of Orgrimmar, ancient trees once grew thick, and the thunder lizards that denned among them lived in step with the forest. Then a human lumber camp began stripping the ridge, and the lizards went mad. Rexxar, sent to gather shimmerweed for Thrall's farseer Drek'Thar, saw the change in the beasts. Together they found the camp and had to put the lizards down to prevent a disaster. Rexxar gathered their eggs for Drek'Thar to study.\n\nNow a band of Burning Blade cultists under Fizzle Darkclaw camps in the gorge, spreading chaos.", "Thunder_Ridge" },
  ["tiragarde keep"] = { "Tiragarde Keep rises white against the red rock of the Scuttle Coast, a stronghold that should never have stood in Durotar at all. Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore had it raised in haste when he brought his fleet from Kul Tiras to make war on the orcs. The attack was thrown back and the keep fell. Daelin died at Theramore, but orders he had left behind sent Lieutenant Benedict to retake the ruin. Kul Tiras marines and sailors now patrol its crumbling ramparts, well trained and full of contempt for the Horde. Young Horde warriors are sent again and again to test themselves against them, and none has driven them out. Sometimes the soldiers rob Razor Hill.", "Tiragarde_Keep" },
  ["torkren farm"] = { "Misha Tor'kren runs this pig farm in northwestern Durotar. It has never made as much profit as the other pig farms nearby. Her son Kron went west some days ago to hunt crocolisks along the Southfury River, and he has not come back.", "Tor'kren_Farm" },
  ["valley of trials"] = { "Young orcs and trolls of the Horde begin here, in a walled bowl of scorched earth in southern Durotar. They are put through rites of passage, and those who fail and die are left to bake in the sun. To the north the Burning Blade has taken over old caves, and rumor says the Shadow Council has gained a foothold in the region.", "Valley_of_Trials" },
}
areas[1412] = {
  ["baeldun digsite"] = { "A dwarven expedition from Bael Modan has cut a large quarry into the mountainside at the western edge of Mulgore. Baine Bloodhoof warned the dwarves off, for he saw the digging as an insult to the Earth Mother. Talks went nowhere, and the Bloodhoof tribe means to drive the dwarves out.\n\nThe dwarves refuse to listen and keep on digging. Their quarry is a scar cut into Mulgore's earth.", "Bael'dun_Digsite" },
  ["bloodhoof village"] = { "Drums made in Bloodhoof Village are unlike any others, and the village is known for the craft. It stands in the central plains of Mulgore around the waters of Stonebull Lake and takes its name from the Bloodhoof tribe. The wild kodo of the plains are tamed here until they will carry a tauren rider.\n\nBaine Bloodhoof, son of Cairne, leads the village and sees to its defense. Zarlman Two-Moons looks after the spiritual needs of the tribe and of the young tauren who pass through.", "Bloodhoof_Village" },
  ["brambleblade ravine"] = { "The Bristleback quilboar hold Brambleblade Ravine, on the northeastern edge of Red Cloud Mesa. Their raids on the tauren come from here. Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle lives in a large hut toward the back of the ravine.", "Brambleblade_Ravine" },
  ["camp narache"] = { "Camp Narache is the chief village of the Hawkwind tribe, on the northern edge of Red Cloud Mesa in southern Mulgore. Chief Hawkwind trains the young tauren here and sends the promising ones out into the world. Grull Hawkwind welcomes each newcomer, for news of an arrival spreads quickly among the tribe.\n\nThe chief's mother, Greatmother Hawkwind, is the elder of the camp. She keeps to the well out on Red Cloud Mesa.", "Camp_Narache" },
  ["golden plains"] = { "Great herds of kodo gather on the Golden Plains, a basin of shifting green grass southeast of Thunder Bluff. The tauren count it among the most treasured parts of their homeland and one of their favorite hunting grounds. Somewhere in the grass, it is said, the famous kodo Arra'chea makes her home.", "Golden_Plains" },
  ["kodo rock"] = { "A natural monument of stone, Kodo Rock stands about halfway between Camp Narache and Brambleblade Ravine. It cannot be seen from far off, so a traveler has to look closely to find it.", "Kodo_Rock" },
  ["palemane rock"] = { "The gnolls of the Palemane tribe live in Palemane Rock, a cave that runs deep into the mountainside in western Mulgore. They keep a camp outside as well as the dwelling within. They are aggressive and attack anyone who strays too near.", "Palemane_Rock" },
  ["ravaged caravan"] = { "On the northeastern shore of Stonebull Lake a Venture Company caravan lies burned on a mound, and black smoke still rises from the wreck. It was carrying supplies to Supervisor Fizsprocket's mining operation when the tauren, who had seen goblins traveling all over Mulgore, attacked it. The goblins who survived stay with the wreck. They guard the smoldering cargo and drive off anyone who comes close.", "Ravaged_Caravan" },
  ["red cloud mesa"] = { "Red Cloud Mesa rolls out as open plain on top of the high cliffs of southern Mulgore, known for its quiet beauty. Camp Narache stands in its northwest corner, the main training camp for the young of the tauren. They prove themselves here against the Bristleback quilboar, who hold Brambleblade Ravine to the northeast.", "Red_Cloud_Mesa" },
  ["red rocks"] = { "Red sandstone rises in the northeast of Mulgore. The tauren send their most valiant dead here, to the Earth Mother and Sky Father, by cleansing flame. Only warriors who helped found and defend Thunder Bluff, or gave their lives for their tribes and chieftains, are given this honor. Young braves also come to the stones to enter adulthood and listen for the blessings of their ancestors on the wind. Bristleback quilboar have lately broken in and fouled the holy ground, and the tauren of Thunder Bluff are furious.", "Red_Rocks" },
  ["rolling plains"] = { "Wolves, kodo, tallstriders and cougars share the grass of the Rolling Plains, which cover much of southern Mulgore and run from Bloodhoof Village to the border of the Barrens. Centaur outriders wander the open fields. A nest of Windfury harpies sits in the eastern part.", "Rolling_Plains" },
  ["stonebull lake"] = { "A long horseshoe of cool water wraps around three sides of Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore. The fish are plentiful, and many hours go to casting lines into the lake. A bridge crosses it at the narrowest point, and the road over the bridge links Thunder Bluff to the village.", "Stonebull_Lake" },
  ["thunderhorn water well"] = { "In the open grass beside the north road between Thunder Bluff and Bloodhoof Village stands the Thunderhorn Water Well, one of three sacred wells of the tauren. The Venture Company has taken it to control the water supply.", "Thunderhorn_Water_Well" },
  ["venture co mine"] = { "Mining rights in Mulgore were refused to the Venture Company, and the company dug anyway. Its mine cuts into the eastern cliffs, and Supervisor Fizsprocket, the goblin in charge of the company's work in Mulgore, runs it from the main entrance. The tauren are angry at the intrusion.", "Venture_Co._Mine" },
  ["wildmane water well"] = { "North of Thunder Bluff lies the northernmost of the sacred water wells of the tauren, the Wildmane Water Well. The Venture Company has taken it as well.", "Wildmane_Water_Well" },
  ["windfury ridge"] = { "Harpies nest in the heights of Windfury Ridge, and they swoop down to kill or carry off anyone who strays near their nests. Wild cougars and wolves roam the hills around. The tauren of Thunder Bluff still hunt the harpies, because their brilliant feathers decorate weddings and feasts.", "Windfury_Ridge" },
  ["winterhoof water well"] = { "The Winterhoof Water Well lies at the edge of the Rolling Plains in southern Mulgore. Years after the Third War, the Venture Company seized it and poisoned its waters to choke off the tauren's supply. Mull Thunderhorn crafts totems to cleanse the taint, but the well is not yet pure.", "Winterhoof_Water_Well" },
}
areas[1413] = {
  ["agamagor"] = { "The Bristleback quilboar hold a large settlement here, north of the road that runs west from Camp Taurajo. They dig blood shards out of the ground and carry them into battle as totems of strength and magic.", "Agama'gor" },
  ["bael modan"] = { "The stone fortress of Bael'dun Keep rises beside the old village of the Stonespire tauren, and next to it the dwarves of Ironforge have opened a digsite. Beneath the hill lies an ancient titan research complex. The dwarves are digging toward it, and its relics lie mostly unclaimed in the dark.\n\nThe dwarves dig with explosives, and the Stonespire held that ground holy. Most of the tribe was killed or driven off, and the old village is torn apart by digging machines. Gann Stonespire, a tauren who walks the roads nearby, tried to settle it by talking and got nowhere. He now seeks revenge.", "Bael_Modan" },
  ["baeldun keep"] = { "General Twinbraid, a cunning commander, rules the dwarven fortress that overlooks the Bael Modan digsite in the southern Barrens. The dwarves under him are hostile to the Horde. Bael'dun soldiers guard the keep, and it has flying machines of its own.", "Bael'dun_Keep" },
  ["blackthorn ridge"] = { "Thorns crowd this valley just north of Razorfen Kraul, the quilboar capital, and Razormane quilboar hold the ground. On a hill at the northern end of the ridge an orc shaman named Brine has her camp. She is an expert on the spirits and nature of water. Just below the camp lies a pool of notably clear and pure water.", "Blackthorn_Ridge" },
  ["boulder lode mine"] = { "The Venture Company holds this mine in the northeast of the Barrens, and Boss Copperplug runs it. Copperplug was once the best engineer of the Tinkers' Union, until he went over to the Venture Company and took his best techniques with him.\n\nOrcish timbers and Horde banners still hang about the galleries, and the mine seems to have belonged to the Horde once.", "Boulder_Lode_Mine" },
  ["bramblescar"] = { "A tribe of Razormane quilboar has its lair at Bramblescar. Across the road at Agama'gor the Bristleback tribe holds a settlement, and the two tribes are at war. The Razormane attack anyone who wanders close to their lair.", "Bramblescar" },
  ["camp taurajo"] = { "Tauren keep an outpost here in the Barrens, near the mountain pass that leads into Mulgore. It has an inn for travelers coming out of the tauren homeland. The camp also holds a prisoner, the Razormane quilboar Mangletooth, whom the orcs and tauren captured and who awaits his execution.", "Camp_Taurajo" },
  ["crossroads"] = { "Two roads cross here, the Gold Road running north and south and the road that runs from Ratchet to the Stonetalon Mountains. The Crossroads is the largest Horde town in the Barrens, with few lodgings and a great deal of trade, and traders shout their wares across the stalls.\n\nThork, an orc, leads its defense. Raptors once made off with a shipment of silver from the guard tower, the payroll meant for the guards. Just past the settlement lies a burial ground where the Horde pays respects to its dead.", "Crossroads" },
  ["dreadmist den"] = { "Within Dreadmist Peak the Burning Blade keeps a den, shrouded in a dark red mist and an unending twilight. The cultists who live there worship demons, and the mist cloaks them.", "Dreadmist_Den" },
  ["dreadmist peak"] = { "Northwest of the Crossroads, Dreadmist Peak climbs into a red fog. The mist thickens toward the summit and is all that remains of an ancient demonic curse. Burning Blade cultists make their home at the top.", "Dreadmist_Peak" },
  ["dry hills"] = { "Despite their name, the Dry Hills are among the greener parts of the Barrens. They lie in the far northwest corner, the foothills of the Stonetalon Mountains. Witchwing harpies hold them under Serena Bloodfeather and have lately raided the caravans on the northern Gold Road. An abandoned orcish watch tower and a few catapults stand in the middle of the hills.", "Dry_Hills" },
  ["far watch post"] = { "Just west of the bridge between Durotar and the Barrens, the Horde keeps a watch tower and a few catapults. An orc burrow stands beside them, and two peons tend a riding wolf pen a short way south. It is not a comfortable posting. Supply caravans depend on it, and it helps keep Durotar safe from the Alliance and the quilboar.", "Far_Watch_Post" },
  ["field of giants"] = { "Herds of thunder lizards and kodo roam the Field of Giants, across the southernmost plains of the Barrens. The southern leg of the Gold Road runs through them, less well defended than the north. Its watch towers look abandoned.\n\nThe Horde has not given up the field. The wise shaman Brine lives on a ridge along its southern border. The tauren Gann Stonespire, whose tribe lost its village to the dwarves, seeks revenge here.", "Field_of_Giants" },
  ["forgotten pools"] = { "North of the western road out of the Crossroads lie the Forgotten Pools, the northernmost of the oases in the Barrens. Their shores are green, and turtles live in the water. Kolkar centaurs patrol the pools alongside the wildlife.", "Forgotten_Pools" },
  ["fray island"] = { "Warriors of every race and creed train on Fray Island, under a human named Klannoc Macleod. The island is small and arid, and lies off the eastern shore of the Barrens, south of Ratchet. Fighters come here to meet one another and fight, with crowds of onlookers gathered around them.", "Fray_Island" },
  ["gold road"] = { "The long highway of the Barrens, the Gold Road, starts at the Mor'shan Rampart in the north and runs south across the land to a bridge that joins the northern half of the Barrens to the southern. Past the bridge it is called the Southern Gold Road.", "Gold_Road" },
  ["groldom farm"] = { "Kranal Fiss and his children raise pigs on Grol'dom Farm, one of the many orc farms that have sprung up since Orgrimmar was founded. Razormane quilboar raid it constantly.", "Grol'dom_Farm" },
  ["lushwater oasis"] = { "Lushwater Oasis lies just south of the Crossroads, one of the three major oases of the Barrens. Wild beasts come to its pools to drink, and Kolkar centaurs stalk between the trees, hunting them.\n\nA cave mouth here opens into the Wailing Caverns, which are called accursed. The life energy in the oasis water is said to flow from them.", "Lushwater_Oasis" },
  ["merchant coast"] = { "The Merchant Coast takes its name from the trade that passes along it, all of it through the thrifty hands of the goblins of Ratchet. The goblin port stands on this shore, and Fray Island lies off it. The Southsea Freebooters hold the southern stretch of shore. Gazlowe, who leads Ratchet, has put a price on the head of Baron Longshore for piracy, and for leading ships of the Freebooters.", "Merchant_Coast" },
  ["morshan base camp"] = { "Mor'shan Base Camp stands just south of the border with Ashenvale, near the Mor'shan Rampart. It is one of the larger Horde settlements in the Barrens, and the base of the Warsong Outriders.", "Mor'shan_Base_Camp" },
  ["morshan rampart"] = { "Where the Gold Road ends in the north, the Horde raised the Mor'shan Rampart to hold back the Alliance push out of Ashenvale. It is a makeshift defensive post, built around watch towers. The orcs also use it as a launching point for raids on Astranaar, the night elf town to the north.", "Mor'shan_Rampart" },
  ["raptor grounds"] = { "Sunscale raptors crowd a tiny valley in the southern Barrens, and they are odd among their kind. They show an unnatural intelligence. They build crude huts and make their own tools. Anything shiny, or merely out of the ordinary, draws them, and they have stolen many things from the Horde settlements nearby.", "Raptor_Grounds" },
  ["ratchet"] = { "Gazlowe was paid well by Warchief Thrall for the help he and his workers gave in raising Orgrimmar after the Third War. Some time after the city was finished, he founded Ratchet on the eastern shore of the Barrens. Smugglers began it, and it filled with the rest of those whom other societies had cast out.\n\nIt is a goblin harbor now, and most of its trade runs through Booty Bay and Baron Revilgaz across the sea. A coven of warlocks lives in a tower above the town.", "Ratchet" },
  ["shrine of the fallen warrior"] = { "A short trail leads up a small hill west of the Crossroads to a shrine. It is a stone obelisk with the initials M K cut into its base. A spirit healer named Koiter stands in front.", "Shrine_of_the_Fallen_Warrior" },
  ["sludge fen"] = { "A pool of crystal-blue water once lay here. Then the Venture Company built an oil rig on top of it and turned it into a mire of industrial waste. Supervisor Lugwizzle, a goblin, runs the operation from the control room at the top of the rig.", "Sludge_Fen" },
  ["southern gold road"] = { "Trade caravans give the Southern Gold Road its name. It runs from the Great Lift at the southern end of the Barrens to the bridge joining the northern and southern halves of the land. North of the bridge it is simply the Gold Road.\n\nTraders come along it from as far off as Gadgetzan in Tanaris, Auberdine in Darkshore and Nighthaven in the north.", "Southern_Gold_Road" },
  ["southfury river"] = { "On the Barrens side the Southfury is the border with Durotar, and one of its three bridges carries the road across it from Durotar into the Barrens. At its mouth the river spills over a cascading waterfall into the Great Sea, northeast of Ratchet.\n\nJust north of the western bridge to Orgrimmar there is one stretch of water where Ashenvale, Azshara, Durotar and the Barrens all meet.", "Southfury_River" },
  ["stagnant oasis"] = { "Kolkar centaurs camp on the fringes of the Stagnant Oasis, which lies between the Merchant Coast and the Gold Road. Lost Barrens kodo wander here, weakened and solitary, and far easier to hunt than the kodo of the south. Deviate fish swim in the water, and oasis snapjaws live there too.", "Stagnant_Oasis" },
  ["thorn hill"] = { "Razormane quilboar hold Thorn Hill, a sizable settlement on the north face of a mountaintop between the Crossroads and Durotar. Lately they have raided Horde caravans in the region, and the local guards are angry about it.", "Thorn_Hill" },
  ["tidus stair"] = { "Broad terraces of stone rise above the coast southeast of Ratchet, stacked like a giant set of stairs that looks out over part of the Merchant Coast. Two tauren, Islen Waterseer and Mahren Skyseer, sit on them to fish and to speak with the water spirits.", "Tidus_Stair" },
  ["wailing caverns"] = { "The night elf druid Naralex found these caverns and named them for the mournful howls of wind and steam in the rock. Underground rivers run for miles beneath the savannah and bubble up as small oases across the Barrens. From the front, the entrance looks vaguely like a snake skull.\n\nNaralex believed that joining those waters to the Emerald Dream would make the land green again. He went down with his loyal disciples and settled into meditation, letting the Dream reach Azeroth through his body. The Emerald Nightmare overwhelmed him instead. The waters were corrupted, the wildlife turned into deviate forms, and most of his disciples went insane and became the Druids of the Fang.", "Wailing_Caverns" },
}
areas[1416] = {
  ["dalaran"] = { "Magi of the Arathorian Empire founded Dalaran after the Troll Wars, and it grew into a nation ruled by the Kirin Tor under the Council of Six. Its Violet Citadel was the sanctum of those who studied the arcane, and no city outside Quel'Thalas held so great a concentration of magical power. Most human and high elven mages bowed to the sovereignty of the Kirin Tor.\n\nThe Scourge besieged the city, and the demon lord Archimonde destroyed it. The surviving wizards returned to rebuild the ruins, raised a vast dome of magic around the heart of their city, and fought the Forsaken while they worked.\n\nNow the dome has opened on a city under repair, with workers at their labor and Kirin Tor guards in the streets. Dalaran is wary of Horde and Alliance alike, but it has welcomed the Skyborne, elves from Zephras Isle above the clouds. Their High Order seeks to save the isle through the arcane with the Kirin Tor's help, and a skycutter ship sails between the isle and the city.", "Dalaran" },
  ["dandreds fold"] = { "A small farm and manor stands at the western edge of the Uplands, where a harbor opens onto Lordamere Lake. Lord Aliden Perenolde lives here. He is the eldest son of King Aiden Perenolde of Alterac and the last known leader of the Syndicate. He styles himself lord of the mountains and means to put the orcs back in chains.\n\nHis mistress Elysa lives with him, and Syndicate assassins and enforcers are quartered nearby.", "Dandred's_Fold" },
  ["lordamere internment camp"] = { "The Lordamere Internment Camp stands on a hill overlooking Dalaran, the only internment camp in the Alterac Mountains. Wizards of Dalaran hold it, and Warden Belamoore commands the garrison, though she gives more of her time to her studies than to her post.", "Lordamere_Internment_Camp" },
  ["ruins of alterac"] = { "King Aiden Perenolde of Alterac lost hope of a human victory in the Second War and made a pact with the Horde. The orcs could cross his lands unopposed, and he would be protected in return. Alteraci soldiers were found fighting beside the orcs, and a prison camp near the city held captive citizens of Dalaran. When the treason came out, the other human kingdoms marched on his city. King Terenas Menethil II of Lordaeron put it under martial law and imprisoned him.\n\nCrushridge ogres under the warlord Mug'thol live in the ruins now. The palace stands half whole, though the church and town hall are in ruins, and the gate is built much like the one at Stormwind. Wrecked meat wagons lie by the gate, and shattered ballistae litter the rubble.", "Ruins_of_Alterac" },
  ["uplands"] = { "These hills were the bread basket of the kingdom of Alterac. When the kingdom fell, the Syndicate took them over and pitched several camps here. Nowhere in the mountains are they stronger.\n\nBaron Vardus, once a noble of Alterac and now the Syndicate's quartermaster, is found in these camps.", "Uplands" },
}
areas[1417] = {
  ["boulderfist hall"] = { "Kor'gresh Coldrage, an ogre mage and chief of the Boulderfist clan, holds the larger cave in this strongly fortified mound of the ogres. It stands in the south, near Thandol Span and Witherbark Village.", "Boulderfist_Hall" },
  ["bouldergor"] = { "A rough, broken hill rises north of Stromgarde Keep. Boulderfist ogres hold a mound on it, and Witherbark forest trolls loiter on its slopes beside them.", "Boulder'gor" },
  ["circle of east binding"] = { "The Stone of East Binding stands in the east of Arathi Highlands, near Hammerfall, watched over by water elementals. It is one of four binding stones set across the highlands.", "Circle_of_East_Binding" },
  ["circle of inner binding"] = { "The strongest of the four binding stones lies here, in the south near Stromgarde Keep. Earth elementals guard the Stone of Inner Binding.", "Circle_of_Inner_Binding" },
  ["circle of outer binding"] = { "Air elementals circle the Stone of Outer Binding at the middle of the highlands, near Refuge Pointe. It is one of four binding stones set across the highlands.", "Circle_of_Outer_Binding" },
  ["circle of west binding"] = { "Fire elementals guard the Stone of West Binding in the west of the highlands, near Thoradin's Wall. It is one of four binding stones set across the highlands.", "Circle_of_West_Binding" },
  ["dabyries farmstead"] = { "Kenata Dabyrie farms the north of Arathi Highlands with her children Fardel and Marcel. They are citizens of Stromgarde and loyal to the Alliance, and a militia guards their fields. They came through the Third War and prospered. Stromgarde's cavalry, led by Lieutenant Valorcall, rides the road between the farm and Stromgarde Keep.", "Dabyrie's_Farmstead" },
  ["drowned reef"] = { "A ruined elven city lies sunken beneath the bay on the southern shore, and the Daggerspine naga under Prince Nazjak have made it theirs. The reef above has wrecked many ships, among them the Maiden's Folly and the Spirit of Silverpine.\n\nThe naga seem able to turn the tides of the bay. Crews who sail into Faldir's Cove thinking they have found a safe harbor are thrown onto the stones.", "Drowned_Reef" },
  ["drywhisker gorge"] = { "A gorge cuts through the mountains of eastern Arathi Highlands, northeast of Hammerfall, and ends in a cave. Drywhisker kobolds live there, allies of the giants. Their diggers and surveyors work the gorge, and a geomancer named Flintdagger lives among them.", "Drywhisker_Gorge" },
  ["faldirs cove"] = { "A strange tide met the Blackwater Raiders when they sailed three ships into this cove for an elven treasure, led by Captain Steelgut and Captain O'Breen. The naga guarding the sunken ruins offshore raised it, and two of the ships went down. Only the Harbinger reached the shore, and her crew is trapped in the bay.\n\nThe cove is named for Faldir, who is said to have marked this spot when he and his followers set out across the sea.", "Faldir's_Cove" },
  ["goshek farm"] = { "Primwell Skye, a veteran of the Second War, once held this farmstead. After the Third War, Forsaken and orcs from Hammerfall took it. The orcs draw supplies from it for Hammerfall, and the Forsaken use it as a testing ground for alchemical experiments. Hammerfall Grunts guard the place.\n\nTwo Alliance agents, Quae and Kinelory, have come to find out what the Forsaken are brewing here.", "Go'Shek_Farm" },
  ["hammerfall"] = { "Orgrim Doomhammer fell here. After the Second War the Alliance of Lordaeron kept captive orcs in one of its largest internment camps on this ground. Thrall and Doomhammer led the Horde to free them, and in the chaos a charging knight drove a lance through the Warchief from behind.\n\nAfter the Third War the Horde took the ruins as a forward base and named it Hammerfall in his honor. It became a refuge for those who felt they had no place in the world. Boulderfist ogres and Witherbark trolls have allied to wipe it out.", "Hammerfall" },
  ["northfold manor"] = { "A farm on the northwestern edge of the Arathi Highlands became a Syndicate outpost after the fall of Stromgarde. Syndicate highwaymen, mercenaries and pathstalkers hold the manor and its fields now.", "Northfold_Manor" },
  ["refuge pointe"] = { "When Stromgarde's capital fell in ruins after the Third War, most of its people fled south. The remnants of the army stayed behind, determined to win their land back, and they hold this outpost. From here the Alliance means to retake Stromgarde Keep and to secure the resources of the Highlands.\n\nStromgarde cavalrymen under Lieutenant Valorcall, the cavalry leader of the Stromic army, patrol the road to Stromgarde Keep.", "Refuge_Pointe" },
  ["stromgarde keep"] = { "Legend says King Thoradin saw a black wolf roaming this dry land and chose the spot for his capital. With his sword, Strom'kar, he cut the boundaries of a city into the dirt, and his masons began to build Strom, seat of the first human kingdom.\n\nIn the Second War and after it, the city stood as a power among the Alliance of Lordaeron. Now it is sacked and divided three ways. The Syndicate holds the inner keep. Boulderfist ogres, helped by Witherbark trolls, hold the old mage district. Prince Galen Trollbane commands the last quarter from what remains of his army.", "Stromgarde_Keep" },
  ["thandol span"] = { "Two great stone arches carry the road between the Wetlands of Khaz Modan and the Arathi Highlands. The dwarves of Ironforge raised them after the War of the Three Hammers, to keep faith and trade with their Wildhammer cousins, and the Anvilmar family has guarded them for three generations. The bridges could bear the weight of whole armies and were the main road of trade between dwarves and humans. When the orcs lost the battle for Lordaeron, Anduin Lothar drove the Old Horde back into Khaz Modan across the Span.\n\nNow a Dark Iron attack has all but destroyed one bridge. The dwarves fight to hold the other and to retake the town of Dun Modr, and many have died.", "Thandol_Span" },
  ["thoradins wall"] = { "Against the Amani trolls, King Thoradin ordered a great wall raised soon after he founded Strom, the capital of Arathor. For generations it stood watch over the border between the Arathi Highlands and Hillsbrad Foothills.\n\nMany centuries later it still stands, but heavily damaged, its stones broken and gaping wide.", "Thoradin's_Wall" },
  ["tower of arathor"] = { "The Tower of Arathor stands in the former mage district of Stromgarde Keep and is named after the Empire of Arathor.\n\nArchmage Trelane once studied in this tower. When the Syndicate and the Boulderfist ogres besieged Stromgarde, he tried to hold the tower and failed, and he fled the city. The ogres took the tower and the district around it, and the ogre mage Ruul Onestone now lives inside.", "Tower_of_Arathor" },
  ["witherbark village"] = { "Around a small lake in the southeast of the Arathi Highlands sits the village of the Witherbark trolls. Behind it is a large cavern where the tribe's leaders are often found.\n\nThe Witherbark have made a loose alliance with the nearby Boulderfist ogres against Stromgarde and Hammerfall. Together they hold a section of Stromgarde itself.", "Witherbark_Village" },
}
areas[1418] = {
  ["agmonds end"] = { "Prospector Agmond was in charge of the dwarf excavations in the Badlands, and this small dig in the south central part of the zone was the southernmost of them. Troggs had infested the site, and Agmond met his end here. The dig stands abandoned, and it bears his name.", "Agmond's_End" },
  ["angor fortress"] = { "Angor Fortress is where the Dark Iron dwarves direct their work in the Badlands. From here they organize their exploration of Uldaman, and they mean to exploit it. Ambassador Infernus, a fire elemental who serves Ragnaros, also lives in the keep and oversees the Shadowforge clan's digging.", "Angor_Fortress" },
  ["apocryphans rest"] = { "The bones of Apocryphan's Rest sprawl across the Badlands south of Kargath, a vast draconic skeleton fallen and left to bleach in the dry heat. A flock of hungry buzzards has taken over the bones and nests among the ribs.", "Apocryphan's_Rest" },
  ["camp boff"] = { "Only three ogres guard Camp Boff, the southeastern of the four camps the Dustbelcher ogres keep in the Badlands.", "Camp_Boff" },
  ["camp cagg"] = { "Boss Tho'grun, a mercenary leader of the Dustbelcher ogres, walks a circuit between the tribe's camps with his bodyguards, and Camp Cagg is one of his stops. Some of the Dustbelcher make their home here.", "Camp_Cagg" },
  ["camp kosh"] = { "The ogres of Camp Kosh prey on the supply caravans that must cross the barren Badlands. It is the northernmost of the Dustbelcher camps.\n\nSigrun Ironhew and his dwarves were forced to leave the dig at Uldaman and had to abandon wagons and crates along the road. The ogres of Kosh plundered them, and took every weapon and all the food.", "Camp_Kosh" },
  ["camp wurg"] = { "Some of the Dustbelcher ogres live at Camp Wurg in the northwest of the Badlands, and Boss Tho'grun's mercenary band stops here on its rounds.", "Camp_Wurg" },
  ["dustbelch grotto"] = { "In the southwestern Badlands rises a great mound of earth and stone, home of the Dustbelcher ogres. More of the tribe live in camps scattered across the cracked, sun-scorched land around it.", "Dustbelch_Grotto" },
  ["dustbowl"] = { "Cracked, dry ground spreads across the Dustbowl, a wide flat in the northwest of the Badlands. Coyotes range over it, buzzards circle above, and rock elementals stir among the stones. Only cacti and a few trees have found a way to grow here.\n\nCamp Wurg stands in the waste, one of the homes of the Dustbelcher ogres.", "Dustbowl" },
  ["dustwind gulch"] = { "Dustwind Gulch cuts through the dry, cracked ground of the Badlands, west of Lethlor Ravine. Stonevault troggs roam it, and rabid crag coyotes, ridge stalkers and starving buzzards hunt there.", "Dustwind_Gulch" },
  ["hammertoes digsite"] = { "Hammertoe Grez, a dwarf, made this dig in the northern Badlands while he searched for lost titan artifacts with the historian Karnik. He has abandoned it. A gang of Dark Iron dwarves has taken it over, most likely part of the same force that has moved into Uldaman.", "Hammertoe's_Digsite" },
  ["kargath"] = { "Kargath is the only outpost the Horde holds in Khaz Modan. It is named for Kargath Bladefist, chieftain of the Shattered Hand clan.\n\nWarchief Thrall sent the Kargath Expeditionary Force here to explore the Badlands. Its scouts found Uldaman, a dig the dwarves value highly. The force's main aim is a road through the Searing Gorge to Blackrock Mountain, so the Horde can strike at Rend Blackhand, Warchief of the Dark Horde. On the way it ran into the Dark Iron dwarves, and the two sides are at war again.", "Kargath" },
  ["lethlor ravine"] = { "A wide, craggy valley runs through the eastern Badlands, and whelps and drakes of the black dragonflight roam it. Crystalline obelisks stand among the rocks.", "Lethlor_Ravine" },
  ["makers terrace"] = { "Dark Iron dwarves patrol the wide excavation of the Maker's Terrace, just outside the entrance to Uldaman. The earth has been cut back in wide swathes and left open to the sky.", "Maker's_Terrace" },
  ["mirage flats"] = { "Giant buzzards, ridge stalker patriarchs and rabid crag coyotes live on the Mirage Flats, east of Dustbelch Grotto.", "Mirage_Flats" },
  ["uldaman"] = { "Legends say the titans made the troggs from stone, judged them a failure and locked them away in a vault under the earth. Then they tried again, and the dwarves were the result. That vault is Uldaman.\n\nDwarves from Ironforge dug into it before the Third War and woke the troggs. Now the Dark Iron dwarves push inside, hoping to take the Discs of Norgannon, which record how the dwarves were made, for their master Ragnaros.", "Uldaman" },
  ["valley of fangs"] = { "A small valley runs between two massive stone formations in the central Badlands. Wolves and cougars hunt there, and the way through is dangerous. Martek the Exiled, Rigglefuzz and Jazzrik keep a camp in the valley.", "Valley_of_Fangs" },
}
areas[1419] = {
  ["dark portal"] = { "Medivh, a human mage in the grip of Sargeras, and the orc warlock Gul'dan tore a rift between Draenor and Azeroth in the Black Morass. Through it came the Horde. At the end of the Second War, Archmage Khadgar and his magi destroyed the gateway to stop the orcs returning.\n\nThe stone arch that stands here now was raised when Ner'zhul reopened the rift. Hooded guardians hold swords on either side, and a stone serpent bares its fangs above. Lightning strikes the crater. The gate itself is still.", "Dark_Portal" },
  ["dreadmaul post"] = { "The Dreadmaul ogres came to Azeroth from Draenor and never wandered far from the Dark Portal. This mound is theirs, and they keep a strong presence around it. The mages of Nethergarde Keep say they serve Lord Kazzak.", "Dreadmaul_Post" },
  ["garrison armory"] = { "In the scarred ground of the Blasted Lands, Alliance miners work the Garrison Armory, wresting minerals from the earth for Nethergarde Keep. What they gather is shipped on to Stormwind, and in return the keep receives the other supplies it needs to endure in that blighted place.", "Garrison_Armory" },
  ["nethergarde keep"] = { "Nethergarde Keep stands on a high bluff above the only road into the Blasted Lands, its walls raised to watch the ruin of a gateway that no one wanted opened again. Archmage Khadgar called for it after the Second War, to make sure no second invasion came through the Dark Portal. Mages of Dalaran manned it and studied what the portal's energy had done to the soil and the beasts.\n\nDalaran was destroyed in the Third War, and the keep lives on caravans from Stormwind. Its mages and warriors fight ogres and demons every day, and they have little cheer left.", "Nethergarde_Keep" },
  ["rise of the defiler"] = { "Craggy mountains rise near the middle of the Blasted Lands, and a great demon lives among them. Razelikh the Defiler is a dreadlord, the most fearsome lieutenant of Lord Kazzak, who set him to secure the land.", "Rise_of_the_Defiler" },
  ["serpents coil"] = { "Burnt-out orcish buildings left from the Second War stand at the mouth of this winding cave system in the east. Cultists of the Shadowsworn worship a demon in the caves behind them, and Archmage Allistarj leads the cult from within.", "Serpent's_Coil" },
  ["tainted scar"] = { "This stretch of the western Blasted Lands was once part of the Black Morass. The corruption of the Dark Portal left it waterless and treeless, the soil the color of blood, and demonic beasts prowl over it.\n\nAfter the Burning Legion's defeat at Mount Hyjal, Lord Kazzak led much of its remaining force on Azeroth into the Scar and made it his own. He is there still, and the dreadlord Razelikh the Defiler serves him. An elderly human warlock, Daio the Decrepit, has settled at its altar. He performs rituals with the demons and trains recruits in the infernal arts.", "Tainted_Scar" },
}
areas[1420] = {
  ["agamand family crypt"] = { "Just north of Agamand Mills lies the crypt where the Agamand family buried its dead. Captain Dargol, a Scourge commander, has made it his headquarters, the strongest point in the mills. With necromancy he has raised the family's ancestors, every corpse and spirit within, to use against the Forsaken. The crypt is dark and dusty, and the vengeful dead attack anyone who enters unbidden.", "Agamand_Family_Crypt" },
  ["agamand mills"] = { "The windmills of Agamand stand in the highlands of northern Tirisfal Glades, and they once turned for a wealthy family. When the Scourge first entered the glades, Gregor Agamand would not leave. His family fortified the mills, stocked them well and persuaded their workers to stay and fight.\n\nThe defense failed from within. Devlin, the youngest son, sided with the Scourge, killed two watchmen and led undead through the breach to where the men slept. A third of the defenders died, and too few remained to hold. Then the plague took the whole family, and the Agamands walk their own mills as mindless dead. The Scourge now hold the place as an outpost.", "Agamand_Mills" },
  ["balnir farmstead"] = { "Northeast of the fallen capital of Lordaeron stands the ruined farm of the Balnir family, who bred the great white horses the royal house of Lordaeron rode. Scourge from the Plaguelands that slip across the border now shelter among the ruins. Snapdragons once tended in the garden still grow here.", "Balnir_Farmstead" },
  ["brightwater lake"] = { "The largest lake in Tirisfal Glades lies northeast of Brill. A free-willed lich named Gunther Arcanus lives on the big island at its center.", "Brightwater_Lake" },
  ["brill"] = { "Brill was an ordinary town of Lordaeron, held by the Barov family, with the Agamands keeping a manor of their own within its bounds. Then Kel'Thuzad, still a living man, chose it to begin his experiments with the plague of undeath. They worked so well that he convinced the Lich King to launch the invasion before it was planned. The graveyard just outside the town was made to hold the many dead. Brill's defenders could not save the villagers and were driven out of the town. They joined Prince Arthas Menethil in his hunt for the source of the plague. The Forsaken have since rebuilt the town, and Magistrate Sevren governs it.", "Brill" },
  ["brill town hall"] = { "The town hall is the most prominent building in Brill. From his podium, Magistrate Sevren leads the town and gives orders and tasks to the Forsaken who serve him. Few town halls anywhere are run by the Forsaken, and this is one of them.", "Brill_Town_Hall" },
  ["bulwark"] = { "High Executor Derrington commands the Bulwark, the last bastion the Forsaken hold against the Scourge to the east, where Tirisfal Glades meets the Western Plaguelands. Forsaken defenders and the Argent Dawn have teamed up to hold the border, and Derrington means to uproot the foothold the Scourge has made beyond it. A plaguemist hangs over the camp and tints the sky yellow-gray, as it does in the Western Plaguelands and nowhere else in Tirisfal Glades.", "Bulwark" },
  ["cold hearth manor"] = { "Cold Hearth Manor was known for its warm atmosphere and bountiful crops. The Plague of Undeath killed everyone who lived or worked here, and the fields turned barren. Mindless Scourge zombies now infest them. The Forsaken hold the farmhouse beside the main road.", "Cold_Hearth_Manor" },
  ["crusader outpost"] = { "A scout tower stands in the eastern hills of Tirisfal Glades, held by the Scarlet Crusade. From it the Crusaders watch the main road through the glades to the south. They also use it to flank any force that tries to assault the Scarlet Monastery. Captain Vachon commands the tower.", "Crusader_Outpost" },
  ["deathknell"] = { "Every other Forsaken settlement kept the name it had as a human town. Deathknell was renamed, and no one knows why. It lies in a well-guarded valley, and the newly risen Forsaken come down to it from the crypt where they first open their eyes.\n\nThe Scourge, whom the villagers call the Mindless Ones, hold the northern end of the valley and its forge, so the Forsaken have dug in around the church. Survival is a struggle, and gold is the scarcest thing of all. A gold mine to the northwest is overrun with spiders, and the villagers keep trying to clear it. Deathguard Elites watch the valley.\n\nNot every risen soul has lost the Light. In the church the paladin Aramis Hammerhand welcomes back those who still feel it and trains them.", "Deathknell" },
  ["faols rest"] = { "Archbishop Alonsus Faol, the much revered head of the Church of the Holy Light, was buried here in eastern Tirisfal Glades. He died some time before the Scourge came.\n\nMourners visited the grave often in the days before the Plague of Undeath. Now it lies untended and mostly forgotten.", "Faol's_Rest" },
  ["gallows end tavern"] = { "In Brill, the Forsaken run the only fully working tavern their people are known to keep. In its cellar a captured Scarlet Zealot and a dwarven mountaineer are held prisoner.", "Gallows'_End_Tavern" },
  ["garrens haunt"] = { "Garren's Haunt was once a bountiful farmstead in the north of Tirisfal Glades, with a farmhouse, a barn, a couple of silos and a wide field. When the Scourge swept through Lordaeron, they murdered Garren and his family. The plague turned the land gray and lifeless.\n\nRot Hide gnolls now hold the farmhouse and barn under their leader, Maggot Eye. They attack anyone who comes close.", "Garren's_Haunt" },
  ["gunthers retreat"] = { "On an island in Brightwater Lake lives Gunther Arcanus, a free-willed lich. He keeps to the island, and his zombies attack anyone who comes near.", "Gunther's_Retreat" },
  ["night webs hollow"] = { "Spiders hold this small valley and the gold mine cut into it. Young Night Web Spiders wander the open hollow, and grown ones skitter through the mine and lurk in its webbed corners.\n\nYoung Forsaken come to test themselves on the spiders outside before they venture deeper. In the deepest recess of the mine sits the Night Web Matriarch, the smallest of the spiders and the hardest to reach.", "Night_Web's_Hollow" },
  ["nightmare vale"] = { "This wide valley was once green and full of forest animals. Now the trees have withered, and hungry darkhounds and huge vampire bats have eaten the wildlife. A rare white bat called Ressan the Needler hunts among them.", "Nightmare_Vale" },
  ["north coast"] = { "The beach along the northern edge of Tirisfal Glades looks out on the Great Sea. Ravenous murlocs and other sea creatures hold it.\n\nSince the Plague of Undeath, the coast has turned dark and cold. Dead sea creatures rot at the edge of the water.", "North_Coast" },
  ["ruins of lordaeron"] = { "Prince Arthas Menethil turned on his own people. He came home to Lordaeron at the head of an army and found his father, King Terenas, waiting in the throne room, and he killed him there. Standing over the body, he proclaimed that the kingdom would fall and that from its ashes a new order would rise to shake the world. At his command the Scourge then marched in and slaughtered the city's people.\n\nThe palace of Lordaeron still stands above the Undercity, and the hour of the king's death is still heard there. Voices whisper in the courtyard, bells clang in the entryway, a cheer welcomes the prince, and a murmur rises from the throne room. The citizens of Lordaeron still haunt the courtyard, and those who can see the dead find them there.", "Ruins_of_Lordaeron" },
  ["scarlet monastery"] = { "The monastery in the northeast corner of Tirisfal Glades was once a cathedral of the Church of the Holy Light. It held great libraries and served as a seminary where paladins trained. Alexandros Mograine spent months here in his youth, praying and studying.\n\nAfter the Third War the Scarlet Crusade took it as their stronghold in the war on the Scourge and the Forsaken. They made it a military camp and a place to hold their prisoners. They are fanatics who hate every race but human and hold that any outsider may carry the plague. High Inquisitor Whitemane is the monastery's true master.", "Scarlet_Monastery" },
  ["scarlet watch post"] = { "Captain Melrache commands the scout tower north of the Scarlet Monastery. It stands in a remote place, and the Scarlet Crusade keeps it to stop its enemies from striking the monastery on its less defensible north side.\n\nIt is one of four Crusader outposts in Tirisfal Glades.", "Scarlet_Watch_Post" },
  ["shadow grave"] = { "Newly risen Forsaken wake in this crypt, which stands at the top of a slope in the mountains of Tirisfal Glades. Deathguard Balteus and Deathguard Hansel stand guard here. A worn path runs down from the entrance to Deathknell.", "Shadow_Grave" },
  ["solliden farmstead"] = { "Farmer Solliden owns this pumpkin farm, and he is hostile to both the Horde and the Alliance. His people are among the few in northern Lordaeron the Plague of Undeath did not touch, and they still work their rows while the country around them has fallen.\n\nSettlers from Arathor began farming Tirisfal as their kingdom grew. Later, noble families from Strom bought up large plots the first settlers had cleared, Solliden among them.", "Solliden_Farmstead" },
  ["stillwater pond"] = { "Before the plague, Stillwater Pond was a place for the living to be young. Families from the glades came to picnic on its banks, and children too small to brave the deeper waters of Brightwater Lake learned to swim here instead.\n\nThat water is gray now. The fish beneath the surface are only bones still swimming, as if no one told them to stop. Wandering undead and wild beasts pass along the shore, and the darkhound Bayne prowls the water's edge, feeding on whatever is unwary enough to linger.", "Stillwater_Pond" },
  ["terrace of repose"] = { "North of the Scarlet Monastery entrance lies a small stretch of ground the Plague of Undeath has never reached. The Scarlet Crusade grows green hedges here and keeps a fountain running with fresh water, while the water elsewhere in Tirisfal Glades has gone stagnant.", "Terrace_of_Repose" },
  ["venomweb vale"] = { "A brood of poisonous spiders under the deadly Sri'skulk holds Venomweb Vale now, and they have given the valley its name. Before they came the grass was green and the trees were healthy, and hunters found deer and rabbits here. Darkhounds roam the northern end.\n\nA rock of fool's gold in the vale marks the spot where Aegwynn once met with members of the Council of Tirisfal.", "Venomweb_Vale" },
  ["whispering gardens"] = { "A great battle was fought on this hill south of the Scarlet Monastery entrance. On one side stood the men who became the Scarlet Crusade, on the other a host of the Scourge. At twilight the spirits of the fallen can still be heard here as faint whispers. Scarlet magicians, preservers and scouts hold the ground now.", "Whispering_Gardens" },
  ["whispering shore"] = { "The water off the Whispering Shore is cold and dark, and the bounty that once drew fishermen to this stretch of coast is gone. A band of murlocs led by the fierce Muad has overrun it and falls on anyone who wanders near.", "Whispering_Shore" },
}
areas[1421] = {
  ["ambermill"] = { "Ambermill was a lumber town of little account until the Third War. Then the Scourge and the Burning Legion tore through the north, and the Kirin Tor of Dalaran, rebuilding their own city, found a use for it. The town sits on a dormant ley line that the wizards mean to wake. Their support makes Ambermill the one real source of human resistance in the forest, and the mills, the stables and the town hall are in Kirin Tor hands. The Forsaken fear losing their hold on Silverpine Forest.", "Ambermill" },
  ["berens peril"] = { "In the hills southwest of Ambermill, between the main road and the side road from the town, a band of undead hides at Beren's Peril, preparing an attack on the Sepulcher. They are servants of the necromancer Thule Ravenclaw.", "Beren's_Peril" },
  ["dawning isles"] = { "The Dawning Isles were once an idyllic set of low islets rising from the clear water of Lordamere Lake. The plague of undeath reached them, and their trees grew sickly. Murlocs roamed the shores, and now the Vile Fin murlocs, grown very numerous, hold the isles entirely.", "Dawning_Isles" },
  ["dead field"] = { "Nobody knows who farmed the Dead Field. The farm in northern Silverpine Forest is abandoned and rotting, and the Rot Hide gnolls, servants of the Scourge, have made it a base. Their numbers keep growing, which points to an attack in the making. The Forsaken have struck at the field again and again and failed each time. When the gnolls come under attack, the banshee Nightlash, who commands them, may rise among them.", "Dead_Field" },
  ["decrepit ferry"] = { "A ferry once ran from this shore to Fenris Isle, carrying goods and passengers for a small fee. The nearby farms sold part of their crops to the rich lord on the island and his family. Then the Scourge invaded Lordaeron. The ferryman was murdered and the ferry destroyed. Ghouls, skeletons and Rot Hide gnolls now crowd the docks, and the ground is dangerous.", "Decrepit_Ferry" },
  ["deep elem mine"] = { "Arugal's magic has reached Deep Elem Mine, in the hills of central Silverpine Forest, and worgen of the Moonrage pack have made it their home. Gluttons and darksouls fill its shafts. They follow Grimson the Pale, the cunning foreman who once ran the mine.", "Deep_Elem_Mine" },
  ["fenris isle"] = { "Fenris Isle forms, with the Dawning Isles, a broken ring of dry land at the center of Lordamere Lake. It is a large island, and before the Third War it held Fenris Keep, a thriving military outpost of Lordaeron. When the Scourge took the country, everyone on the isle either fled or was murdered. The Rot Hide gnolls then took the keep as their base, and their treacherous master, Thule Ravenclaw, holds the whole island.", "Fenris_Isle" },
  ["fenris keep"] = { "Fenris Keep once housed a sizable part of Lordaeron's army. When the Scourge washed over the land, the keep did not escape. Thule Ravenclaw, an ambitious young mage once of the Kirin Tor, sided with the Scourge and bent the local gnolls to his will, making them the Rot Hide. He is master of the keep, and his gnolls fall on anyone who comes near.", "Fenris_Keep" },
  ["greymane wall"] = { "King Genn Greymane built this wall to cut Gilneas off from the world. He was tired of paying Gilnean taxes toward the orc internment camps, and tired of the quarrels among the kings of the Alliance of Lordaeron. The wall is huge, with one great gate, and it kept the Scourge out of his kingdom. Refugees fleeing south from Lordaeron camp in its shadow. They are haggard and sick, and the gate stays shut to them.", "Greymane_Wall" },
  ["ivar patch"] = { "Ivar's farm was a pleasant, prosperous place, and he employed many farmhands and servants. Then the plague of undeath came. Ivar, his family and every farmhand turned into rotting ghouls, and they fall on anyone who comes near. Ivar himself is known now as Ivar the Foul. A Forsaken scouting party was ambushed here, and Ivar wounded the deathstalker Quinn Yorick. Quinn and Rane Yorick hold out in Ivar's old house.", "Ivar_Patch" },
  ["lordamere lake"] = { "Many human kingdoms prospered around Lordamere Lake in the days of the Alliance of Lordaeron. Lordaeron built its capital on the northern shore, and the lake very likely took its name from it. Dalaran and Alterac stood nearby. That age is over, and the lake is now a border. It lies between the Forsaken lands of Tirisfal Glades and Silverpine Forest and the contested Alterac Mountains.\n\nAt its heart sits Fenris Isle, where Rot Hide gnolls hold an old keep of Lordaeron's army. East of it, the Vile Fin murlocs have overrun the small Dawning Isles.", "Lordamere_Lake" },
  ["maldens orchard"] = { "The trees of Malden's Orchard have gone dark and evergreen, and their fruit has not been picked in years. The orchard takes its name from its owner, who fled his home when the Scourge invaded Lordaeron.\n\nBlack worgs now den among the trees and around the orchard's single small cottage.", "Malden's_Orchard" },
  ["north tides hollow"] = { "North Tide's Hollow lies just north of the Sepulcher in Silverpine Forest. It is one of the many haunts of the Moonrage worgen, servants of Archmage Arugal. Here they raise crude encampments with only the barest necessities for survival, shelter enough for creatures who live by tooth and strength.", "North_Tide's_Hollow" },
  ["north tides run"] = { "The northwestern shoreline of Silverpine Forest, where the beach meets the Great Sea, bears the name North Tide's Run. A derelict boat rests on the shore, the most prominent mark on this stretch of coast. Corrupted water spirits haunt these waters. A fisherman, Killian Sanatha, keeps a campfire on the beach and sells fishing recipes, and a troll, Tiev Mordune, keeps to the shore.", "North_Tide's_Run" },
  ["olsens farthing"] = { "Olsen's Farthing was once a busy farmstead on the main trade road through Silverpine Forest. Now it lies in ruins. Undead under the command of Thule Ravenclaw hold the farm, skeletal raiders and ghouls he keeps as slaves, and they attack anyone who comes near.", "Olsen's_Farthing" },
  ["pyrewood village"] = { "By day Pyrewood Village is a human settlement with a town hall, a blacksmith and a chapel. Its people lived through the coming of the Scourge, and they still sell cloth, leather and mail to travelers.\n\nThen night falls. From Shadowfang Keep on the bluff above, Archmage Arugal has cursed the village. Every evening its people turn into Moonrage worgen and attack anyone near, whatever side they stand on. At dawn they wake human again.", "Pyrewood_Village" },
  ["sepulcher"] = { "The Sepulcher sits in the western hills above the Lordaeron coast. Once it was only a large graveyard beside the road that crosses Silverpine Forest, its crypts a resting place for the dead. Since the Third War the Forsaken have turned the old graveyard into their base in the region, a place built for the dead made to serve the undying.\n\nWorgen are to the south, the Scourge to the east, and the forces of the Kirin Tor to the southeast. Surrounded on nearly every side, the Forsaken hold one of the few places in the forest where the Horde can stand in safety.", "Sepulcher" },
  ["shadowfang keep"] = { "Baron Silverlaine once held this keep on a high bluff above Pyrewood Village. When the Scourge swept over Lordaeron, Archmage Arugal summoned the worgen against the counsel of the Kirin Tor.\n\nThe worgen cut down the Scourge, then besieged the keep and killed the Baron. Arugal, mad with guilt, took the worgen as his children and withdrew inside. He renamed the place Shadowfang Keep. He is said to be there still, guarded by his great pet Fenrus and haunted by the Baron's ghost.", "Shadowfang_Keep" },
  ["shining strand"] = { "A long shoreline of Lordamere Lake runs from the capital city of Lordaeron in the north to the rough hills that divide Silverpine Forest from the Alterac Mountains. This is the Shining Strand.\n\nThe Vile Fin murlocs have several settlements along it, and travelers who do not know the shore find it dangerous.", "Shining_Strand" },
  ["skittering dark"] = { "Spiders have taken over the Skittering Dark, an abandoned mine near the northwestern coast of Silverpine Forest. Moss stalkers crawl through the mine and the ground around its mouth, mist creepers lurk in the woods just east of it, and giant grizzled bears hunt among them. A rare spider, Krethis Shadowspinner, sometimes prowls nearby.", "Skittering_Dark" },
  ["south tides run"] = { "The southwestern coast of Silverpine Forest, where the land meets the Great Sea, is called South Tide's Run.", "South_Tide's_Run" },
  ["valgans field"] = { "Valgan's Field was a wheat farm, and it takes its name from the man who owned it. When the Scourge arrived in Silverpine Forest he was either killed or forced to flee. From the fields there was a fine view of Lordamere Lake.\n\nNow it is one of the desolate farms in the north of the forest, west of the Shining Strand, and feral Moonrage worgen roam the empty farm.", "Valgan's_Field" },
}
areas[1422] = {
  ["bulwark"] = { "After the fall of Lordaeron, Uther the Lightbringer made a camp here for the survivors, on the border where Tirisfal Glades meets the Western Plaguelands. Now the Forsaken hold the ground against the Scourge, and Argent Dawn soldiers stand with them.", "Bulwark" },
  ["caer darrow"] = { "Humans built the fortress of Caer Darrow long ago, and an ancient high elven runestone was set within it. In the Second War the Horde invaded the island, sacked the keep and hewed the runestone into the pieces from which the Altars of Storms were made. Gul'dan used them to instill magic into his ogre allies.\n\nThe keep was restored after the war and became the manor of House Barov. In the years before the Third War the Barovs grew greedy and bitter and wanted to keep their fortune and land beyond their own lifespan. Kel'Thuzad, leader of the Cult of the Damned, offered a way. Beneath the manor the catacombs became Scholomance, a school of necromancy, and the family's servants were used to test the plague of undeath. Within six months a sickness broke loose and killed everyone on the island. Their spirits remain, each bound to relive the last thing it knew. Two girls still race each other by the fountain, and a baker still sells his bread.", "Caer_Darrow" },
  ["chillwind camp"] = { "Chillwind Camp stands at the southern entrance to the Western Plaguelands, where the road comes down from the Alterac Mountains, and it is the last safe ground for Alliance travelers. A small contingent of the Argent Dawn keeps it. With only a few troops and specialists, the Dawn leans on mercenaries and fights a guerrilla war against the Scourge at Andorhal and the plague cauldrons. Commander Ashlam Valorfist is here too, sent by the Kingdom of Stormwind and its allies to counter the Scourge.", "Chillwind_Camp" },
  ["dalsons tears"] = { "Dalson's Orchard once bore fruit for the family that gave it its name. Now the Scourge hold the farm. A plague cauldron squats in the field and breathes out disease, and blighted zombies, rotting cadavers and skeletal terrors guard it.", "Dalson's_Tears" },
  ["darrowmere lake"] = { "Darrowmere is one of the largest lakes in the Eastern Kingdoms and among the most symbolic places of the fallen kingdom of Lordaeron. The Thondroril River is joined to it in the north and the Darrowmere River in the south. In the middle lies the island town of Caer Darrow, where the House of Barov once lived. It is abandoned now. Beneath it is Scholomance, where the Cult of the Damned works among its undead.", "Darrowmere_Lake" },
  ["felstone field"] = { "Felstone Field once fed the living. The Scourge overran it and set a cauldron in the middle of the field, and vapor thick with disease has poured out of it ever since. Skeletal sorcerers, skeletal flayers and slavering ghouls guard it. A party of the Scarlet Crusade is here too, and it takes vengeance on the living and the dead alike. In the house at the northeastern corner of the field, Janice Felstone is still at home.", "Felstone_Field" },
  ["gahrrons withering"] = { "The farm at the eastern edge of the Western Plaguelands once belonged to Gahrron, and in its better days it was called Gahrron's Quickening. Nothing grows there now. The Scourge took the field and raised a cauldron in the middle of it, and haunting visions, wailing deaths and hungering wraiths guard it, drifting over the ruined rows.", "Gahrron's_Withering" },
  ["hearthglen"] = { "The Horde never took Hearthglen in the Second War, and the Scourge did not take it either, though its people had just baked bread from plagued grain out of Andorhal when the dead came. Its walls enclose a town hall, a mage tower, a lumber mill, stables, a mine and Mardenholde Keep, from which the region was governed. Now the Scarlet Crusade holds the town and the keep, and its soldiers strike out from behind the walls against the Scourge.", "Hearthglen" },
  ["mardenholde keep"] = { "Tirion Fordring was lord of Mardenholde Keep until he was excommunicated, and his son Taelan took the keep after him. Taelan is Highlord of the Scarlet Crusade now, and the keep is the Crusade's headquarters in the Western Plaguelands.\n\nIn Tirion's day Mardenholde was the center of commerce and trade for the principality of Hearthglen. A few years before the Third War it filled with dignitaries from the nations of the Alliance, passing through Hearthglen on secret diplomatic errands.", "Mardenholde_Keep" },
  ["northridge lumber camp"] = { "The Scarlet Crusade runs the lumber mill here, in the northern Western Plaguelands close to Hearthglen. The timber it cuts is a supply the Crusade prizes. Before the Third War the mill was run by the family of Nathaniel Dumah.", "Northridge_Lumber_Camp" },
  ["ruins of andorhal"] = { "Andorhal was the granary of northern Lordaeron. Its grain went out to the towns of the north, and the necromancer Kel'Thuzad and his Cult of the Damned infected it with the Plague of Undeath before it left.\n\nWhen the Scourge came for the city, its desperate defenders set it ablaze so that the Scourge could not raise more warriors from the dead. Now it lies in ruin. Ghouls drag themselves between the fallen stones, skeletons stand sentinel where guards once did, banshees cry over the ruins and abominations lumber through the wreckage of homes. The lich Araj the Summoner holds the city as his base and oversees the plague cauldrons across the Western Plaguelands.", "Ruins_of_Andorhal" },
  ["scholomance"] = { "The House of Barov kept its manor on this rise above Caer Darrow. To keep its fortune beyond death, the family gave the manor and its crypts to Kel'Thuzad and his Cult of the Damned in exchange for the Lich King's blessing of immortality.\n\nThe cult made the crypts a school of necromancy, and the Barovs remain as undead monsters. The lich Ras Frostwhisper rules over the site and guards it for the Scourge, while the mortal necromancer Darkmaster Gandling serves as its headmaster.", "Scholomance" },
  ["sorrow hill"] = { "In peaceful years Sorrow Hill was where Andorhal buried its dead, rich and poor alike. When the plague came and the Scourge raided without end, the graves multiplied faster than anyone could mark them. Hurried mounds still cover the ground where defenders fell. At last the survivors stopped burying and burned their dead in a mass grave south of the cemetery, afraid of what the plague would make of them. When Prince Arthas Menethil struck down Uther the Lightbringer, a great tomb was raised on the hill and the paladin's torn body laid within it.\n\nEven so, the plague cauldrons raised the dead of the hill. Soldiers and townsfolk stand guard over their own graves.", "Sorrow_Hill" },
  ["thondroril river"] = { "The Thondroril River marks the line between the Western and Eastern Plaguelands. It runs south into Darrowmere Lake and goes on below it as the Darrowmere River. Maggots, vampire bats, plaguebears and spiders crowd its banks.", "Thondroril_River" },
  ["uthers tomb"] = { "Uther's Tomb stands at Sorrow Hill, the one place in the Western Plaguelands where the Light still holds against the dying of the land.\n\nUther the Lightbringer once held Andorhal against the Scourge, standing beside Sage Truthbearer and Ballador the Bright with the Knights of the Silver Hand at his back. Arthas Menethil ended that stand. He brought his undead army against the city and cut down Truthbearer, Ballador and Uther himself.\n\nUther's own soldiers gathered up his body, carried it south of Andorhal's ruins and raised a great tomb over it. His headstone shows him as he was in life, a tome of the Light in one hand and his warhammer in the other. High Priest Thel'danis keeps the tomb as its caretaker and guardian, and the grave is sacred to those who follow the Light.", "Uther's_Tomb" },
  ["weeping cave"] = { "The Weeping Cave opens in the northeastern part of the Western Plaguelands, just west of the Thondroril River. Bog beasts and oozes fill its hollows, with rotting behemoths and decaying horrors shambling in the dark. A rare elemental called the Husk lurks among them.", "Weeping_Cave" },
  ["writhing haunt"] = { "Crops once grew in the furrows of the Writhing Haunt, tended for the sustenance of the living. The Scourge took the fields and set at their center a great cauldron, which has not stopped its work since. A constant spew of disease-filled vapor rolls out across the rows.\n\nRotting ghouls, freezing ghouls and fetid zombies stand watch over it, bound to the place as its wardens. In one of the farm's houses lies Mulgris Deepriver, a tauren druid who came to these lands to heal what the plague had done to nature. He is dying.", "Writhing_Haunt" },
}
areas[1423] = {
  ["blackwood lake"] = { "A town called Blackwood once stood on this shore, home to Grand Marshal Othmar Garithos, who ruled it as baron. Nothing of it remains but the name.\n\nThe water is foul now, and corrupted water elementals coil through it.", "Blackwood_Lake" },
  ["browman mill"] = { "Skeletons, banshees and abominations crowd Browman Mill, a haunted lumber mill just north of Light's Hope Chapel.", "Browman_Mill" },
  ["corins crossing"] = { "Corin's Crossing sits where the roads to Andorhal, Darrowshire, Stratholme and Tyr's Hand meet, between the Infectis Scar and Lake Mereldar. Its town hall and blacksmith still stand among the ruins, but the Scourge fills the streets to the brim. Tutors and summoners of the Cult of the Damned work there, and banshees shriek through the ruins.\n\nAmong the dead move things that cannot be seen at all, the Unseen Servants, and nothing creeps past them unnoticed.", "Corin's_Crossing" },
  ["crown guard tower"] = { "Lordaeron raised scout towers like this one to watch its borders and spot enemies from far off. When the Scourge swept through the kingdom, Crown Guard Tower was destroyed. It stands in ruins now.\n\nThe Horde and the Alliance both contest the broken tower, each hoping to use it to fortify itself in Lordaeron.", "Crown_Guard_Tower" },
  ["darrowshire"] = { "Darrowshire lies in the southern hills of the Eastern Plaguelands. In the Third War the Scourge fell on it, and its defenders were slaughtered.\n\nThey rose again as ghouls, their spirits trapped in rotting flesh, and serve the Scourge. Darrowshire lies silent and in ruins now, a place where only ghosts and memories remain, and the ghost of Pamela Redpath, a little girl, still lingers there.", "Darrowshire" },
  ["eastwall gate"] = { "Eastwall Gate is a side way into Stratholme. It opens straight onto Elder's Square, beside the Alonsus Chapel, where the first paladins of the Silver Hand were ordained before the Second War.\n\nBeyond the gate the Scarlet Crusade and the Scourge fight over the city.", "Eastwall_Gate" },
  ["eastwall tower"] = { "Eastwall Tower stands near Light's Hope Chapel, a scout tower of Lordaeron's. The Scourge invasion destroyed it, and only ruins are left to watch the dead land.\n\nThe Horde and the Alliance both fight over the ruin, each wanting to hold ground in Lordaeron from it.", "Eastwall_Tower" },
  ["fungal vale"] = { "North of the Marris Stead and the Crown Guard Tower lies the Fungal Vale, ground gone dark purple beneath a murky haze. The Plague of Undeath has taken one of its deepest holds here, and the Scourge and the Cult of the Damned move freely through the corruption.", "Fungal_Vale" },
  ["infectis scar"] = { "The Infectis Scar is a deep gorge in the blighted earth near Corin's Crossing.\n\nOozes of living decay and rotting sludge crawl along its floor, and spectral corpses and spectral marauders drift among them.", "Infectis_Scar" },
  ["lake mereldar"] = { "Corrupted water elementals move through Lake Mereldar, a large lake south of Corin's Crossing whose water the plague has polluted. On the shore stand the ruins of an old house, and a shack and ferry that belonged to a farm.", "Lake_Mereldar" },
  ["lights hope chapel"] = { "Among the oldest and most revered holy sites of the Church of the Holy Light, Light's Hope Chapel stands on the slopes of the mountains in the east of the region. A small camp of the Argent Dawn holds it now, and the Dawn takes fighters of every race and counts both the Alliance and the Horde as allies.\n\nInside, Commander Eligor Dawnbringer of the Brotherhood of the Light keeps the peace between the Dawn and the Scarlet Crusade. He has met Scarlet envoys here to plan against Kel'Thuzad and the Scourge.", "Light's_Hope_Chapel" },
  ["marris stead"] = { "The Marris Stead stands on a small ridge beside the main road, the long-time homestead of Nathanos Marris. His cousin Stephon lived here for a while, and Lady Sylvanas Windrunner sometimes visited.\n\nNathanos was killed when the Scourge swept over Lordaeron, then freed by Sylvanas. Now one of the Forsaken, he has come home at her order, with his blighthounds. He recruits Horde soldiers to fight the Scourge and the Scarlet Crusade.", "Marris_Stead" },
  ["mazraalor"] = { "Forest troll shadow hunters of the Mossflayer tribe hold Mazra'Alor, the great ziggurat at the heart of Zul'Mashar. Zul'Mashar is home to Warlord Thresh'jin and Zul'Brin Warpbranch.", "Mazra'Alor" },
  ["northdale"] = { "Banshees and unseen, eyeless watchers haunt the wrecked houses of Northdale, a destroyed town north of Light's Hope Chapel and southeast of the entrance to Quel'Thalas. The lake nearby is full of corrupted water elementals.", "Northdale" },
  ["northpass tower"] = { "Northpass Tower stands farthest north of the watchtowers in the Eastern Plaguelands, marking the road toward Quel'Thalas. The Scourge swept through Lordaeron, and the tower wears its scars still.", "Northpass_Tower" },
  ["noxious glade"] = { "The air in the Noxious Glade hangs thick and sickly green, and the ground is stained dark purple. The glade lies up a hill north of Light's Hope Chapel, and the blight has fouled it worse than the rest of the Plaguelands. The Scourge has built a plague cauldron and a slaughterhouse here, and its creatures move through the poisoned ground.", "Noxious_Glade" },
  ["pestilent scar"] = { "A deep gorge scars the blighted earth of the Eastern Plaguelands. It looks to be a natural one, perhaps a lake that dried up when the Plague of Undeath struck. Oozes still live in its depths.", "Pestilent_Scar" },
  ["plaguewood"] = { "South of Stratholme, this wood was called Greenwood before the plague. It stood green and untroubled, a woodland much like Elwynn Forest, with a small village near the arch at its entrance. When Kel'Thuzad began his work, poisoned grain from Andorhal reached the village. Sergeant Morigan, a Lordaeronian soldier from there, was the first to see that something was wrong. Prince Arthas crossed the woodland on his way to Stratholme.\n\nWhat was left afterward was not a forest anymore. The soil went spongy underfoot, and mushrooms and toadstools swelled to the size of trees, towering over what remained of the old woodland. They breathe out a dark brown gas not unlike the fumes of the Scourge's plague cauldrons. No one can say whether the fungus simply grows in poisoned ground or the Scourge grows it to spread its sickness. The wood took the name Plaguewood from this change.", "Plaguewood" },
  ["quellithien lodge"] = { "Many travelers once stopped at Quel'Lithien Lodge, a Farstrider camp near the entrance to Quel'Thalas. It was one of two major gatherings of high elves in Lordaeron.\n\nBy the Second War, Ranger Lord Hawkspear led the hunters here. The Scourge's invasion of Quel'Thalas brought the lodge hard times, and rangers under him still hold it, with Aurora Skycaller and Kriss Goldenlight among them.", "Quel'Lithien_Lodge" },
  ["scarlet base camp"] = { "The Scarlet Base Camp is a small fortification of barricades on the east bank of Lake Mereldar, not far from Tyr's Hand. Scarlet warders, clerics and enchanters keep it for the Crusade.", "Scarlet_Base_Camp" },
  ["scarlet basilica"] = { "The high seat of the Scarlet Crusade's religious power is the Scarlet Basilica, a great cathedral in Tyr's Hand, where Crusader Lord Valdelmar is found. It stands close to Tyr's Hand Abbey and has ties to the Scarlet Monastery far off in Tirisfal Glades. The building is much like the Cathedral of Light in Stormwind.", "Scarlet_Basilica" },
  ["stratholme"] = { "Stratholme's ruin began with grain. Shipments from Andorhal, infected by the Cult of the Damned, were baked into bread and eaten by a populace who never knew they were dying. Prince Arthas Menethil arrived with Uther the Lightbringer and Jaina Proudmoore to find the plague already spreading. Arthas believed the only way to keep the city from becoming a Scourge stronghold was to purge it, and he ordered the plagued citizens killed. Uther and Jaina thought it madness and refused. Arthas went on with his own soldiers. Fire swept the streets, and almost every soul in Stratholme was massacred. The dreadlord Mal'Ganis, whom Arthas had come to kill, escaped the slaughter.\n\nThe undead moved in, and the city became the Scourge's capital in Lordaeron. Stratholme burns still. The Scarlet Crusade under Grand Crusader Dathrohan holds one half of the ruin, the Scourge under Baron Rivendare the other, and the two sides fight without pause.", "Stratholme" },
  ["terrordale"] = { "Terrordale is a small town in the northwest corner of the Eastern Plaguelands. It sits on the edge of the Plaguewood and is a ghost town now: only the Scourge and plagued animals roam it, apart from the merchant Augustus the Touched and the ghost hunter Egan. Before the Scourge came, Augustus had a great business and bought and sold everything, and he still trades with anyone who passes.", "Terrordale" },
  ["terrorweb tunnel"] = { "Crypt fiends fill the Terrorweb Tunnel, a long, winding passage through the mountain that runs from Terrordale in the north to the Thondroril River in the south. Now and then a Nerubian Overseer, far more powerful than the rest, stalks its length. Tirion Fordring stands at the tunnel's mouth by the river.", "Terrorweb_Tunnel" },
  ["thondroril river"] = { "Tirion Fordring, one of the first paladins of the Silver Hand, keeps to the eastern side of the Thondroril River, near a ruined farmhouse. The river marks the line between the Western and Eastern Plaguelands. It rises in the north and runs south into Darrowmere Lake, and below the lake it goes on as the Darrowmere River.", "Thondroril_River" },
  ["tyrs hand"] = { "Crusader Lord Valdelmar holds Tyr's Hand for the Scarlet Crusade against the Scourge, one of the last strongholds of the living in Lordaeron. Churches fill the fortified city, which the Church of the Holy Light counts among its oldest and most revered holy sites. The Scarlet Basilica and the fortified abbey stand among them, and the walls are heavy. The city sends supplies and reinforcements to other Scarlet strongholds, while couriers fight their way in from Stratholme with daily reports. The land around it is in better shape than the rest of the Plaguelands, though the trees still decay.", "Tyr's_Hand" },
  ["undercroft"] = { "Undead trolls of the Mossflayer tribe have made a crypt in the southwest of the Eastern Plaguelands their den, zombies and unliving Mossflayers alike. Zaeldarr the Outcast, a dire troll risen from the dead, leads them. From time to time his band raids the Argent Dawn's grave pit at Light's Hope Chapel and carries the bodies back to the Undercroft.", "Undercroft" },
  ["zulmashar"] = { "The Mossflayers, a small tribe of forest trolls, once ruled from Zul'Mashar. Long fighting with the humans of Lordaeron threw their land into disarray, and the trolls retook parts of it before the plague of undeath arrived and did more damage still. The city is a shadow of its former glory.", "Zul'Mashar" },
}
areas[1424] = {
  ["azurelode mine"] = { "Iron comes out of this mine for the armories of the Alliance. Miners from Hillsbrad Fields work it under Foreman Bonds, and guards stand watch to protect them. The mine lies in the southwest of the Foothills, south of the fields.", "Azurelode_Mine" },
  ["chillwind point"] = { "Cold wind pours down the ravine here from the Plaguelands, and the rise takes its name from it. The pass once belonged to the kingdom of Lordaeron. An ancient troll ruin crowns the hill. A jungle troll shaman, Bath'rah the Windwatcher, has made the ruin his home and lives there as a hermit. He is a skilled weaponcrafter.", "Chillwind_Point" },
  ["corrahns dagger"] = { "Outlaws of the Syndicate hold this hill. They are human criminals led by nobles of the fallen kingdom of Alterac, and they are enemies of the Alliance and the Horde alike.", "Corrahn's_Dagger" },
  ["crushridge hold"] = { "Near the ruins of Alterac, a cave in an earthen mound shelters a large number of Crushridge ogres, and more of them camp around it. The kingdom of Alterac had made a pact with the Horde in the Second War, and the other human kingdoms struck its capital for the treason. Most of the people fled. Now the ogres hold the empty ruins.", "Crushridge_Hold" },
  ["darrow hill"] = { "Darrow Hill lies in the northern Hillsbrad Foothills, beneath the Headland. Mountain lions and stags range its open fields, and within the Foothill Caverns yetis have made their den, among veins of Alterac granite that run through the rock.", "Darrow_Hill" },
  ["dun garok"] = { "Dwarves hold this keep in the southeastern hills, under Captain Ironhill. They guard the humans of Hillsbrad and watch over the Azurelode Mine. The Forsaken hate having them so close and want them dead.", "Dun_Garok" },
  ["durnholde keep"] = { "In the Second War, Orgrim Doomhammer himself raided Durnholde Keep to free the troll warlord Zul'jin from its cells. Afterward it became the command center of the orc internment camps, ruled by Lieutenant General Aedelas Blackmoore, a self-absorbed drunkard. Blackmoore found an orc infant among the bodies of his murdered parents, named him Thrall and raised him here as a slave and gladiator.\n\nThrall escaped, freed the orcs of the camps, and came back to lay siege to Durnholde. He killed Blackmoore in single combat and called on the spirit of the earth to level the fortress.\n\nThe keep lies in ruins now, east of Tarren Mill. It once held inner barracks, a mage tower and an arena. The Syndicate holds it as its base in the Foothills, and from here its outlaws steal horses and shipments from Southshore.", "Durnholde_Keep" },
  ["eastern strand"] = { "The Eastern Strand runs along the southeastern coast of the Hillsbrad Foothills, its counterpart lying further west along the same shore. This stretch of sand belongs to the Daggerspine naga, who hold the beaches and the shallow water beyond them.", "Eastern_Strand" },
  ["gallows corner"] = { "At Gallows' Corner, where the roads meet in the Alterac Mountains near the Ruins of Alterac, a scout tower stands broken and empty. Crushridge ogres now hold the crossing, prowling the stones around the tower's remains, and nothing else stirs there.", "Gallows'_Corner" },
  ["gavins naze"] = { "Mountain lions roam this hill, and the rare Araga is sometimes among them.\n\nDwarven battleguards of the Stormpike Guard watch the tunnel here that leads into Alterac Valley, where their clan fights the Frostwolf orcs. Sergeant Durgen Stormpike keeps watch with them and has no kind word for the Frostwolves.", "Gavin's_Naze" },
  ["growless cave"] = { "Deep in this cave in the Alterac Mountains stands the Flame of Uzel, an old ceremonial pyre. Mountain yetis shelter in the cave around it, and a yeti named Skhowl is often found just outside its mouth.", "Growless_Cave" },
  ["headland"] = { "A mountain lion with a slight mane and a striped tail rests on the cliffside of the Headland. She is Araga, and no other cat in the hills looks like her.\n\nDwarves of the Stormpike Guard keep a post here, close to the way into Alterac Valley, where their clan is at open war with the Frostwolf orcs. Lieutenant Haggerdin enlists recruits for the Guard.", "Headland" },
  ["hillsbrad fields"] = { "Apple orchards and pumpkin patches cover the fields of Hillsbrad, with cattle grazing between them, and the harvest feeds all of Hillsbrad Foothills. Orgrim Doomhammer had the town destroyed in the Second War as a gruesome example, and the news spread through the human kingdoms. It was rebuilt after the war.\n\nHillsbrad and Southshore are the only parts of former Lordaeron the Third War did not leave completely devastated, and neither joined the Scarlet Crusade. Their guards still wear the tabard of Lordaeron. Magistrate Burnside governs from the town hall, aided by the Council of Hillsbrad and the clerk Horrace Whitesteed.\n\nThe Forsaken at Tarren Mill under High Executor Darthalia threaten the farms. The farmers answer with ambushes, armed by the blacksmith Verringtan and his apprentices.", "Hillsbrad_Fields" },
  ["misty shore"] = { "The Misty Shore runs along Lordamere Lake, between Dandred's Fold and Dalaran. Turtles move slowly through its shallows.\n\nTwo humans allied with the Scarlet Crusade hold this shore, falling on any who come too near.\n\nAcross the water, the Dawning Isles can be seen, with the eerie Fenris Keep in the distance.", "Misty_Shore" },
  ["nethander stead"] = { "Nethander Stead lies just east of Southshore, its fields abandoned and overrun by Mudsnout gnolls, the only place their tribe is found. Giant white mushrooms called Mudsnout Blossoms grow wild there.", "Nethander_Stead" },
  ["purgation isle"] = { "A towering spiral of rock rises from the water south of the Hillsbrad shore, with a cemetery on its top and the ruins of a scout tower. Before the First War the island belonged to Kul Tiras.\n\nDozens of condemned human and dwarf spirits haunt it, paladins and priests, monks and mages, who cannot find rest.", "Purgation_Isle" },
  ["ravenholdt manor"] = { "Lord Jorach Ravenholdt leads his Assassin's League from this hidden manor in the Alterac Mountains. A narrow path climbs to it from a plateau, twisting through the mountains and a small cave. The League takes in only those of extraordinary skill, and a stranger who leaves without a blade in the back or poison in the veins has done well.\n\nFor years the League has fought the Syndicate. Its agents steal Syndicate emblems, since a member who loses his emblem is dealt with by his own side.", "Ravenholdt_Manor" },
  ["slaughter hollow"] = { "The Crushridge ogres make their home in Slaughter Hollow, an ogre mound set deep in the Alterac Mountains.\n\nA captured gnome, Rizz Loosebolt, is kept in a cage among them. He begs anyone who passes not to let the ogres eat him, and trades engineering supplies through the bars.", "Slaughter_Hollow" },
  ["soferas naze"] = { "The Syndicate holds this hill above Hillsbrad. Its footpads and thieves camp here, and some of them have been at the Southshore stout.", "Sofera's_Naze" },
  ["southpoint tower"] = { "The Forsaken Deathstalker Lesh keeps watch from this ruined scout tower at the far western edge of Hillsbrad Foothills, just south of the road to Silverpine Forest. She spies on the Alliance activity in the area.", "Southpoint_Tower" },
  ["strahnbrad"] = { "Strahnbrad stands on the northeastern foothills of the Alterac Mountains, a ghost town with an abandoned town hall and blacksmith. It once belonged to the Kingdom of Alterac, and after the Second War it passed to Lordaeron.\n\nShortly before the Third War, Blackrock orcs raided it, taking slaves to sacrifice to the Burning Legion. Only a handful of footmen guarded the town, but it got word to the Knights of the Silver Hand. Uther the Lightbringer went after the orc camp, and Prince Arthas Menethil rode to hold the town. The raid had begun when he arrived and many villagers died, but Arthas and the surviving defenders routed the orcs and killed the Slave Master who led them.\n\nNow the Syndicate holds the town as its main base.", "Strahnbrad" },
  ["tarren mill"] = { "Early in the Second War, high elven archers from Silvermoon came south to see the orc threat. They were ambushed in the grasslands near Tarren Mill and taken to a nearby prison camp. King Terenas sent Alliance soldiers, who freed them. The Council of Silvermoon then committed the armies of Quel'Thalas to the Alliance of Lordaeron.\n\nThe Forsaken hold the town now. A church, a few houses and withered orchards stand in disrepair. From here they make war on the Alliance towns of Southshore and Dun Garok. The ground between Tarren Mill and Southshore is infamous for constant battle.", "Tarren_Mill" },
  ["thoradins wall"] = { "Soon after the kingdom of Arathor was founded and Strom became its capital, King Thoradin ordered a great wall built to shield humanity from the Amani trolls. It runs between Hillsbrad Foothills and the Arathi Highlands.\n\nAfter many centuries the wall is heavily damaged and in ruins.", "Thoradin's_Wall" },
  ["western strand"] = { "The Western Strand lies on the southwest coast of Hillsbrad Foothills. Torn Fin murlocs have raised small camps along its shore, and Andy Murgole patrols the area.", "Western_Strand" },
}
areas[1425] = {
  ["aerie peak"] = { "The Wildhammers went home to Grim Batol after the War of the Three Hammers and found the fortress tainted by the death of Modgud. They could not live there. Having fought the Dark Iron dwarves in the tunnels, they would not burrow into stone again, and built Aerie Peak on the outside of a mountain instead.\n\nA great gryphon aviary crests its middle, and the Wildhammer keep stands at the far end.", "Aerie_Peak" },
  ["agolwatha"] = { "Agol'watha was raised as a temple of the Amani Empire, and the empire is gone. Green sludges and jade oozes have overrun it, crawling through its ruined courts.", "Agol'watha" },
  ["altar of zul"] = { "The Vilebranch forest trolls keep an altar here. It is one of the ancient bastions of the Amani, and the Vilebranch, strongest of the three troll tribes of the Hinterlands, hold it along with Jintha'Alor. Qiaga the Keeper stands at its top.", "Altar_of_Zul" },
  ["bogens ledge"] = { "Bogen's Ledge is a cave in the southwestern Hinterlands. Vicious owlbeasts prowl around it.", "Bogen's_Ledge" },
  ["creeping ruin"] = { "Forest trolls built the Creeping Ruin, and hostile slimes have overrun what they left. They creep through the broken stone.", "Creeping_Ruin" },
  ["featherbeards hovel"] = { "A small house stands next to Aerie Peak, the home of Featherbeard.", "Featherbeard's_Hovel" },
  ["hiriwatha"] = { "Witherbark forest trolls live among the old ruins of Hiri'watha, south of Quel'Danil Lodge. When emissaries of the Horde came to the Hinterlands, the Witherbark refused them outright. Zul'arek Hatefowler, a Witherbark troll, is sometimes seen there.", "Hiri'watha" },
  ["jinthaalor"] = { "Trolls of the Amani Empire raised Jintha'Alor and named it for Jintha, one of their ancient leaders. Among forest troll cities only Zul'Aman is larger. The Vilebranch hold it now and use its tiers as a fortress for sacrifice.\n\nVile Priestess Hexx rules here in the name of Hakkar the Soulflayer. The Vilebranch prey on the weaker Revantusk to feed him. Elder Torntusk was taken in one raid and is held in the city still, and his guard captain, Huntsman Malkhor, was killed, his remains thrown to the wolves and oozes within the walls.", "Jintha'Alor" },
  ["overlook cliffs"] = { "The great river that runs through the Hinterlands ends at the Overlook Cliffs, pouring over the edge in one massive waterfall where the mountains give way to the eastern shoreline. The cliffs are steep and deceptively high, and many travelers have misjudged the drop and fallen to their deaths.", "Overlook_Cliffs" },
  ["plaguemist ravine"] = { "A mist hangs over Plaguemist Ravine, a narrow pathway that runs north through the mountains toward Caer Darrow in the Western Plaguelands. The entrance lies in the woods west of Quel'Danil Lodge. The ravine takes its name from the sickly mist that fouls this one path and stands in sharp contrast to the clean freshness of the rest of the Hinterlands. No one lives in it.", "Plaguemist_Ravine" },
  ["queldanil lodge"] = { "The Highvale elves keep this lodge as their base near Aerie Peak. It is the larger of the two great gatherings of high elves left in Lordaeron, and one of the last high elven settlements.\n\nAfter the Third War the lodge became a center of communication. Highvale ambassadors reached out to the other peoples of the Alliance and shared what they knew of the Witherbark trolls. The elves later allied with the Wildhammer dwarves, which angered the Revantusk trolls.", "Quel'Danil_Lodge" },
  ["revantusk village"] = { "When the Second War broke the Old Horde, many forest trolls deserted their orc allies without a moment's hesitation. The Revantusk kept some loyalty to the Horde. They were too few to free the captive orcs, who in any case seemed uninterested in freedom, and other forest trolls attacked them for it. The tribe moved to the coast of the Hinterlands, hoping to avoid notice, and built this village.\n\nIn time they learned that Thrall had gathered a new Horde and led his people back to their shamanistic traditions. They sent their fastest runners to him, and the two made a pact of friendship. Other forest trolls call the Revantusk weaklings and enemies, and the Revantusk call them traitors to the new Horde.", "Revantusk_Village" },
  ["seradane"] = { "A great circular portal stands in the northeastern Hinterlands, one of four entrances to the Emerald Dream. Green dragons made this forest their home, among them the drakes Rothos and Dreamtracker.\n\nGreen dragonspawn keep most of the woods. A Verdantine Oracle leads each group, with Verdantine Boughguards and Tree Warders beside it. The skull of an unknown dragon rests on a hill north of the portal.", "Seradane" },
  ["shadraalor"] = { "The Witherbark tribe keeps its main settlement at Shadra'Alor, among old temples that the forest trolls of the Amani Empire raised. The Witherbark worship the loa Shadra here.", "Shadra'Alor" },
  ["shaolwatha"] = { "Little remains of Shaol'watha, an old forest troll temple in the northeastern Hinterlands.", "Shaol'watha" },
  ["shindiggers camp"] = { "Rhapsody Shindigger, a dwarf fond of drink, keeps his small camp in the Hinterlands alongside the high elf Gilveradin Sunchaser, an odd pairing.", "Shindigger's_Camp" },
  ["skulk rock"] = { "During the Second War the Old Horde made a base in this forest troll ruin, until the Alliance of Lordaeron drove them out.\n\nSlimes have overrun the ruin since. A cave here is one of the few places where Ghost Mushrooms still grow.", "Skulk_Rock" },
  ["valorwind lake"] = { "Northeast of Shadra'Alor lies Valorwind Lake, a small and quiet body of water. Its waters seem unremarkable, yet it is the only known place where the Violet Tragan, a mysterious mushroom, grows.", "Valorwind_Lake" },
  ["wildhammer keep"] = { "At one end of Aerie Peak, opposite the great gryphon aviary, stands the keep of the Wildhammer dwarves. Falstad Wildhammer, their High Thane, rules from the top of the peak.\n\nMost dwarves cut their halls into the mountain. The Wildhammers build in the open air, a habit they took from the horror of the underground battles they fought against the Dark Iron dwarves.", "Wildhammer_Keep" },
  ["zunwatha"] = { "Among the old ruins of Zun'watha the Witherbark trolls have settled. Scalpers and zealots of the tribe keep watch among the stones.", "Zun'watha" },
}
areas[1426] = {
  ["amberstill ranch"] = { "The Amberstill dwarves keep mountain rams, the kind dwarves ride, at their ranch off the road to Loch Modan, north of Gol'Bolar Quarry. Young Yarlyn Amberstill looks after a pen of snowshoe rabbits. A wendigo named Vagash stalks the herd, and Rudra Amberstill wants it driven off.", "Amberstill_Ranch" },
  ["anvilmar"] = { "In Coldridge Valley stands Anvilmar, the first town of any size in the north of the valley. A hall built around a great forge is at its center.", "Anvilmar" },
  ["brewnall village"] = { "The Barleybrew family has made its home in Brewnall Village, in Chill Breeze Valley near Gnomeregan. Rejold Barleybrew leads their search for the best recipe for ale, and the family works to improve the old dwarven art of brewing. They send their young out to guard the village from wild animals and to hunt for rare ingredients for the ultimate ale.\n\nThe village is more a camp than a settlement. It answers to Ironforge, though it sits in gnomish country.", "Brewnall_Village" },
  ["chill breeze valley"] = { "Blood shows on the snow in the north and south arms of Chill Breeze Valley. Large stains spread across the white, and torn animal carcasses lie where they were dropped. Few people walk these branches. Hunters go after the beasts responsible, hoping to end their threat to the dwarven lands, and many do not come back. A dangerous wendigo lairs in one of the caves.\n\nThe east and west arm is easier going. It runs between Gnomeregan and Kharanos, and young dwarves and gnomes pass along it often.", "Chill_Breeze_Valley" },
  ["coldridge pass"] = { "Troggs have overrun Coldridge Pass, the tunnel that joins Coldridge Valley to the rest of Dun Morogh. Dwarven recruits come through in a trickle on their way to the frontier town of Kharanos, and they must cut a path through the troggs or run past them.", "Coldridge_Pass" },
  ["coldridge valley"] = { "Cut off in the southwestern corner of Dun Morogh, Coldridge Valley lies under thick snow. Its only way out is the tunnel of Coldridge Pass to the northeast. Dwarven mountaineers keep guard here, though troggs and Frostmane trolls trouble the valley.\n\nThe troggs have turned up all over the hills to the southeast and around the frozen lake, and the trolls keep mostly to a cave in the south.", "Coldridge_Valley" },
  ["frostmane hold"] = { "Frostmane Hold stands on the western edge of Dun Morogh's cold mountains, south of Gnomeregan, a scatter of buildings knit to a cave system driven into the mountainside. It is the base camp of the Frostmane ice trolls, who hold the ground with headhunters and hideskinners guarding every approach.", "Frostmane_Hold" },
  ["gates of ironforge"] = { "The Gates of Ironforge are a grand sight, visible from far across Dun Morogh. They stand at the head of the great ramp that climbs north to the mountain, and they open into Ironforge itself. Just inside, a huge statue of High King Modimus Anvilmar stands guard.\n\nHe was the last king of the united dwarves, a ruler of justice and wisdom, known for his diplomacy with the three great dwarven clans. When he died of old age, before his eldest son could be crowned, those clans went to war with one another.", "Gates_of_Ironforge" },
  ["gnomeregan"] = { "The gnomes built Gnomeregan underground, in the foothills of Dun Morogh, and filled it with iron and invention. During the Third War, mutant troggs infested the city. In a desperate attempt to destroy them, High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque ordered the waste tanks vented and filled the halls with radiation. The gnomes sheltered from the fumes and waited for the troggs to die or flee. The troggs sickened, but they kept coming. Nearly four in five gnomes died within days, and the survivors fled to Ironforge. Others were twisted into leper gnomes, mindless and cruel.\n\nSicco Thermaplugg, once Mekkatorque's trusted advisor, was driven mad by the poison. He now rules the ruined halls as a techno-overlord over troggs and leper gnomes.", "Gnomeregan" },
  ["golbolar quarry"] = { "In the southeast of Dun Morogh, dwarves keep a small mining camp beside Gol'Bolar Quarry, a large dig site. Troggs have overrun the excavation and the mine below, and the miners hold only the camp.", "Gol'Bolar_Quarry" },
  ["grizzled den"] = { "The wendigos of the Grizzled Den do not scatter when strangers come. They flock together, breathe frost, and drive intruders back out of this large cave system just southwest of Kharanos. Hunters go in after their manes, which are prized for seat covers. Copper veins run through the rock, and a tougher wendigo lairs at the back.", "Grizzled_Den" },
  ["helms bed lake"] = { "Ice covers most of Helm's Bed Lake, but one hole is kept open so that fresh water can be drawn. The ice is safe to cross. The shore is not, because wild animals and stray troggs roam around it.\n\nSouth of the lake, Dark Iron dwarves have taken an abandoned building, and they strike at anyone who comes too near.", "Helm's_Bed_Lake" },
  ["iceflow lake"] = { "Iceflow Lake freezes hard in the cold of Dun Morogh, but the dwarves of Brewnall Village keep one hole cut through the ice, drawing fresh water and fish from beneath the frozen surface.\n\nOut toward the lake's center rise a pair of islands, best left alone. A pack of ravenous wolves has claimed them, running under a worg named Timber, and their presence keeps the islands empty of all but the beasts that hold them.", "Iceflow_Lake" },
  ["ironbands compound"] = { "South of Helm's Bed Lake, Ironband's Compound stands as a foothold for Dark Iron dwarves, who strike at anyone who strays too near. It once belonged to Sturgy Ironband, a master blacksmith who taught his craft there, until a band of Dark Irons attacked it and killed everyone inside.", "Ironband's_Compound" },
  ["kharanos"] = { "Kharanos sits among the mountain peaks of Dun Morogh, a bustling little town ringed by wintry wilds. A large forge burns here, a gnomish workshop clatters away, and the Thunderbrew Distillery pours some of the best ale in the world. Behind the charm, Kharanos is a hub of military activity, defending against every threat the wilds send.\n\nA large cavern full of fierce wendigo lies directly to the west. To the northwest, Frostmane trolls have rooted themselves on top of Shimmer Ridge, watching the town below, and Senir Whitebeard is set on driving them out.", "Kharanos" },
  ["misty pine refuge"] = { "Misty Pine Refuge is a small outpost of the Argent Dawn in central Dun Morogh. Father Gavin is the only agent of the order stationed there.", "Misty_Pine_Refuge" },
  ["north gate outpost"] = { "The northeasternmost settlement the dwarves have built in Dun Morogh stands in the middle of North Gate Pass, which links Dun Morogh to Loch Modan. Mountaineers are stationed here, and they are kept busy driving off the wildlife that presses in from the mountains. Some of them went hunting the fiercer beasts and have gone missing.", "North_Gate_Outpost" },
  ["north gate pass"] = { "North Gate Pass carries the road from the high mountains of Dun Morogh down to the lower mountains of Loch Modan. Travelers heading to or from the Wetlands use it most, since it lies closest to the descent from the loch. The guard station of North Gate Outpost stands here.", "North_Gate_Pass" },
  ["shimmer ridge"] = { "Frostmane trolls hold the high ground of Shimmer Ridge, west of the Gates of Ironforge. They live in two camps joined by a bridge, and they tame boars and grow shimmerweed. The dwarves use the weed in their beer, but it is the trolls who tend it.", "Shimmer_Ridge" },
  ["south gate outpost"] = { "Dwarves keep South Gate Outpost in the middle of the pass that leads down into Loch Modan, with snow on the ground around it. The mountaineers stationed here have cleared out most of the wildlife that pressed in on them, so they face less trouble than most garrisons in these mountains. Many travelers pass through on their way to or from Thelsamar.", "South_Gate_Outpost" },
  ["south gate pass"] = { "Travelers bound for Thelsamar take South Gate Pass, which joins the high mountains of Dun Morogh to the lower mountains of Loch Modan. It is the pass closest to the dwarven town. The guard station of South Gate Outpost stands here.", "South_Gate_Pass" },
  ["steelgrills depot"] = { "Steelgrill's Depot sits east of Kharanos, a small camp of miners and engineers working the frozen ground of Dun Morogh. It belongs to Beldin Steelgrill, and pilots of the Siege Brigade wait here while he works on their siege engines.", "Steelgrill's_Depot" },
  ["thunderbrew distillery"] = { "The ale of the Thunderbrew Distillery is known far and wide. The dwarven tavern in Kharanos was founded, so Ragnar Thunderbrew says, by his father, Arkilus Thunderbrew. Ragnar has run it since his older brother Grimbooze disappeared. Innkeeper Belm keeps the inn.", "Thunderbrew_Distillery" },
  ["tundrid hills"] = { "Few travelers cross the Tundrid Hills without a weapon in hand. Several kinds of hostile wildlife roam these hills in the south of central Dun Morogh, west of Gol'Bolar Quarry. A few still take them as a shortcut between Coldridge Valley and eastern Dun Morogh, though the road through Kharanos is far safer.", "Tundrid_Hills" },
}
areas[1427] = {
  ["blackchar cave"] = { "Blackchar Cave, in the southwest of the gorge near Blackrock Mountain, is sealed off. What it holds is a mystery.", "Blackchar_Cave" },
  ["blackrock mountain"] = { "The Bronzebeard and Wildhammer armies were marching to purge the Dark Iron clan when the ridge burst upward into Blackrock Mountain. King Madoran and King Khardros turned their armies back rather than face the Firelord who had come with it. Where the Dark Iron kingdom had stood, a volcano rose, and Ragnaros withdrew into its core and set the surviving dwarves to carve a new city beneath it.\n\nTwo powers share the mountain now. Ragnaros rules from the molten core with his Dark Iron dwarves, golems and elementals. Nefarian watches from Blackwing Lair above, with the Dark Horde of Blackrock orcs at his command. Each means to raise an army and take Azeroth.", "Blackrock_Mountain" },
  ["cauldron"] = { "The Dark Irons dug the Cauldron into the middle of the gorge, a vast excavation rimmed by their great digging machines, and they fortified it after Ragnaros was summoned. Fire and magma elementals move through the glowing crevices below. Slaves, mostly captured enemies, labor there without rest. The work, the heat and their masters have driven them mad, and they attack anyone who interferes, even those who come to free them.", "Cauldron" },
  ["dustfire valley"] = { "Wind sweeps Dustfire Valley as it runs north through the gorge. At its end stand the gates of Stonewrought Pass, and beyond them lies the Valley of Kings in Loch Modan.", "Dustfire_Valley" },
  ["firewatch ridge"] = { "Firewatch Ridge is the main camp of the Dark Iron dwarves in Searing Gorge, overlooking the great excavation of the Cauldron. Dark Iron dwarves hold the slopes with mechanical golems and giant flame spiders, making the ridge a dangerous place to tread.", "Firewatch_Ridge" },
  ["grimesilt dig site"] = { "Dwarves from Ironforge once dug at the Grimesilt Dig Site. The Dark Iron dwarves took it over and hold it still. Dorius Stonetender, one of the original workers, lies injured at the bottom of the pit.", "Grimesilt_Dig_Site" },
  ["sea of cinders"] = { "The Sea of Cinders lies flat and open in the southern reaches of Searing Gorge, just north of Blackrock Mountain. Nothing grows there. The ground still smolders, and ash rises constantly on the wind, drifting across the barren flats. Fire and wind left nothing behind but waste, and the burning has never stopped.", "Sea_of_Cinders" },
  ["slag pit"] = { "Deep inside the Cauldron lies the Slag Pit, an underground mine rich in ore. Cruel Dark Iron dwarves run it, and slave laborers do the digging while slavers and taskmasters drive them on. The Dark Irons answer anyone who interferes with their mining with open hostility, and ravenous incendosaurs prowl the depths.", "Slag_Pit" },
  ["stonewrought pass"] = { "Franclorn Forgewright, the Dark Iron architect who also oversaw the Stonewrought Dam, designed this tunnel through the mountains. It runs northeast from the head of Dustfire Valley to the Valley of Kings in Loch Modan.\n\nWhen the Dark Irons overran the dwarven garrisons in the gorge, the dwarves fell back to Loch Modan and sealed the pass. Its gates are still locked, and Mountaineer Pebblebitty guards the one on the Loch Modan side, turning travelers away.", "Stonewrought_Pass" },
  ["tanner camp"] = { "Sarah Tanner, a master of elemental leatherworking, keeps a lone tent in this small camp. Behind it a long passage winds off to the lair of a great thunder lizard. Glassweb spiders and fire beetles crawl about the camp, and a lava spider called Rekk'tilac hunts among them.", "Tanner_Camp" },
  ["thorium point"] = { "Overseer Oilfist leads the Thorium Brotherhood, Dark Iron dwarves who left their clan. They built Thorium Point on the cliffs in the far north of the gorge, above the Cauldron, and from there they keep a close eye on what their former kin are doing. The town is neutral, with a forge and anvil ready for work. The Brotherhood's smiths are said to be among the finest Azeroth has seen, and rumor says they know how to make the arms of Ragnaros himself but lack the hands to gather what the work needs.", "Thorium_Point" },
}
areas[1428] = {
  ["blackrock mountain"] = { "Blackrock Mountain bore its name centuries before the Blackrock orcs came to it. It rose out of the ruined Dark Iron kingdom when Ragnaros broke into the world. The Bronzebeard and Wildhammer armies had marched to purge the Dark Irons. Their kings, Madoran and Khardros, watched firestorms swallow the land and turned the armies back toward their kingdoms. Ragnaros withdrew into the blistering heart of the mountain and set the surviving Dark Irons to carve a fortress beneath it, Shadowforge City.\n\nIn the Second War the Horde took Blackrock Spire, the fortress in the mountain, and made it the base for its campaign against Lordaeron. Now Ragnaros and Nefarian fight over the whole mountain. The Firelord rules from the volcano's core with his Dark Irons and his elementals. Nefarian holds the heights with the Blackrock orcs and his black dragons.", "Blackrock_Mountain" },
  ["blackrock pass"] = { "Blackrock Pass is the threshold between two worlds. On one side lie the quiet green hills of the Redridge Mountains. On the other, the Burning Steppes wait, scorched and twisted beyond recognition. A gate stands at the border and marks the line plainly. It is the main way into the Steppes from the south.", "Blackrock_Pass" },
  ["blackrock stronghold"] = { "Hard under the shadow of Blackrock Spire, a fortress of black stone, the Blackrock clan holds a massive stronghold in the north of the Burning Steppes. These orcs keep out of sight of the road and always watch for an Alliance attack. They follow the old brutal ways of the First and Second Wars and mine the mountains around the Spire for the metal in their weapons and armor.\n\nBattlemasters, slayers, sorcerers and warlocks of the clan hold the stronghold, and sentries of the Scarshield Legion stand guard among them.", "Blackrock_Stronghold" },
  ["dracodar"] = { "Scorched hills fill the southwest of the Burning Steppes, blackened and given over to the black dragonflight. Its minions crawl across the ground in numbers.", "Draco'dar" },
  ["dreadmaul rock"] = { "The Firegut ogres make Dreadmaul Rock, also called Dreadmaul Peak, their base. Paths lead to the top of the rock, and a complicated cave system of ogre mounds runs within it.\n\nThe Fireguts are castoffs of the Second War. Some study arcane magic, though their huge bodies still make them better fighters than casters. They mean to claim the Burning Steppes for ogres alone, and the Alliance, the Blackrock orcs, the Dark Iron dwarves and the black dragons will not let them.", "Dreadmaul_Rock" },
  ["flame crest"] = { "Only a crude bridge leads onto the bare outcropping of rock where Flame Crest stands. The outpost belongs to neither the Horde nor the Alliance. In a small cave at the back lives Kibler, who keeps a talkative parrot named Opus. Mathredis Firestar, hungry for magic, bargains for powerful arcane components.", "Flame_Crest" },
  ["morgans vigil"] = { "Fire has been through Morgan's Vigil, in the southeastern corner of the Burning Steppes. Its houses are burned to the ground and its one mage tower has crumbled. The Alliance holds what is left, and its people live in tents among the wreckage. Marshal Maxwell is here, seeking word of a high-ranking officer of Stormwind.", "Morgan's_Vigil" },
  ["pillar of ash"] = { "A tall tower of scorched stone rises at the eastern end of this camp, which the Blackrock orcs hold. Slayers, sorcerers and warlocks of the clan keep it, with their worgs beside them.", "Pillar_of_Ash" },
  ["ruins of thaurissan"] = { "These ashen flats in the Burning Steppes were green once, when the city of Thaurissan stood here. The Dark Iron clan, exiled from Ironforge, founded it, and Sorcerer-Thane Thaurissan ruled it.\n\nThe clan was losing the War of the Three Hammers. Their army had just been broken against the strongholds of the other two clans when Thaurissan gathered seven of his most trusted dwarves. Desperate to turn the tide, he called on the powers sleeping beneath the world for a servant to win the war back. Ragnaros, Elemental Lord of Fire, answered. A volcano split the mountains and burned the valley to ash, and the city was destroyed. The surviving Dark Irons, enslaved to the Firelord, left the ruins for a new home beneath the mountain that rose there.", "Ruins_of_Thaurissan" },
  ["slither rock"] = { "Hidden beyond the Terror Wing Path, in the far northeastern corner of the Burning Steppes, a small cave holds one dweller. Cyrus Therepentous, a black dragonspawn, lives here alone. He will help others against a common foe, though never without rude and derisive comments.", "Slither_Rock" },
  ["terror wing path"] = { "Black drakes patrol the valley called Terror Wing Path, in the eastern Burning Steppes. Whelps and dragonkin of the black dragonflight nest among its rocks and ash.", "Terror_Wing_Path" },
}
areas[1429] = {
  ["brackwell pumpkin patch"] = { "In southeast Elwynn Forest stands a farmstead that once belonged to the Brackwell family. The Defias Brotherhood holds it now, and no one can say what became of the Brackwells. They simply vanished from its fields and fences.\n\nOne thing is still told of them. Their prize sow, Princess, grew enormous on vegetables she stole by creeping over to the neighboring Stonefield Farm.", "Brackwell_Pumpkin_Patch" },
  ["crystal lake"] = { "East of Goldshire, murlocs crawl over the shores of Crystal Lake and wade its shallows in numbers. The water is dangerous for anyone who comes to it unready.", "Crystal_Lake" },
  ["eastvale logging camp"] = { "Mill camps stand scattered through eastern Elwynn Forest, with living quarters between them. Together they make up Eastvale Logging Camp. Buyers send in orders for lumber without pause, and the lumberjacks work to strict deadlines.\n\nThe forest presses close. Wolves and bears attack the camps so often that workers are driven off and deliveries put at risk, and the Stormwind Guard has had to call for outside help to thin them.", "Eastvale_Logging_Camp" },
  ["echo ridge mine"] = { "Marshal McBride keeps sending men into Echo Ridge Mine, in the northwest of Northshire Valley, to thin the kobolds that have taken over its tunnels. The humans living nearby are frustrated by them.", "Echo_Ridge_Mine" },
  ["fargodeep mine"] = { "Stormwind once took gold from Fargodeep Mine. The shafts stand abandoned now, and a clan of kobolds holds them, miners and tunnelers alike. The deepest workings are called the Lower Mines.", "Fargodeep_Mine" },
  ["forests edge"] = { "A swath of forest southwest of Stormwind reaches like a peninsula between the Nazferiti river and a smaller stream running toward Westfall. Boars root through the undergrowth, and copper veins show along the riverbanks.\n\nA band of Riverpaw gnolls holds the woods under their chieftain Hogger, who raids farms and villages across Elwynn Forest. The Stormwind army has put a bounty on him. Rumor ties the pack to the Defias Brotherhood, which is said to supply it with weapons.", "Forest's_Edge" },
  ["goldshire"] = { "Goldshire stands at the center of Elwynn Forest, one of the small towns that grew up around Stormwind. In the First War it and Moonbrook were the last two towns between the orcs and the capital. Warchief Blackhand took both to cut King Llane Wrynn off from his people before the sack of Stormwind Keep. After the Second War the Alliance of Lordaeron rebuilt the town, and Supreme Commander Turalyon oversaw the return of the kingdom's scattered citizens.\n\nMarshal Dughan of the Stormwind Guard keeps order here now. The hearth and ale of the Lion's Pride Inn, one of the most respectable ale houses in Azeroth, draw travelers from every road that meets here. Murlocs from Stone Cairn Lake have killed townsfolk, two guards among them, and the guards fear that fighting will reach the town.", "Goldshire" },
  ["heroes vigil"] = { "On an island in Stone Cairn Lake, a ring of standing stones surrounds the Stone of Remembrance. It honors the dead of the First War, when the orcs sacked Goldshire and the lands around it. Rogue wizards hold the island now.", "Heroes'_Vigil" },
  ["jasperlode mine"] = { "Kobolds have overrun the tunnels of Jasperlode Mine, which once gave Stormwind the raw ore it needed for its wars. The army, stretched thin by other threats, has no men to spare to drive them out.\n\nThe kobolds are not alone down there. A colony of mine spiders has settled in the deepest part, and to them the kobolds are a meal. Their queen, Mother Fang, does not welcome uninvited guests.", "Jasperlode_Mine" },
  ["jerods landing"] = { "The Defias Brotherhood holds Jerod's Landing, a boat landing on the Elwynn Forest bank of the Nazferiti river. Its Dockmaster and his men use it to smuggle stolen goods from the Redridge Mountains to Klaven's Tower in Westfall.", "Jerod's_Landing" },
  ["maclure vineyards"] = { "The Maclure family tends long rows of vine in southern Elwynn Forest. They are in a bitter rivalry with the Stonefield family nearby, and neither side hides its dislike. Yet a child of each family has fallen for the other's.", "Maclure_Vineyards" },
  ["mirror lake"] = { "Mirror Lake lies small and quiet in western Elwynn Forest, its waterfall spilling bright beside a nearby orchard, the kind of place that looks made for swimming or fishing. Defias bandits have taken to the woods around it now, and what should be a place of rest has become one to cross carefully.", "Mirror_Lake" },
  ["mirror lake orchard"] = { "On the southern bank of Mirror Lake stands an orchard with a cottage beside it. Defias bandits have taken both for themselves.", "Mirror_Lake_Orchard" },
  ["northshire abbey"] = { "Centuries ago, clerics of the Church of the Holy Light came south from Lordaeron and founded the Holy Order of Northshire Clerics at this abbey. They comforted Stormwind in hard times, and Medivh, the Guardian of Tirisfal, lay in a coma under their care for years.\n\nIn the First War the clerics stood with Stormwind's armies. When the ogre lord Turok stole the Tome of Divinity, Sir Anduin Lothar led an expedition to take it back, and the tome returned to the abbey. Shortly after the half-orc Garona Halforcen was brought there as a prisoner, the orcs overran the abbey and destroyed it. Refugees fled toward Lordaeron with sacred tomes pulled from the wreckage.\n\nIn the Second War, Cho'gall's Twilight's Hammer clan made the ruins its headquarters. After the Alliance of Lordaeron reclaimed Elwynn Forest, Archbishop Alonsus Faol helped rebuild the abbey and found the Brotherhood of Northshire, which holds it now. Its main hall, hall of arms and library wing stand in Northshire Valley.", "Northshire_Abbey" },
  ["northshire valley"] = { "Impenetrable mountains close in Northshire Valley on every side but one. A pass leads out to the south, protected by thick stone walls and a guarded gate. A river runs through the valley and empties into Crystal Lake.\n\nMarshal Douglas McBride of the Stormwind Guard commands the abbey's garrison and answers for the defense of the valley. His guards still have trouble keeping the peace. Wolves that fled from Duskwood have grown in number in the woods, kobolds infest Echo Ridge Mine, and Defias thugs have taken the vineyards.", "Northshire_Valley" },
  ["northshire vineyards"] = { "Defias bandits drove the farmers out of the Northshire Vineyards, which lie east of the abbey across the bridge. The Brotherhood's agents hold the rows now and keep a tent among the vines. Garrick Padfoot, a cutthroat who has plagued the farmers and merchants for weeks, has gathered a gang of thugs around him there, and there is a bounty on his head.", "Northshire_Vineyards" },
  ["ridgepoint tower"] = { "Ridgepoint Tower watches the border of Elwynn Forest, one of the Stormwind Guard's scout towers. Its sentries turn their eyes toward Redridge, where gnolls and orcs gather, toward Duskwood, where the dead do not stay buried, and toward the nearby Defias camps. The garrison is well defended against all three.", "Ridgepoint_Tower" },
  ["stone cairn lake"] = { "Stone Cairn Lake lies in northeastern Elwynn Forest and takes its name from Heroes' Vigil, the stone cairn on the large island at its heart. A ring of stones stands there with a monument called the Stone of Remembrance, raised for the dead of the First War against the orcs.\n\nPilgrims once came here in safety. Now Rogue Wizards hold the island, and a tribe of murlocs has raised crude dwellings on the southern bank, indifferent to the monument across the water.", "Stone_Cairn_Lake" },
  ["stonefield farm"] = { "Stonefield Farm lies in southern Elwynn Forest, worked by the Stonefield family, who share a bitter rivalry with the nearby Maclures. Yet a child of each house has fallen in love, defying the old grudge between their families. Some hope that romance might succeed where years of quarreling never could, and mend what the feud has broken.", "Stonefield_Farm" },
  ["thunder falls"] = { "Thunder Falls is a narrow ravine in the mountains of northwestern Elwynn Forest, and an unusual one, with water falling at both of its ends. A third waterfall tumbles down from the northern cliffs and feeds it from above.\n\nThe path up from the waterfall at Mirror Lake climbs steep and narrow to a small rural cottage, its anvil and fire kept lit. A little pier and boat rest on the river beside the house. A few bandits linger near the cottage. The river runs on to another waterfall near the border of Westfall.", "Thunder_Falls" },
  ["tower of azora"] = { "The Tower of Azora rises amid the fields of central Elwynn Forest, a small mage tower kept by servants who all appear to be gnomes and who answer to the Archmage Theocritus. Far off in the Redridge Mountains, the evil sorcerer Morganth rules the Tower of Ilgalar. The two towers are in constant feud, and each spies on the other through magic.\n\nThe tower's own aura works on whatever strays too near and turns it into something stranger. The servants have to battle living buckets and mutant butterflies.", "Tower_of_Azora" },
  ["westbrook garrison"] = { "The Stormwind Guard keeps a barracks at Westbrook Garrison, on the western end of Elwynn Forest near the border of Westfall. Corruption and political instability have left the kingdom weak. With the bulk of the Stormwind Army abroad, the garrison barely has enough men to keep the roads safe against growing numbers of gnolls and thieves from Westfall.\n\nDeputy Rainer commands the post. Wanted posters there name Hogger, chieftain of the Riverpaw gnolls, who has overpowered every attempt to capture him. His pack infests the woods south of the guard tower.", "Westbrook_Garrison" },
}
areas[1430] = {
  ["aridens camp"] = { "A deserted campsite lies just north of the main road through the pass. It is known as Ariden's Camp, and nobody is there now.", "Ariden's_Camp" },
  ["deadmans crossing"] = { "A hangman's tree stands at the eastern crossroads of the pass, and a pair of corpses still hang from its branches. Beside it a signpost reads \"Turn Back!\" and \"Abandon hope, all ye who enter here.\"\n\nFrom the crossing one road runs west to Duskwood, another east to the Swamp of Sorrows, and a third south toward Karazhan.", "Deadman's_Crossing" },
  ["deadwind ravine"] = { "A craggy cleft runs south through the mountains toward Karazhan. The western road follows its upper level. At the far end a bridge crosses to the south, and the ominous tower stands close by.", "Deadwind_Ravine" },
  ["groshgok compound"] = { "Of all the Deadwind ogre caves in the Vice, the Grosh'gok Compound lies farthest south, and it is the largest. The Deadwind ogres live in it.", "Grosh'gok_Compound" },
  ["karazhan"] = { "The builders of the Ivory Tower of Karazhan are lost to time. It stands on a nexus of ley lines in southern Deadwind Pass, and it became the seat of Medivh, the last Guardian of Tirisfal. Befitting his title, Medivh had amassed a vast collection of writings and artifacts, greater even than that of the Kirin Tor. He was killed inside the tower in the First War by Khadgar, Anduin Lothar and Garona.\n\nSince then the tower has stood abandoned, and a curse lies on it and the land around it. Ghosts fill its halls, and demons are said to walk among them.", "Karazhan" },
  ["masters cellar"] = { "Below the ruins of a small village south of Karazhan, the ground opens into cellars full of ghosts. The villagers were probably servants of the tower. No one knows who killed them. Some blame Medivh, or something he summoned, and others the Dark Riders.\n\nTwo entrances lead down. One opens into a vast wine cellar, its racks and barrels long abandoned to the dead. The other leads to a cellar whose floor has collapsed into a cave system running deep underground. Unliving residents and caretakers, damned souls, restless shades and wailing spectres fill both.", "Master's_Cellar" },
  ["morgans plot"] = { "A small, decrepit church stands behind Karazhan, its graveyard and crypt sunk into the gloom of Deadwind Pass. Unliving residents haunt the chapel, and another patrols the hill just below.", "Morgan's_Plot" },
  ["sleeping gorge"] = { "A small chasm cuts through eastern Deadwind Pass. One of the paths south to Karazhan runs through it for a stretch before turning east into the Vice.", "Sleeping_Gorge" },
  ["vice"] = { "Along the road to Karazhan, in the south of the pass, lies a desolate stretch of crags called the Vice. The Deadwind ogres live here in mounds and camps scattered beside the road.\n\nNobody knows why they settled where only restless spirits and the vultures circling over them keep them company.", "Vice" },
}
areas[1431] = {
  ["addles stead"] = { "The Defias Brotherhood has taken Addle's Stead, one of the many farmsteads abandoned in southwestern Duskwood when the darkness crept in. Its agents move through the field, hide in the barn and stalk the rooms of the small farmhouse.", "Addle's_Stead" },
  ["beggars haunt"] = { "Beggar's Haunt, an abandoned scout tower of the Stormwind Guard, stands in northeastern Duskwood, east of Manor Mistmantle and north of the road into Deadwind Pass. The Forsaken hold it now, and Deathstalker Zraedus and Apothecary Faustin work experiments within its walls.\n\nA path winds down to a small pool fed by a waterfall, where an unmarked grave rests beneath two great statues carved like those at the Dark Portal. Near the tower lies another small cemetery, holding the grave of an Unknown Soldier.", "Beggar's_Haunt" },
  ["brightwood grove"] = { "The few buildings that stood at the edge of Brightwood Grove lie in ruins, and Nightbane worgen prowl its dark fields. They raise crude camps among the wreckage and attack anyone who comes too close. The grove lies just west of Darkshire. It keeps the old name of the whole forest, which was called Brightwood before the darkness.", "Brightwood_Grove" },
  ["darkened bank"] = { "The Darkened Bank runs along the northern shore of Duskwood, from the far western edge east to the bridge that joins the wood to the Redridge Mountains. At its western end, refugees have made a small camp. Sven Yorgen lives there with Lars, and Sven has lost his wife and children to the Dark Riders of Deadwind Pass.", "Darkened_Bank" },
  ["darkshire"] = { "Darkshire was once a quaint little woodland village called Grand Hamlet, in the forest of Brightwood. Then the darkness came, and the woods turned into a dangerous, thorny blight. Brightwood became Duskwood, and Grand Hamlet became Darkshire. The walking dead choke the graveyards now, and worgen have overrun many of the farmsteads around the town.\n\nStormwind pulled all its troops out of Duskwood after King Varian Wrynn disappeared. The townsfolk armed themselves instead. Their militia, the Night Watch, was formed and trained entirely by the people of the forest, and Commander Althea Ebonlocke leads it. Its watchers keep the main road and the town against wolves and walking corpses.\n\nThe people of Darkshire are jumpy and suspicious, and they need whatever help they can get.", "Darkshire" },
  ["dawning wood catacombs"] = { "Beneath Raven Hill Cemetery, twisting catacombs hold the most powerful undead in the region. There are two entrances. The eastern one leads into a den of ghouls, diseased and ferocious. The western one leads into a hall of skeletons, who guard the tombs of their former brethren.", "Dawning_Wood_Catacombs" },
  ["forlorn rowe"] = { "Forlorn Rowe is a hill on the northern edge of Raven Hill Cemetery, wrapped in a darkness heavier than the shadow of the forest around it. The necromancer Morbent Fel has his home in the dark house on its top, looking down over the graves. He rules the undead of Duskwood through evil arcane magic.", "Forlorn_Rowe" },
  ["hushed bank"] = { "The Hushed Bank follows the western river of Duskwood south from the point where Westfall, Elwynn Forest and Duskwood meet, down toward Stranglethorn Vale. A covered bridge crosses the river here and joins Duskwood to Westfall. Spiders and wolves range among the trees, and Sven Yorgen and Lars keep a camp at its northern end.", "Hushed_Bank" },
  ["manor mistmantle"] = { "Manor Mistmantle stands in northeastern Duskwood, north of Darkshire and west of Beggar's Haunt. Its grounds have rotted, and ghouls shamble through them. The garden has gone to ruin. A single flower still grows in it, the Tear of Tilloa, the only sign of life on the property.", "Manor_Mistmantle" },
  ["raven hill"] = { "The houses of Raven Hill stand empty in western Duskwood, given up to rot and dark. Almost nobody is left. Jitters, a frightened man, hides here from his dark past, and a goblin named Bliztik keeps out of sight on the top floor of one of the buildings. The Unseen haunt the rest of the town, moving through its empty rooms.", "Raven_Hill" },
  ["raven hill cemetery"] = { "The cemetery grew far past its old bounds only after the darkness came to Duskwood. Undead walk among its stones, with massive spiders and gangs of grave robbers who attack anyone who interrupts their work. The undead Mor'Ladim wanders a strange, meandering path through the grounds, and death comes to the unwary who meet him.\n\nOn the hill in the cemetery's northwest corner stands Forlorn Rowe, home of the necromancer Morbent Fel, and in its northeast quarter lies the way down into the Dawning Wood Catacombs.", "Raven_Hill_Cemetery" },
  ["rolands doom"] = { "Roland's Doom is a mine in southeastern Duskwood, due east of the Rotting Orchard. Worgen of the Nightbane pack fill its shafts, and it is home to Gutspill and Nefaru, who lead the worgen at the Rotting Orchard.", "Roland's_Doom" },
  ["rotting orchard"] = { "Worgen of the Nightbane pack have taken the Rotting Orchard in southern Duskwood. They make camps among its dilapidated buildings. Nightbane Shadow Weavers practice their magic there, and Nightbane Stalkers keep watch in the shadows.", "Rotting_Orchard" },
  ["tranquil gardens cemetery"] = { "Nothing about Tranquil Gardens Cemetery is tranquil anymore. It lies southeast of Darkshire, and its dead do not rest, though they are weaker than the undead elsewhere in Duskwood. They wander among the headstones and the ruined church. The Insane Ghoul haunts the ground, sometimes inside the church and sometimes outside. Blind Mary's house stands just north.", "Tranquil_Gardens_Cemetery" },
  ["twilight grove"] = { "An ancient volcano crater, long dead, lies at the center of Duskwood, and it teems with life. The twilight here is unlike the dark of the rest of the woods. A great tree with no name stands in its heart beside a moonwell and an old night elf gazebo. Beneath its boughs is one of only four portals into the Emerald Dream, and the influence of the sleeping druids shows on the land around it.\n\nFour great dragons guard the portal. They were once proud lieutenants of Ysera, Aspect of Dreams, and protectors of the Dream. The Emerald Nightmare has corrupted them, and now they seek only destruction and death.", "Twilight_Grove" },
  ["vulgol ogre mound"] = { "Splinter Fist ogres have overrun a small basin in southern Duskwood, just southwest of the fork in the main road. The mound takes its name from their leader, Zzarc'Vul. Humans lived here once, as the ruined farmhouses show. In the cave the ogres feed on the carcasses of black bears.", "Vul'Gol_Ogre_Mound" },
  ["yorgen farmstead"] = { "Sven Yorgen once farmed this land in southern Duskwood, until Dark Riders from Deadwind Pass came and killed his family. The farm is abandoned now, its fields gone to rot, and Sven keeps to a small camp on the Darkened Bank.\n\nThe Defias Brotherhood holds the farmstead. Its assassins move through the fields unseen.", "Yorgen_Farmstead" },
}
areas[1432] = {
  ["algaz station"] = { "Many dwarven mountaineers are stationed at this outpost, just east of North Gate Pass. It lies south of Dun Algaz, the stronghold in the pass, and takes its name from it.", "Algaz_Station" },
  ["dun algaz"] = { "The tunnel through Dun Algaz is older than the War of the Three Hammers. When that war drove the Wildhammer dwarves out of Ironforge, they passed through here.\n\nThe Horde retreated this way too. Beaten back from Dun Modr in the Second War, the orcs fell south across the Thandol Valley and dug in at the outpost here. Anduin Lothar, wanting them pushed clear out of Khaz Modan, sent his forces to tear the stronghold down. The assault failed. The orcs held on, and Dun Algaz became the last ground the Horde kept in the region.\n\nThe dwarven mountaineers who watch this pass kept mostly to the hills above it, but the Dragonmaw clan has overrun their post and driven them back to the upper tunnel. With the lower pass lost, the road between the dwarven homelands and Menethil Harbor has grown far more dangerous.", "Dun_Algaz" },
  ["farstrider lodge"] = { "The Farstrider Lodge stands in the southeastern hills of Loch Modan, a hunters' lodge whose stonework is plainly high elven. Only one high elf is known to live there now. Humans and dwarves, hunters among them, have since taken up residence within its elven walls.", "Farstrider_Lodge" },
  ["grizzlepaw ridge"] = { "Grizzlepaw Ridge rises south of Thelsamar, thick with bears that have made the heights their own. The most ferocious of them, a large black bear called Ol' Sooty, keeps a lair on the slopes. Now and then he wanders down to the base of the valley and pounces on anyone unlucky enough to irritate him.", "Grizzlepaw_Ridge" },
  ["ironbands excavation site"] = { "Prospector Ironband leads an excavation of ancient ruins here, in the southeastern corner of Loch Modan. Like the other dwarven digs, it has been invaded, and the prospectors and miners have been driven out. The invaders are troggs of an especially large and volatile breed.", "Ironband's_Excavation_Site" },
  ["loch"] = { "No other lake on Khaz Modan comes close to the Loch. Only Lordamere Lake and Darrowmere Lake are larger anywhere in the known world.\n\nThe Stonewrought Dam on its northern edge holds the water in, and the lake is more than twice the size it was before the dam was built. Threshadons and frenzies swim in it, and the broken catapults and ballistae of the Second War still lie on its bottom and on its islands.", "The_Loch" },
  ["mogrosh stronghold"] = { "A tribe of ogres, the Mo'grosh, holds the caves of the northeastern hills. Chok'sul, thought to be their leader, has coordinated attacks on Thelsamar, the Stonewrought Dam and the dwarven digs, and the magistrate of Thelsamar has put a price on his head. One band ambushed a dwarven caravan coming from the Wetlands and left no one alive but a mortally wounded guard.", "Mo'grosh_Stronghold" },
  ["north gate pass"] = { "The pass runs between the high mountains of Dun Morogh to the west and the lower mountains of Loch Modan to the east. In its middle stands North Gate Outpost, the northeasternmost settlement the dwarves have built in Dun Morogh.\n\nThe mountaineers here are kept busy driving the wildlife back from the walls. Some dwarves have gone missing in the mountains while searching for the fiercer beasts.", "North_Gate_Pass" },
  ["silver stream mine"] = { "Silver once came out of this mine in plenty, and it went to the dwarves of Ironforge. The vein ran dry long ago, and the Miners' League turned the tunnels into a storage depot.\n\nNow Tunnel Rat kobolds have overrun it, hungry for what the mine still holds. Behind the mine lie the wrecked wagons and catapults of the orcs.", "Silver_Stream_Mine" },
  ["south gate pass"] = { "Halfway through the pass stands a dwarven outpost, and its mountaineers have cleared out most of the wildlife that once pressed in on it. Snow lies on the ground here, as it does in the mountains to the west.\n\nThe pass is the closest way down to the dwarven town of Thelsamar.", "South_Gate_Pass" },
  ["stonesplinter valley"] = { "Stonesplinter Valley was a pristine place once, carved among the mountains of southwestern Loch Modan. Now it crawls with hostile Stonesplinter troggs.\n\nThe dwarves hold that the tribe's push into their lands is led by a trogg named Grawmug, who lives in the innermost cave. Two elite guards, Gnasher and Brawler, never leave his side. Captain Rugelfuss wants him dead.", "Stonesplinter_Valley" },
  ["stonewrought dam"] = { "Few who cross the Stonewrought Dam know that a Dark Iron dwarf built it. Franclorn Forgewright, the great architect of that clan, oversaw the work and invented the stonewrought method that the dam is named for.\n\nThe dam rises east of Dun Algaz along the northern edge of Loch Modan, and the water it holds back more than doubled the size of the loch. The Dark Iron clan has lately attacked it, and the dwarves of Ironforge have tightened their guard. Chief Engineer Hinderweir VII now oversees it.", "Stonewrought_Dam" },
  ["stoutlager inn"] = { "The Stoutlager Inn stands in Thelsamar. Dwarves and travelers gather here to drink and talk. Innkeeper Hearthstove keeps it, and Lina Hearthstove tends the stable.", "Stoutlager_Inn" },
  ["thelsamar"] = { "A dwarven town stands on the west shore of Loch Modan, north of Grizzlepaw Ridge and northeast of Stonesplinter Valley. Mountaineers keep watch over its houses, and Magistrate Bluntnose presides over the town. It has an inn, the Stoutlager, and a forge and anvil near its southwesternmost house. The Explorers' League keeps a house here too, and fishermen work the shore of the loch below the town.\n\nA north-south road runs just west of the town, leading to the passes into Dun Morogh and the Wetlands.", "Thelsamar" },
  ["valley of kings"] = { "The Valley of Kings lies in the southwestern reach of Loch Modan, where the green land narrows toward the gate leading down into the blackened Searing Gorge. The gate stays locked and guarded, and Mountaineer Pebblebitty holds it.\n\nTwo great statues rise over the pass, King Madoran Bronzebeard and King Khardros Wildhammer. When the two kings died, their sons commissioned the statues together in honor of their fathers. The land to the south had turned volcanic in the wake of Ragnaros, and the statues stand guard over the road into it. They are a warning to anyone who would attack the dwarven kingdoms, and a reminder of the price the Dark Irons paid for their crimes.", "Valley_of_Kings" },
}
areas[1433] = {
  ["althers mill"] = { "The lumber mill northeast of Lakeshire is abandoned. It was always a backwater, a place folk passed through on the way to Stonewatch Keep, and spiders, buzzards and the odd stray orc have overrun it.", "Alther's_Mill" },
  ["galardell valley"] = { "The Tower of Ilgalar stands in Galardell Valley, in the northeastern Redridge Mountains. Morganth, a dark mage cast out of Stormwind, lives there.\n\nThe valley below crawls with the Shadowhide gnolls that serve him. The strongest is their leader, Lieutenant Fangore, who calls the nearby gnolls to his side at the first sign of trespass, and Magistrate Solomon has put a price on his head.", "Galardell_Valley" },
  ["lake everstill"] = { "Wide, clear and azure, Lake Everstill fills the middle of the Redridge Mountains and gives the sleepy town of Lakeshire its setting. Its eastern river runs out past the dark walls of Stonewatch Keep.\n\nThe lake was once known for its fishing. Murloc tribes have overrun it since, and a great thresher called the Lake Thresher patrols the deeps. The Everstill Bridge carries the road across the water, but the Blackrock clan damaged it, and Foreman Oslow and his workers are still at the repairs.", "Lake_Everstill" },
  ["lakeridge highway"] = { "Traffic once moved steadily along the Lakeridge Highway, on the southern edge of Lake Everstill, in the days before the First War. Since the orcs took Stonewatch Keep the old road has fallen into disuse. Gnolls patrol most of its length, and few travelers risk it.", "Lakeridge_Highway" },
  ["lakeshire"] = { "Lakeshire sits on the northern bank of Lake Everstill, and the town has bled for that ground more than once. In the First War, Warchief Blackhand led a raiding party into these mountains to take it for the Horde. Anduin Lothar and the Knights of Stormwind ambushed the orcs from the slopes and nearly beat them. Two Shadow Council warlocks turned the fight and drove Lothar back. Blackhand returned weeks later with a larger force and took the town. After the Second War, the Alliance of Lordaeron retook and rebuilt it, and Supreme Commander Turalyon oversaw the return of its people.\n\nMagistrate Solomon now leads Lakeshire through its own court, and Marshal Marris commands the guard. Gnolls and Blackrock orcs press on the town, and little help comes from Stormwind. One of the Blackrock assaults damaged the Everstill Bridge, and Foreman Oslow and his crew are still repairing it, slowed by the supplies they lose to the raids.", "Lakeshire" },
  ["redridge canyons"] = { "The canyons run through the northwestern Redridge Mountains. Yowler, who leads the local gnolls, has his den here.", "Redridge_Canyons" },
  ["renders camp"] = { "Blackrock orcs hold an outpost in the northwestern Redridge Mountains, near the entrance to the Burning Steppes.", "Render's_Camp" },
  ["renders rock"] = { "Powerful Blackrock orcs hold a large cave here, in the northwestern Redridge Mountains, close to the way into the Burning Steppes.", "Render's_Rock" },
  ["renders valley"] = { "The orcs of this valley in the southeastern Redridge Mountains are not as strong as their kin farther northwest. The camp is theirs all the same, an outpost of the Blackrock clan.", "Render's_Valley" },
  ["rethban caverns"] = { "Gnolls hold the deep tunnels of the Rethban Caverns, in the northwestern Redridge Mountains. Their leader, Ardo Dirtpaw, is a cunning one. The gnolls carry Rethban ore, which takes its name from Bart Rethban, the first man to find it. The caverns were probably named for him too.", "Rethban_Caverns" },
  ["stonewatch"] = { "The humans of Lakeshire held Stonewatch as a fortress against the orc raids that come down from the Burning Steppes. It has an easily defended keep, an armored tower beside it, and falls that pour down the cliff face below.\n\nThe warlock Morganth struck a bargain with the Blackrock warlord Gath'Ilzogg. With Morganth's dark magic behind them, the orcs fell on the keep with horrific savagery, beat the 12th Sabre Regiment and took the fortress.\n\nThe alliance did not last. Morganth turned on the orcs and raised a tower of his own, where he built an army of Shadowhide gnolls. Left to fend for themselves, the Blackrock orcs hold Stonewatch still.", "Stonewatch" },
  ["stonewatch falls"] = { "Lake Everstill's eastern river ends here, spilling down the hillside below Stonewatch into a deep lake in the nook of the eastern mountains. A small tribe of murlocs lives along its banks. They catch fish from the water and build crude shelters to sleep in.", "Stonewatch_Falls" },
  ["stonewatch keep"] = { "Gath'Ilzogg, a warlord of the Blackrock clan, holds Stonewatch Keep with his trusted lieutenant Tharil'zun. The orcs took the keep from the humans of Lakeshire, and Gath'Ilzogg leads raids against the town from it.\n\nIn the fighting the orcs took Corporal Keeshan of the Stormwind Army prisoner and dragged him off to their camps farther north. Magistrate Solomon has put a bounty on Gath'Ilzogg, and Marshal Marris of Lakeshire wants Tharil'zun to answer for his misdeeds.", "Stonewatch_Keep" },
  ["stonewatch tower"] = { "Beside the keep stands the armored scout tower of Stonewatch. It once belonged to the humans of Lakeshire, like the keep. Now it is held by underlings of Gath'Ilzogg, Blackrock orcs who watch from it over the stronghold their clan took.", "Stonewatch_Tower" },
  ["three corners"] = { "At Three Corners the road splits three ways, toward the Three Shires. One road goes to Goldshire in Elwynn Forest, one to Lakeshire, and one to Darkshire in Duskwood. Only the crossroads marks the seam between the three lands. A lone Stormwind guard, Guard Parker, watches it, and gnolls roam the roads nearby.", "Three_Corners" },
  ["tower of ilgalar"] = { "Morganth once studied in the mage tower of Stormwind with two other mages, until he fell to the dark arts. He was urged to turn away from that magic and could not, and he was banished from the Mage Quarter.\n\nNow he lives in the Tower of Ilgalar, in the northeastern Redridge Mountains, and goes on with his dark studies. Dragon bones hang on display through the tower, and a dragon skull and a human skull sit on the alchemy table at the top. He commands the Shadowhide gnolls, who have brought down orc and man alike.", "Tower_of_Ilgalar" },
}
areas[1434] = {
  ["baliamah ruins"] = { "Ancient troll stonework stands on the east bank of the river here, and the Skullsplitter trolls have made their home in it. The ogre mound of Mosh'Ogg lies to the northeast.", "Balia'mah_Ruins" },
  ["ballal ruins"] = { "All that is left of Bal'lal is a line of broken columns on the northwestern coast, facing the Vile Reef. The trolls who raised it are long gone, and Bloodscalp trolls now live among the fallen stone.", "Bal'lal_Ruins" },
  ["bloodsail compound"] = { "The Bloodsail Buccaneers, a renegade band of sailors, keep a loose camp on the western coast of Stranglethorn Vale, toward the middle of the shore. Swashbucklers and warlocks gather among the pirates.", "Bloodsail_Compound" },
  ["booty bay"] = { "Visitors on foot enter Booty Bay through the bleached jaws of a giant shark. Beyond them a port city climbs the cliffs around a blue lagoon at the southern tip of Stranglethorn Vale. Life is cheap here and no one is to be trusted. It is a rogue's paradise, full of informants.\n\nThe place was Blackwater Cove once, a small human port, until jungle trolls overran it. Goblins of the Steamwheedle Cartel drove the trolls back from the shore and made the cove their center of operations in the Eastern Kingdoms. That was before the First War.\n\nThe Blackwater Raiders run the port now, in close company with the Cartel, and it serves any traveler whatever banner they follow. Baron Revilgaz rules from the top of the inn, alongside Fleet Master Seahorn of the Raiders, and hires every sword he can against the Bloodsail Buccaneers, who threaten the bay.", "Booty_Bay" },
  ["cape of stranglethorn"] = { "The southern reaches of Stranglethorn Vale lie thick with troll ruins, half-swallowed by jungle, and long stretches of sandy shore where the trees give way to sea. This is the ground of a long quarrel between the goblin pirates of Booty Bay and their rivals, the Bloodsail Buccaneers.", "Cape_of_Stranglethorn" },
  ["crystal shore"] = { "The South Seas break on a long sandy shore here, across from the eastern beaches of Jaguero Isle. A steep ridge walls the beach off from the land behind it, and only two paths cut through, one of them climbing toward Mistvale Valley. At the north end of the shore stand the Ruins of Aboraz.", "Crystal_Shore" },
  ["crystalvein mine"] = { "The rock of Crystalvein Mine, near Zul'Mamwe, holds strange crystals that Crank Fizzlebub called Singing Crystals. They are found nowhere but Stranglethorn and are worth a good deal. The basilisks that nest here eat them, and that is what hardens their hides. The Venture Company came hoping to harvest the mine, but the basilisks have stopped them so far. Outside the entrance, trolls and humans stand crystallized where they were caught.", "Crystalvein_Mine" },
  ["gromgol base camp"] = { "Commander Aggro'gosh holds Grom'gol Base Camp, an orc fort on the western coast. It is heavily fortified against jungle trolls, naga from the surf, and the Alliance men who come in along the shore. The camp keeps a safe chain of supply open to Stonard in the Swamp of Sorrows.", "Grom'gol_Base_Camp" },
  ["gurubashi arena"] = { "In ancient times the fights in the Gurubashi Arena were to the death, and the sand turned to mud with troll blood. The Gurubashi Empire raised it when it ruled vast tracts of the south of the Eastern Kingdoms. It stands in the northwest of the Cape of Stranglethorn, just north of Booty Bay, and it has outlasted the empire.\n\nShort John Mithril now oversees matches that are less deadly, with fame and treasure as the prize. Old stories say that anyone who finds a blood-red amulet at midnight will have their soul devoured by a demon before daybreak.", "Gurubashi_Arena" },
  ["jaguero isle"] = { "Jaguero Isle lies off the coast of Stranglethorn Vale, a jungle island of panthers and gorillas. King Mukla, an enormous ape, rules there and keeps a tauren, Princess Poobah, captive.", "Jaguero_Isle" },
  ["janeiros point"] = { "A giant statue of a goblin stands on this small island outside Booty Bay, its arms flung wide. No one knows whose likeness it is. It was built as a beacon to welcome ships to the port. The sea giant Mok'rash has made the island his home.\n\nHe bellows across the water at Captain Smotts, boasting that he has the captain's belt tied around his finger, asking whether Smotts has run out of ships to send, and grumbling that the statue is poor company.", "Janeiro's_Point" },
  ["kalai ruins"] = { "The Kal'ai Ruins lie half-swallowed by the sea in Stranglethorn Vale, ancient troll stonework worn by tide and age. The Murkgill tribe of murlocs now nests among the fallen columns, having claimed the drowned halls for its own.", "Kal'ai_Ruins" },
  ["kurzens compound"] = { "Colonel Conrad Kurzen's expedition raised this unusual human settlement, east of the Rebel Camp, and it has a lumber mill. Madness has taken Kurzen. The men who would not follow him broke away and founded the Rebel Camp, and the two sides are locked in a deadly fight, men once of one command killing each other among the trees.\n\nNear the stables, panthers and Stranglethorn tigers sit in cages while the men train them into war beasts.", "Kurzen's_Compound" },
  ["lake nazferiti"] = { "A large base of the goblin Venture Company stands on the shore of Lake Nazferiti. Frenzies and crocolisks fill the water.\n\nThe Nazferiti river runs through the lake, and it may be the longest river in the southern Eastern Kingdoms. Its source is a waterfall in the Redridge Mountains, though some place its headwaters farther off, in Deadwind Pass.", "Lake_Nazferiti" },
  ["mistvale valley"] = { "Mistvale Valley lies in the southern reaches of Stranglethorn Vale, and the great Mistvale gorillas make their home among its trees.", "Mistvale_Valley" },
  ["mizjah ruins"] = { "The Mizjah Ruins lie southeast of Grom'gol Base Camp, held by Mosh'Ogg Brutes and their witch doctors. From these broken stones the ogres ambush the supply caravans bound for Stonard.", "Mizjah_Ruins" },
  ["moshogg ogre mound"] = { "The Mosh'Ogg Ogre Mound lies south of Zul'Gurub, its dark hollows home to the Mosh'Ogg tribe of ogres. Deep within, their leader, the ogre mage Mai'Zoth, rules the mound.", "Mosh'Ogg_Ogre_Mound" },
  ["nekmani wellspring"] = { "On the western edge of Stranglethorn, high in the cliffs above the Southern Savage Coast, a holy spring rises. Its water is the only clean fresh water south of Duskwood.\n\nScholars quarrel over it, because every race claims the waters were made divine for its own people, and none has settled the matter. An expedition of naga explorers has lately advanced on the spring, and they guard it fiercely.", "Nek'mani_Wellspring" },
  ["nesingwarys expedition"] = { "The dwarf Hemet Nesingwary, a master hunter, led an expedition into Stranglethorn to hunt the wild game that crosses the jungle. His camp sits just below the river, north of Lake Nazferiti.\n\nHired guns keep watch over it. His servant, Barnil Stonepot, calls him a war hero of the Alliance.", "Nesingwary's_Expedition" },
  ["rebel camp"] = { "Soldiers of Stormwind who broke with their own colonel built this camp. It stands in the northernmost part of Stranglethorn Vale, west of the road that runs south from Duskwood.\n\nColonel Conrad Kurzen led an expedition out of Stormwind, and madness fell on it. Most of his men still follow him. Lieutenant Doren and a small band escaped from Kurzen's Compound, fled west and set up here. Their loyalty is still to Stormwind, and Doren wants Kurzen's head to end the madness.", "Rebel_Camp" },
  ["ruins of aboraz"] = { "Zanzil the Outcast keeps these ancient troll ruins on the southeastern coast of Stranglethorn Vale. The Skullsplitter tribe exiled him for poisoning his own people.\n\nHere he brews a mixture that leaves whoever drinks it strong and feeble-minded. It has bent ghouls, naga and troll minions to his will, and they wander the ruins.", "Ruins_of_Aboraz" },
  ["ruins of jubuwal"] = { "Old troll ruins stand just east of the Gurubashi Arena, beside the main road that runs the length of Stranglethorn Vale. Ghouls, naga and trolls in the service of Zanzil the Outcast have taken them over.\n\nTwo men are among them, Jon-Jon the Crow and Maury \"Club Foot\" Wilkins, who owe money to the ruthless \"Sea Wolf\" MacKinley.", "Ruins_of_Jubuwal" },
  ["ruins of zulkunda"] = { "The Bloodscalp tribe holds the vast jungle troll ruins of Zul'Kunda, in the northwest of Stranglethorn Vale. Nezzliok the Dire and Gan'zulah command here.\n\nThe Bloodscalps broke from the Gurubashi Empire in its civil war. They fight off every intruder, the Skullsplitters included, to keep the land they took. They have only contempt for the Darkspear tribe, the first jungle trolls to join the Horde.", "Ruins_of_Zul'Kunda" },
  ["ruins of zulmamwe"] = { "Mountains close in the ruins of Zul'Mamwe on every side except the north. The Skullsplitter tribe lives here, the second great troll tribe of Stranglethorn Vale, under its vicious chieftain, Ana'thek the Cruel.\n\nLike the Bloodscalps, the Skullsplitters broke from the Gurubashi Empire in its civil war. They hold the Darkspear tribe in contempt and fight off every intruder, the nearby Bloodscalps most of all. Their witch doctor, Mogh the Undying, has bent several men of Colonel Conrad Kurzen's expedition to his will and keeps them at his side.", "Ruins_of_Zul'Mamwe" },
  ["savage coast"] = { "Crocolisks fill the shallows of the Savage Coast, and basilisks live along its shore. Several troll settlements stand beside it.\n\nIt takes up the northern shore of Stranglethorn Vale, from the Westfall border south to Grom'gol Base Camp, on the shore east of the Vile Reef.", "Savage_Coast" },
  ["south seas"] = { "Between the continents, the South Seas wash the southern shores of the Eastern Kingdoms and Kalimdor. They are a part of the Great Sea, and Zandalar lies out among their islands. Nearer Stranglethorn Vale, the sea grows treacherous with reefs and small islands, some no more than a sandbar.\n\nIn the Second War the Stormreaver clan caught giant turtles in these waters and used them as submarines for the Horde.", "South_Seas" },
  ["southern savage coast"] = { "Beaches and shallow water line the west side of the Cape of Stranglethorn, where the South Seas meet the shore. The coast runs south past Booty Bay and Janeiro's Point to the Wild Shore.\n\nThe Vile Reef divides it from the Savage Coast to the north. There the Great Sea gives way to the South Seas.", "Southern_Savage_Coast" },
  ["spirit den"] = { "A jungle troll witch doctor named Unbagwa keeps to a hidden cave at the northern end of Mistvale Valley. The valley around the Spirit Den is the range of the great Mistvale gorillas.", "Spirit_Den" },
  ["stockpile"] = { "A cave at the back of Kurzen's Compound serves as the arsenal of Colonel Conrad Kurzen's jungle fighters, who keep their arms and equipment here. Whatever has gone wrong in the colonel's mind, most of his men still follow him.", "Stockpile" },
  ["tkashi ruins"] = { "Broken troll walls stand in the jungle southwest of Nesingwary's Expedition. A large band of Bloodscalp trolls lives among them, warriors, axe throwers and shamans. The hunter Hemet Nesingwary wrote that he and his followers passed through the ruins without incident.", "Tkashi_Ruins" },
  ["venture co base camp"] = { "Goblins of the Venture Company have cleared a camp beside Lake Nazferiti. Lumberjacks and shredders work the jungle timber while geologists dig the hills for crystals found only in Stranglethorn, which certain buyers value highly.", "Venture_Co._Base_Camp" },
  ["venture co operations center"] = { "An oil platform stands on a lake in the northern jungle, built by goblins of the Venture Company. Mechanics and geologists crowd the rig, and the crews press from here deeper into the jungle. Their hold on these remote works is thin. The jungle trolls are bent on purging their old lands of every foreign invader.", "Venture_Co._Operations_Center" },
  ["vile reef"] = { "Off the western coast of Stranglethorn Vale, near Grom'gol Base Camp, the sea breaks bright over coral. Beneath it lie sunken troll ruins, and among that drowned stone the murlocs make their home, moving through halls that once belonged to someone else. The Altar of the Tides still stands there. Giant clams litter the reef floor, and rumor says they hold blue pearls. Wreckage from ships washes in along the shore.", "Vile_Reef" },
  ["wild shore"] = { "The southernmost shore of the Eastern Kingdoms is where the land gives way to the South Seas. The Bloodsail Buccaneers have anchored their fleet off the beach, and a sea giant wanders the sand.\n\nThe land ends in a rocky point at its western edge, with Booty Bay on the north side of it.", "Wild_Shore" },
  ["yojamba isle"] = { "Small islands lie off the northwestern coast of Stranglethorn, near the border with Westfall, and the Zandalar tribe has made camp on them. Hakkar the Soulflayer, an ancient blood god, holds Zul'Gurub and has made it a place of blood and madness. The tribe cannot bring him down alone, and the troll tribes are splintered. So they send word to the Alliance and the Horde, and ask champions of every race to set their war aside long enough to help.", "Yojamba_Isle" },
  ["ziatajai ruins"] = { "Troll ruins lie in the jungle here, and the Skullsplitter tribe holds them against every interloper that comes near.", "Ziata'jai_Ruins" },
  ["zulgurub"] = { "Zul'Gurub rises out of the jungle in the northeast of Stranglethorn Vale, a temple city of vine-covered ziggurats and overgrown idols. It was the capital of the Gurubashi Empire, and every jungle troll tribe answered to it. Now only the Gurubashi tribe holds it.\n\nLong before the Dark Portal opened, civil war tore the empire apart. A circle of priests called the Atal'ai summoned the avatar of Hakkar the Soulflayer, an ancient blood god, and were driven into exile. The exiles had learned that Hakkar could take physical form only in the old capital.\n\nNow Hakkar dwells in its deepest part. Jin'do the Hexxer has enslaved several Gurubashi high priests to serve him. Champions of the Primal Gods went in to quell the blood god and fell under his sway, and their power feeds him.", "Zul'Gurub" },
  ["zuuldaia ruins"] = { "Bloodscalp trolls in numbers live among the ancient ruins on the northern stretch of Stranglethorn's western coast. They drive off every interloper.", "Zuuldaia_Ruins" },
}
areas[1435] = {
  ["fallow sanctuary"] = { "A tribe known simply as the Lost Ones lives in this village. They are draenei who came through the Dark Portal, and homesickness has driven them mad and feral. They attack on sight, and they send assassins and saboteurs against the orcs.", "Fallow_Sanctuary" },
  ["harborage"] = { "Magtoor leads a handful of draenei here, in the northern reaches of the swamp. Unlike most of their kind they kept their minds, and they remember their noble roots and live apart from their mad kin. Magtoor has had the grim task of killing those who were once his own brethren.\n\nThe village deals with both the Horde and the Alliance. The Lost Ones of the Fallow Sanctuary are at open war with it, and Noboru and his gang wander the swamp killing any who cross their path.", "Harborage" },
  ["ithariuss cave"] = { "A mysterious high elf named Itharius lives in this cave, in the far southwestern corner of the swamp. Unruly green whelps scrap and stumble on the ground outside, and Itharius has taken it upon himself to watch over them while they grow.", "Itharius's_Cave" },
  ["misty reed post"] = { "Tok'Kar holds this small outpost for Stonard, with Katar, a few hunters and a shaman from the town. It sits between the Stagalbog and the Misty Reed Strand. It is a camp with no burrow and no tower, and Tok'Kar dreams of a burrow here, or at least a tower.", "Misty_Reed_Post" },
  ["misty reed strand"] = { "Driftwood and debris line this gloomy stretch of coast, with little else along its vast beach. Crocolisks and crabs share the shore with murlocs.", "Misty_Reed_Strand" },
  ["misty valley"] = { "Mist hangs low in this valley in the northwestern corner of the swamp, north of the way into Deadwind Pass, and swampwalkers and tangled horrors crowd it. The Mire Lord, an elemental, keeps to a cave deep at the back.", "Misty_Valley" },
  ["pool of tears"] = { "The Pool of Tears lies broad and still on the eastern edge of the Swamp of Sorrows. Beneath its calm surface sits the drowned troll temple of Atal'Hakkar, and members of the green dragonflight keep watch over the ground nearby and over the ruin below.\n\nBloodthirsty frenzies swim in the water. Broken Atal'ai stonework lies along the shore and under the surface.", "Pool_of_Tears" },
  ["shifting mire"] = { "The fine silt of the Shifting Mire never lies still. A mysterious force gathers it into drifts and drags it across the ground, curling and pooling as though it were alive. The Mire makes up the central bulk of the swamp, west of the Pool of Tears. Crocolisks and swamp jaguars hunt in it, and Lost Ones wander through.", "Shifting_Mire" },
  ["sorrowmurk"] = { "Green dragonspawn crawl through the mire here, just east of the Pool of Tears and the Temple of Atal'Hakkar. A large green drake patrols the region. Only the boldest travelers go in.", "Sorrowmurk" },
  ["splinterspear junction"] = { "The road into the swamp forks here. One branch runs north and the other runs south toward Stonard. An old orcish watch tower stands at the fork, and Horde guards patrol the crossing.", "Splinterspear_Junction" },
  ["stagalbog"] = { "Dense marshland lies south of the Pool of Tears, where the swamp runs up against the mountains that close it in on the south. A cave opens into the rock at the mountains' edge.", "Stagalbog" },
  ["stagalbog cave"] = { "Many murlocs of the Marsh tribe live in this large cave in the eastern marsh, under their leader, the Swamp Talker.", "Stagalbog_Cave" },
  ["stonard"] = { "Orcs of the Old Horde raised Stonard in the Black Morass soon after the Dark Portal first opened. It is one of the oldest orcish settlements on Azeroth, and the First War left it badly damaged. After Thrall took command of the Horde, the orcs rebuilt it.\n\nIt is the Horde's only town in the Swamp of Sorrows. A great hall still stands in its midst, a rare thing among orcish communities, and two burrows guard the approach. Patrols range out from it, and small encampments nearby strengthen the Horde's hold on the region. The swamp is still largely unmapped to the orcs, and they send wayfinders and cartographers from here to chart it. Researchers have come to study its ancient magical artifacts and its plants and beasts.", "Stonard" },
  ["temple of atalhakkar"] = { "More than a thousand years ago, the Gurubashi Empire was torn apart by civil war. At its heart were the Atal'ai, troll priests who sought to bring the blood god Hakkar the Soulflayer back into the world. They were defeated and driven out, and they fled north into the Swamp of Sorrows. There they raised a great temple to prepare for his coming.\n\nYsera the Dreamer, Aspect of the green dragonflight, learned of their plan and smashed the temple down beneath the marsh. Green dragons guard the drowned ruins, and they let no one in or out. It is believed that some of the fanatical Atal'ai lived through her wrath and went back to the dark service of Hakkar. Their master, Jammal'an the Prophet, leads them in the temple.", "Temple_of_Atal'Hakkar" },
}
areas[1436] = {
  ["alexston farmstead"] = { "The fields at Alexston Farmstead once fed families, before the Defias Brotherhood took the land. It is one of many farms in northern and central Westfall that the Brotherhood now holds, and mechanical harvest golems roam its furrows, guarding rows that no one plants anymore.\n\nBaros Alexston, an architect in the service of Stormwind, worked these fields as a boy, before the land that shaped him passed into other hands.", "Alexston_Farmstead" },
  ["dagger hills"] = { "Behind Moonbrook, a long ridge of hills runs through the south of Westfall. Little lives there. Defias knuckledusters, pathstalkers and highwaymen watch the ground. A jovial dwarf brewer named Grimbooze Thunderbrew keeps to the hills and speaks in rhymes, and the ghoul Leprithus is known to rise out of the earth nearby.", "Dagger_Hills" },
  ["dead acre"] = { "A farm once stood here in the southeast of Westfall, close to the bridge into Duskwood. The Defias Brotherhood burned it and the fields around it. No farmer walks the ground now. Smoking ruins remain, and harvest golems roam the smoldering acres.", "Dead_Acre" },
  ["deadmines"] = { "Before the First War, the tunnels beneath Moonbrook were the greatest gold mine in human lands. The First War emptied them of miners, and afterward they were left alone and thought haunted. Later a crew from the Miners' League worked part of the tunnels under Foreman Thistlenettle. The Defias Brotherhood attacked them at their work, a tunnel fell in, and the miners who died there walk the dark as restless undead.\n\nThe Brotherhood has made the mines its headquarters. Edwin VanCleef is building a juggernaut at the bottom of them with the help of goblins, a ship meant to destroy Stormwind from the sea.", "Deadmines" },
  ["demonts place"] = { "Little remains of the old farmstead at Demont's Place, west of Moonbrook. Defias agents occupy it now and hold it as their own.", "Demont's_Place" },
  ["dust plains"] = { "The southern end of Westfall is dry, open ground. Gnolls hold a few camps there and threaten anyone who crosses it. On the eastern edge stands an old mage tower, now a Defias stronghold called Klaven's Tower after its master, the Defias rogue Klaven Mortwake. It is a well known launching point for the Brotherhood's activity.", "Dust_Plains" },
  ["furlbrows pumpkin farm"] = { "Farmer Furlbrow and his wife Verna kept a pumpkin farm here until the Defias Brotherhood swept through Westfall with their mechanical harvest golems and took it. Defias now hold the farm buildings. The Furlbrows fled to the road in the north of Westfall, leaving their pumpkins behind.", "Furlbrow's_Pumpkin_Farm" },
  ["gold coast quarry"] = { "For a long time the pits of Gold Coast Quarry gave Stormwind gold and ore, before Westfall fell into ruin. The Defias Brotherhood owns the quarry now and patrols its stone.", "Gold_Coast_Quarry" },
  ["jangolode mine"] = { "Kobolds occupy the shafts of the Jangolode Mine now, and it is rumored that the Defias Brotherhood has dealings with them. The mine was one of the main ore sources in Westfall, and its ore went to Stormwind.", "Jangolode_Mine" },
  ["jansen stead"] = { "The northernmost farm in Westfall, the Jansen Stead, stands close to the bridge into Elwynn Forest. Its owners were driven off by the Defias Brotherhood, whose agents and harvest golems control it now.", "Jansen_Stead" },
  ["longshore"] = { "Westfall's coast runs long and wide, one of the biggest beaches in Azeroth. Murlocs roam it in swarms, and Old Murk-Eye roams among them. Off the coast lie the wrecks of many ships, elven destroyers among them.\n\nOn the far southwestern coast stands the farm of Daphne Stilwell. The Defias Brotherhood has all but taken over the land around it.", "Longshore" },
  ["molsen farm"] = { "The Molsen Farm lies among the many farmsteads of Westfall held by the Defias Brotherhood. The Brotherhood's harvest golems patrol its fields, striking down anyone who wanders too close.", "Molsen_Farm" },
  ["moonbrook"] = { "Moonbrook was a mining town and the center of Westfall's farmland, the breadbasket of the kingdom. Before the orcs came, the gold deposits beneath it made up a third of Stormwind's treasury. The orcs razed it in the First War, among the last towns to fall before they destroyed Stormwind Keep, and it was rebuilt after the Second War.\n\nThe Defias Brotherhood holds it now. They drove out the townsfolk, and only a few Defias stay on above ground as sentries. The rest live below, in the old mining tunnels beneath the town.", "Moonbrook" },
  ["saldeans farm"] = { "Farmer Saldean and his wife Salma have refused to leave. This is the one farm in Westfall the Defias Brotherhood never overran, though harvest golems of the Brotherhood roam its fields. The farmhouse has been a halfway house for years, and the couple do what they can to keep their guests alive.", "Saldean's_Farm" },
  ["sentinel hill"] = { "Sentinel Hill stands on high ground in eastern Westfall. A tower watches from the top, and below it on the slope are cottages, a forge, an inn and a lumber mill. Gryan Stoutmantle's People's Militia holds the hill as the last military bastion in the region and calls on anyone willing to fight the Defias. Stormwind gives it little help.", "Sentinel_Hill" },
  ["stendels pond"] = { "A small pond lies southwest of Sentinel Hill. Gnolls ring it, and a few old bones and two graves lie at its edge, which hints that the water is no longer fit to drink.", "Stendel's_Pond" },
  ["westfall lighthouse"] = { "The lighthouse stands off the far southern coast of Westfall, and at night its beam still circles out over the water, though the hand that keeps it belongs to a dead man. The ghost of Captain Grayson keeps the light. In life he was a pirate captain whose ship broke on the rocks one night when the lighthouse was dark. After he died he came to the tower and watched Old Murk-Eye drive the keeper's family away.\n\nThe family returned and relit the flame. Old Murk-Eye came back with the murlocs of the coast, and this time the family died before Grayson's eyes. No one is left to tend the light but him, and he stands guard outside the tower.", "Westfall_Lighthouse" },
}
areas[1437] = {
  ["angerfang encampment"] = { "Angerfang Encampment sprawls across the central Wetlands, the largest camp the Dragonmaw clan holds in the region, home to the Angerfang orcs. Nek'rosh Skullcrusher, son of Nekros Skullcrusher, commands them and means to make the camp the gathering place for an attack on Menethil Harbor. He has ordered catapults built for the assault.", "Angerfang_Encampment" },
  ["baradin bay"] = { "The waters of Baradin Bay wash the coast where Khaz Modan meets Lordaeron. The bay's history lies broken along its bottom. The Kul Tiran navy patrolled it regularly during and after the Second War, and its Third Fleet was destroyed in that war. Some of the wrecks lie shattered along the coast of the Wetlands, and others went down to the dark bottom of the sea.\n\nMurlocs and crocolisks drift in the shallows. Crabs and sharks hold the deeper water.", "Baradin_Bay" },
  ["black channel marsh"] = { "Raptors have their lairs all through Black Channel Marsh, in the southwestern Wetlands. The road to Menethil Harbor skirts the marsh, and the hungrier raptors wander close to the roadside.", "Black_Channel_Marsh" },
  ["bluegill marsh"] = { "Several camps of Bluegill murlocs stand in the marshes of the western Wetlands. The murlocs here move in groups of two or three, and Gobbler, the fiercest of them, leads them.", "Bluegill_Marsh" },
  ["deepwater tavern"] = { "Fights break out often in the Deepwater Tavern, and Tapoke \"Slim\" Jahn starts many of them. It is a busy gathering place in Menethil Harbor.", "Deepwater_Tavern" },
  ["direforge hill"] = { "Dark Iron dwarves keep a major campsite on Direforge Hill, in the northeastern Wetlands. The warlock Balgaras the Foul leads them.", "Direforge_Hill" },
  ["dragonmaw gates"] = { "Four grand gates block the winding path that climbs from the Wetlands to Grim Batol. The red dragonflight guards them under the mighty drake Axtroz and holds the way against anyone who comes near. Outside the gates, along the road, lie the ruins of an orc caravan that tried to flee the fortress.", "Dragonmaw_Gates" },
  ["dun algaz"] = { "The tunnel through Dun Algaz is older than the War of the Three Hammers. When that war drove the Wildhammer dwarves out of Ironforge, they passed through here.\n\nThe Horde retreated this way too. Beaten back from Dun Modr in the Second War, the orcs fell south across the Thandol Valley and dug in at the outpost here. Anduin Lothar, wanting them pushed clear out of Khaz Modan, sent his forces to tear the stronghold down. The assault failed. The orcs held on, and Dun Algaz became the last ground the Horde kept in the region.\n\nThe dwarven mountaineers who watch this pass kept mostly to the hills above it, but the Dragonmaw clan has overrun their post and driven them back to the upper tunnel. With the lower pass lost, the road between the dwarven homelands and Menethil Harbor has grown far more dangerous.", "Dun_Algaz" },
  ["dun modr"] = { "Whoever holds the old dwarven city of Dun Modr commands the country around it, and it has changed hands often. In the Second War the Horde built an outpost beside its ruins and used it as a staging ground, beating back Alliance troops and raiding the farmlands nearby. Warriors from Stromgarde laid siege and drove the orcs south, but the Horde struck back and took the place again. When Anduin Lothar pushed the Horde back through Thandol Span, the city came back into Alliance hands and stayed there after the war.\n\nNow the Dark Iron dwarves have it. While Ironforge's soldiers died in wars far from home, the Dark Irons under the warlock Balgaras the Foul laid waste to the Span, and southern Dun Modr fell soon after. A blacksmith's shop and an inn with a brewery still stand among the wreckage.", "Dun_Modr" },
  ["green belt"] = { "Crocolisks, bog beasts and oozes fill the Green Belt in the eastern Wetlands. Rethiel the Greenwarden, a bog beast himself, watches over the land here. Gnolls have pushed into the marsh, and some of his lesser bog beast kin have been corrupted.\n\nA wrecked Dragonmaw caravan lies in the Belt, red whelps denning among its ruin. It seems to have been broken when the freed dragons turned on the clan.", "Green_Belt" },
  ["grim batol"] = { "The walls and spires of Grim Batol rise in the eastern Wetlands toward a steel-gray sky, but most of the fortress lies underground. The Wildhammer dwarves founded it and lived there until a curse fell on it in the War of the Three Hammers. They left it empty.\n\nIn the Second War, Orgrim Doomhammer sent the Dragonmaw clan to take the empty fortress. They held the dragon queen Alexstrasza prisoner in its depths and forced the red dragons to serve the Horde. Their hold broke when the dragons were freed and burned the orcs who had enslaved them.\n\nMost thought the fortress's evil had died with the clan. The dwarves sensed a darkness still in the mountain and left. Now Alexstrasza has set red dragonkin around it, and they let no one enter.", "Grim_Batol" },
  ["ironbeards tomb"] = { "Over the graveyard at Ironbeard's Tomb stands a statue of a dwarf, carved, it seems, in the likeness of Ironbeard himself. The crypt lies just west of the road to Dun Modr, in the north-central Wetlands. Oozes of black, crimson and monstrous kinds have defiled it, and no one who wishes to bury their dead comes here any longer.", "Ironbeard's_Tomb" },
  ["lost fleet"] = { "The Wave Mistress and the Intrepid lie on the seabed of Baradin Bay, off the coast of northern Bluegill Marsh. They belonged to the Kul Tiran Third Fleet, commanded in the Second War by Derek Proudmoore, elder son of Lord Admiral Daelin Proudmoore. At the Battle of Crestfall, red dragons enslaved by the Dragonmaw clan fell on the fleet and set it ablaze. Derek climbed the crow's nest to warn his father, and a dragon killed him there. His ship went down soon after. Only First Mate Fitzsimmons of the Wave Mistress lived.\n\nThe dead did not rest. A curse has bound the sunken crews into undeath. Only First Mate Snellig kept his mind, and his ghost walks the nearby shore.", "Lost_Fleet" },
  ["menethil bay"] = { "Crocolisks of every age and size crowd Menethil Bay, and hunters take them for their fine leather. The bay is an inlet of Baradin Bay, lying between Menethil Harbor and the northern cliffs of Dun Morogh. At its entrance rests the wreck of the Flying Osprey, a ship of the Kul Tiran Third Fleet.", "Menethil_Bay" },
  ["menethil harbor"] = { "The wall is the first thing anyone notices, thick stone thrown up around a harbor town on a small island off the western shore. Four towers rise above it, and there is no way in but the bridge, the sea, or a secret path running underground to the mainland. Sailors, travelers, smugglers and pirates fill its streets.\n\nThe Horde built the first shipyard here. After the First War, Warchief Orgrim Doomhammer chose this shore as the place to build the fleet that would carry his army past Lordaeron's ground defenses, and the orcs stripped iron and timber from the dwarven mines of Khaz Modan to make it. When the Second War ended, the Alliance took the place and rebuilt it as a harbor town. Grand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore named it Menethil Harbor, for King Terenas Menethil II of Lordaeron. Raptors out of the hills strike at the town now and then, never in great numbers.", "Menethil_Harbor" },
  ["menethil keep"] = { "Captain Stoutfist commands the Menethil militia from Menethil Keep, whose four towers watch over the harbor town below like stern guardians.", "Menethil_Keep" },
  ["mosshide fen"] = { "The Mosshide gnolls hold this waterlogged fen in the eastern Wetlands, and their camps dot the ground. Their vicious leader Gnawbone orders the tribe to seize any stranger seen in the marsh and eat them.", "Mosshide_Fen" },
  ["raptor ridge"] = { "Raptors lair in large numbers along Raptor Ridge, in the eastern Wetlands, just northwest of the dangerous Dragonmaw Gates. A cavern in the ridge shelters the fierce Razormaw Matriarch, who watches over her brood.", "Raptor_Ridge" },
  ["saltspray glen"] = { "When a strong wind blows off the bay, the ocean throws its spray far inland at Saltspray Glen. That is how the glen got its name. It is a small stretch of the northwestern Wetlands, open to Baradin Bay on the north and west. Raptors nest thickly here.", "Saltspray_Glen" },
  ["sundown marsh"] = { "The deepest part of the Wetlands lies in the northwest, in Sundown Marsh. Here stands Swiftgear Station, a small stone dwelling with a windmill, where an odd couple named Fradd Swiftgear and Wenna Silkbeard make their home. They sell peculiar things they find in the marsh. All around them the swamp is full of aggressive crocolisks, gnolls, raptors, bog beasts and oozes.", "Sundown_Marsh" },
  ["thandol span"] = { "Two great stone arches carry the road over the gap between the Wetlands and the Arathi Highlands, one of the greatest engineering works the dwarves ever finished. Dwarves raised them after the War of the Three Hammers, when Ironforge wanted to keep trade and ties with its Wildhammer cousins in Aerie Peak. The bridges served as both road and symbol between the two dwarven kingdoms, and the trade enriched both. The Anvilmar family has guarded the Span for at least three generations.\n\nNow the bridges are scarred. The Dark Iron dwarves struck, and one arch was all but destroyed. Dwarven forces fought fiercely to defend the other and to retake the nearby town of Dun Modr, and many of them died.", "Thandol_Span" },
  ["thelgen rock"] = { "A great cavern runs deep into the mountains of the southern Wetlands, and the place takes its name, Thelgen Rock, from it. Poisonous spiders crawl through the caves, and veins of incendicite ore lie somewhere in the deeper tunnels.", "Thelgen_Rock" },
  ["whelgars excavation site"] = { "Dwarves came to dig here in peace, east of Menethil Harbor. Then mottled raptors overran the site. A small group of dwarves, Prospector Whelgar and his crew among them, is trapped on a ridge above their own excavation, with raptors roaming the pits below.", "Whelgar's_Excavation_Site" },
}
areas[1438] = {
  ["aldrassil"] = { "Aldrassil rises at the heart of Shadowglen, a giant tree with a village in its boughs. Tenaron Stormgrip, the leader of Shadowglen, makes his home inside the very top, above the reach of the settlement below, and watches over the glen from there. From the crown he sends promising young night elves out into the world.", "Aldrassil" },
  ["banethil barrow den"] = { "The night elves built Ban'ethil Barrow Den as one of their sacred places, a barrow where sleeping druids of the Talon could rest. It has two parts, the Lower Den and the Upper Den. The corrupted furbolgs of the Gnarlpine tribe have taken it over, and it is a dangerous place for a lone night elf to enter.", "Ban'ethil_Barrow_Den" },
  ["banethil hollow"] = { "A small ravine cuts into the land south of the road from Dolanaar to Darnassus. Hostile furbolgs hold its slopes and ambush travelers on the road now and then. At the back of the hollow lies Ban'ethil Barrow Den, sunk into the earth.", "Ban'ethil_Hollow" },
  ["cleft"] = { "The Cleft lies northwest of Dolanaar, close to the corrupted cave of Fel Rock, and nothing makes its home there.", "Cleft" },
  ["dolanaar"] = { "Young night elves leaving Shadowglen come to Dolanaar first. The village stands in the central-eastern part of Teldrassil, just southwest of the glen, and its central position draws it into much of the island's business. Tallonkai Swiftroot leads the effort here to fight back the corrupted furbolgs and satyrs of the Great Tree.", "Dolanaar" },
  ["fel rock"] = { "A cave lies just north of Dolanaar that was once beautiful and is now corrupted. Grells infest its dark hollows, and the vicious satyr Lord Melenas makes his home within. He has gathered a small army of grells to guard his lair, led by one called Threggil.", "Fel_Rock" },
  ["gnarlpine hold"] = { "In the far southwest corner of Teldrassil, Gnarlpine Hold is the home of the Gnarlpine furbolgs and the seat of their chieftain, Ursal the Mauler.\n\nThe tribe was once peaceful and counted the night elves as allies. Now its furbolgs have turned berserk and attack them, and the madness has carried them out of their old villages in the south and west of the island into Starbreeze Village and Ban'ethil Barrow Den.", "Gnarlpine_Hold" },
  ["lake alameth"] = { "South of Dolanaar, Lake Al'Ameth spreads out as one of the largest waters on Teldrassil. Little stands on its shores beyond a small jetty. Lately many enraged timberlings have been seen along the banks, and the lake is no longer safe. Denalan keeps a small tent near the water, where he tries to understand the strange affliction seeping into the island.", "Lake_Al'Ameth" },
  ["oracle glade"] = { "The first and most powerful of Teldrassil's moonwells lies in the Oracle Glade, a shrine in the northwest of the island. Beside it stands the Oracle Tree. Its trunk is wide, and its boughs take the shape of a great eagle perched atop a mighty bear. Many believe nature magic grew it that way. The tree has watched over Teldrassil's growth alongside the druids, and it can shed pieces of inscribed bark to send reports to Archdruid Fandral Staghelm in Darnassus.\n\nBloodfeather harpies have built nests nearby and threaten the tree. A small group of Sentinels has been sent to push them back.", "Oracle_Glade" },
  ["pools of arlithrien"] = { "Small pools gather in the west of Teldrassil, south of the entrance to Darnassus. The place was peaceful once, and a lone moonwell still stands among the pools. The night elves abandoned it when the Gnarlpine furbolgs became a threat, and hostile wildlife now roams the water's edge.", "Pools_of_Arlithrien" },
  ["ruttheran village"] = { "Rut'theran Village sits at the very foot of the World Tree, the port for the night elf city of Darnassus far above. A portal here leads straight up into the capital, and hippogryphs roost at the village. A single dock serves The Moonspray, the boat that crosses to Auberdine on the Darkshore coast. When the Midsummer Fire Festival comes, the braziers in the village are lit.", "Rut'theran_Village" },
  ["shadowglen"] = { "A perpetual dusk lies over Shadowglen, a small glade in the northeast of Teldrassil that never quite sees full daylight. Mountains ring it on every side. The only gap is to the south, where a path parts the range and heads toward Dolanaar. At the center of the clearing stands the great tree Aldrassil, as though the whole glen were arranged around it.", "Shadowglen" },
  ["shadowthread cave"] = { "Great spiders have always lived in Shadowthread Cave, in the northwest corner of Shadowglen. Lately they have grown more hostile, and the cave is far more dangerous. The largest of them, Githyiss the Vile, has been the bane of more than one inexperienced night elf.", "Shadowthread_Cave" },
  ["starbreeze village"] = { "Southeast of Shadowglen stand the few small houses of Starbreeze Village, once a night elf settlement. Corrupted furbolgs of the Gnarlpine tribe have overrun it. Uruson patrols the village, and Ferocitas the Dream Eater is found on its outskirts.", "Starbreeze_Village" },
  ["wellspring lake"] = { "Night elf fishermen once favored Wellspring Lake, a small lake northeast of Darnassus. A natural spring deep within the land of the World Tree feeds it, and the Wellspring River drains it. Corrupted timberlings now make their home here, and nightsabers and spiders infest the land around it.", "Wellspring_Lake" },
  ["wellspring river"] = { "The Wellspring River rises from a spring deep within the great tree. It flows north from Wellspring Lake and cascades over the edge of Teldrassil in a great waterfall, falling to the sea far below. Crazed timberlings patrol its banks and attack anyone who wanders near.", "Wellspring_River" },
}
areas[1439] = {
  ["ametharan"] = { "The ruins of Ameth'Aran lie southeast of Auberdine. Queen Azshara raised it and its twin Bashal'Aran for her favored Highborne, with bazaars and temples to Elune, and Athrikus Narassin governed it for her. In the last days of the old empire the town was already in ruins, and its people clung to the ground as though that might spare them from the destruction. Athrikus came to them and said a spell would keep them safe. As he spoke, the elves around him died, and their souls went into his soul gems, which he said would give them eternal life.\n\nTheir restless spirits still fill the ruins. Two large tablets stand among the stones, carved with the story of the town and its fall. A sentinel who tried to read them was driven off by the Highborne.", "Ameth'Aran" },
  ["auberdine"] = { "Auberdine stands on a coast the Sundering made, where the sea ruined the great cities of the old night elf empire. Darkshore stayed empty after that, and every attempt to settle it failed except this one town on the Veiled Sea.\n\nDwarves, gnomes, humans and even high elves walk its streets, which is unusual for a night elf town. Its port draws traders from the Eastern Kingdoms, and to help them the town is lit more brightly than most night elf places, with some signposts written in both Darnassian and Common. The elder Thundris Windweaver runs the town's daily affairs, and Sentinels guard it. Murlocs sink boats along this coast, and the townsfolk make armor from the scales of the ones they kill.\n\nBy the lighthouse, the wreck of a ship commanded by an elf named Elura lies on the rocks. Her cargo of elunite ore went down with her.", "Auberdine" },
  ["bashalaran"] = { "Asterion was a mage as skilled with a blade as with the arcane arts, and he served as second to Athrikus Narassin in Bashal'Aran. He lived among its people and often traveled to Ameth'Aran to tend to its folk. Then Athrikus used his craft to make a moonstone seal and bound Asterion in the ruins. No one knows why he turned on his second. Asterion lived on in pain, and when he died he found that his spirit was held as surely as his body had been.\n\nThe town itself fell when the first Well of Eternity was shattered. Satyrs and grells have since made their home in the ruins and grow stronger on the magic left in the stones. They never go near the shrine where Asterion is imprisoned.", "Bashal'Aran" },
  ["blackwood den"] = { "In the far south of Darkshore, near the border of Ashenvale, a few furbolg camps and a small cave make up Blackwood Den, also called Blackwood Hold. The Blackwood furbolgs who live here have been corrupted by fel moss. Their warriors, shamans and great ursas hold the camps and the cave.", "Blackwood_Den" },
  ["cliffspring falls"] = { "Cliffspring Falls feed the Cliffspring River and are the main supply of drinking water in Darkshore. The water was once pure. Now the cave it flows from has become corrupted, like much of the land around it.\n\nPeople disagree about the cause. Some say the corruption seeps in from Felwood. Others blame the Twilight's Hammer, whose cultists have been found deep in the caves where the water begins. Naga crawl through the cave as well.", "Cliffspring_Falls" },
  ["cliffspring river"] = { "The Cliffspring River runs from Cliffspring Falls out to the sea in northern Darkshore. The Stormscale naga have tainted it, and the wildlife that drinks from it often falls sick.", "Cliffspring_River" },
  ["grove of the ancients"] = { "Onu, an Ancient of Lore, makes his home among the stones of the Grove of the Ancients, the sacred ruin of an old night elf temple in the mountains of southeastern Darkshore. It was built around the surging Wildbend River, and the Circle of Ancients meets here. Onu is patient and wise, with a sleeping druid for company, and he is troubled by the naga and by the Twilight's Hammer cult at work in the region.", "Grove_of_the_Ancients" },
  ["long wash"] = { "Despite its name, the Long Wash is the shortest stretch of coast in Darkshore. Gray sand runs south from Auberdine to the water west of Ameth'Aran, under an overcast sky that never lifts. Debris strews the beach, and murlocs have been showing up on it more often.", "Long_Wash" },
  ["masters glaive"] = { "A colossal skull lies in the southwest of Darkshore with an adamantine glaive driven through the bone. It is what remains of Soggoth the Slitherer, a herald of the Old Gods, and the blade is the Master's Glaive. The night elves once held the place holy.\n\nTablets found by Explorers' League diggers tell what happened here. The titans sent twenty stone giants against Soggoth, and the battle was so terrible that only the largest, Kronn, lived to see its end. He let Soggoth's tendrils wrap around him, just long enough to be lifted within reach of the skull and his great sword. The sword stayed where it struck.\n\nThe Twilight's Hammer now controls the site. Its cultists dig into Soggoth's remains, meaning to bring him back to life.", "Master's_Glaive" },
  ["mists edge"] = { "Gray beaches and gloomy bays make up Mist's Edge, the longest shoreline in Darkshore, running from near Auberdine to the region's northern tip. Huge carcasses of sea animals lie on the sand, and the wrecks of sunken ships dot the water. Crabs scuttle along the entire shore, and murlocs in the bays are an ever-present threat.", "Mist's_Edge" },
  ["remtravels excavation"] = { "Prospector Remtravel of the Explorers' League, a famed archaeologist, gave this dig its name. His team uncovered evidence of a great society here, and he found a fossil of unusual mystic properties, then forgot where he put it. Then huge golems sprang up out of the ground and overran the site, and Remtravel never seemed to notice.", "Remtravel's_Excavation" },
  ["ruins of mathystra"] = { "Past the Tower of Althalaxx, in the north of Darkshore, lie the ruins of Mathystra. Before the Great Sundering, it stood as a city of the night elves, raised in the age of the Highborne. When the Well of Eternity tore itself apart and the world broke beneath it, Mathystra was ruined along with so much else, and the night elves who survived did not return to rebuild it. They withdrew instead to smaller villages along the coast further south, leaving the ruins behind.\n\nStormscale naga have since taken the ruins for their own and guard them fiercely against anyone who comes near. Scattered relics of the old Highborne still lie among the fallen stones, and young night elves sometimes come searching through Mathystra for them, wary of the naga and of the ghost sabers that prowl among the ruins of what their people once built.", "Ruins_of_Mathystra" },
  ["tower of althalaxx"] = { "Athrikus Narassin governed the twin towns for Queen Azshara, but he grew bored with the post and spent his days in a tower of his own, Althalaxx, some way from them. The explosion of the Well of Eternity threw him far off and left him immortal, with the power to drain other mages to feed his own.\n\nNow the tower holds a cult that calls itself the Cult of the Dark Strand. Warlocks gather there, a satyr among them, and Athrikus leads them.", "Tower_of_Althalaxx" },
  ["twilight vale"] = { "Twilight Vale takes in the forests south of Ameth'Aran, running to the border of Ashenvale. Corrupted furbolgs, feral nightsabers and rabid bears haunt these woods, and travelers are counseled to keep to the roads rather than wander beneath the trees.", "Twilight_Vale" },
  ["wildbend river"] = { "The Wildbend River carries its sickness down from the mountains, a continuation of the Bloodvenom River spilling out of Felwood into southern Darkshore, near the Grove of the Ancients. Its waters run tainted, the corruption of Felwood following the current down into these lower lands.", "Wildbend_River" },
}
areas[1440] = {
  ["astranaar"] = { "Astranaar stands on an island in the middle of a lake, joined to the shore by two bridges, with the main road running through it. It is the largest settlement in Ashenvale, and a cemetery lies by the lake.\n\nRaene Wolfrunner commands the Sentinels and scouts here and works to ease the corruption in the ancient forest. To the north the Thistlefur furbolgs have grown in number, and she fears they will find the courage to attack Astranaar itself.", "Astranaar" },
  ["bathrans haunt"] = { "Broken pillars and fallen marble mark a night elf ruin that the elves abandoned long ago. Agents of the Forsaken have moved in, mostly herbalists and the thugs who guard them. Where they work the grass has turned brown and the flowers have withered. Among the stones they have built a makeshift laboratory and use the local herbs for malicious ends.\n\nA remedial herb called Bathran's Hair grows in this one spot and nowhere else.", "Bathran's_Haunt" },
  ["blackfathom deeps"] = { "A temple to Elune, the night elves' moon goddess, once stood on the Zoram Strand. The Great Sundering shattered it and sank it beneath the waves of the Veiled Sea, and for thousands of years it lay untouched.\n\nThen the Twilight's Hammer came, called by whispers and foul dreams. The cult sacrificed countless innocents, and was rewarded with a charge. It must guard and feed Aku'mai the Devourer, a three-headed hydra and a favored pet of the Old Gods, until she is ready to loose her dark power on the world. Twilight Lord Kelris leads the cultists in the flooded caverns, and the corruption of an Old God has tainted the temple's moonwell. Naga and satyrs, drawn by the ruins' ancient power, have come as well to search its secrets. The place is called Blackfathom now, and the name fits.", "Blackfathom_Deeps" },
  ["bloodtooth camp"] = { "Ran Bloodtooth, the king of all the furbolgs in Ashenvale, holds this camp just west of the Falfarren River and southwest of the southern moonwell. The camp takes its name from him. Every furbolg in it has been corrupted, and they attack any outsider who comes near.", "Bloodtooth_Camp" },
  ["bough shadow"] = { "A Great Tree grows here, and it is a gate into the Emerald Dream. The green drakes Phantim and Dreamstalker guard the portal. Cursed green dragonspawn have slipped out of it into the woods, where they patrol in groups.", "Bough_Shadow" },
  ["demon fall canyon"] = { "The pit lord Mannoroth fought Thrall and Grommash Hellscream here. He knocked Thrall senseless, but he was not quick enough to dodge Grommash's axe, which cleaved into his chest. His death burst in fire and light, and it tore the life from Grommash as well. With Mannoroth gone, the Burning Legion's curse on the orcs was broken. The canyon takes its name from that fight.\n\nA monument to Grommash stands here, and many orcs make the pilgrimage to honor the warrior who freed them. The ground is still fel-corrupted, and lesser demons now fill the canyon.", "Demon_Fall_Canyon" },
  ["demon fall ridge"] = { "Lesser demons haunt the slopes of this ridge and the abandoned barrow den cut into its side. In the deepest part of the den waits Diathorus the Seeker, a dreadlord who rules the demonic conclave in Ashenvale.", "Demon_Fall_Ridge" },
  ["dordanil barrow den"] = { "Night elf druids have long come to the Dor'Danil Barrow Den to rest. The Horde's advance into Ashenvale left the den behind its lines, and the orcs cared little for sleeping elves. The Forsaken, who joined the Horde later, cared a great deal. They experimented on the sleeping druids, severing them from their spirits, and drove them insane.\n\nForsaken assassins now skulk through the den among the enraged souls of the severed druids. Three of the spirits are stronger than the rest: Uthil Mooncall, Mavoris Cloudsbreak and Taneel Darkwood.", "Dor'Danil_Barrow_Den" },
  ["falfarren river"] = { "The Falfarren runs across Ashenvale from northeast to southwest. It roughly divides the night elf land under Astranaar in the west from the orc land under Splintertree Post in the east. The river is neither deep nor wide, though heavy rain makes it swell.\n\nIt rises on the slopes of Mount Hyjal, falls through a series of waterfalls into a lake, and in the end slips underground beneath the mountains of the northern Barrens. Aggressive bog beasts roam beneath the water near Felfire Hill.", "Falfarren_River" },
  ["fallen sky lake"] = { "A small lake lies south of Silverwing Outpost, with a single island at its center. Shadethickets, bog beasts of the forest, ring the shore, and the strongest of them, the Shadethicket Oracle, keeps to the island.", "Fallen_Sky_Lake" },
  ["felfire hill"] = { "Felfire Hill still bears the scars of the Third War, when infernals fell burning from the sky and struck the earth here. Foul demons have lived among its stones ever since. The Horde has fortified both bridges onto the hill to keep attacks from reaching its settlements nearby.", "Felfire_Hill" },
  ["fire scar shrine"] = { "The demonic influence of the Shadow Council has corrupted Fire Scar Shrine, in southwestern Ashenvale, until it became a place of great evil. An orcish warlock of the Cult of the Dark Strand, Ilkrud Magthrull, lives here.\n\nDemons infest the shrine now. A rare doomguard named Akkrilus appears in the area from time to time.", "Fire_Scar_Shrine" },
  ["forest song"] = { "The druid Kayneth Stillwind is the lone inhabitant of Forest Song, an old night elf ruin in the northeast of Ashenvale. From its stones he directs the fight against the Forsaken in the region.\n\nThe Forsaken have crept into Ashenvale and lurk at the night elves' ruins and barrows, and the elves believe they mean to spread disease through the forest.", "Forest_Song" },
  ["greenpaw village"] = { "Corrupted furbolgs of the Foulweald tribe have made Greenpaw Village their home, in central Ashenvale south of Raynewood Retreat. The shaman Oakpaw is among them.", "Greenpaw_Village" },
  ["howling vale"] = { "In a cave near the Felwood border stands the Shrine of Mel'Thandris. In front of it is carved an ancient inscription praising Elune, and the shrine bears another, in Darnassian, that reads \"The truth is a guiding light.\"\n\nThe shrine lies in ruins. A pack of worgen called the Terrowulf has taken it over, and their chilling howls give the vale its name.", "Howling_Vale" },
  ["iris lake"] = { "The corruption of Felwood has crept into Iris Lake, in north-central Ashenvale, one of the many parts of the night elf forest to fall to it. The water has turned a sickly yellowish green. Twisted bog beasts roam its shores and attack anyone who comes near.", "Iris_Lake" },
  ["kargathia keep"] = { "The officers of the Warsong clan make their base at Kargathia Keep, in the eastern part of the Warsong Lumber Camp, just west of the Southfury River. Spiked barricades ring the stronghold. Its position lets reinforcements come down from Orgrimmar if the Alliance moves against the camp in force.\n\nThe keep is named for the legendary orc hero Kargath Bladefist.", "Kargathia_Keep" },
  ["lake falathim"] = { "Elune's moonlight touches down on Lake Falathim at every hour, day or night. Night elves once came here to swim and fish. Ferocious murlocs have since overrun the lake. A scholar named Teronis was last seen near its shore and never returned.", "Lake_Falathim" },
  ["maestras post"] = { "A tower and one large night elf hall make up Maestra's Post, and only a few people live here. Two of them are humans, the only ones in Ashenvale. Delgren the Purifier and Feero Ironhand came from the Argent Dawn to help the night elves clear their forest of demons and the undead. They work from the post to undermine the Cult of the Dark Strand.", "Maestra's_Post" },
  ["mystral lake"] = { "The water elementals of Mystral Lake were once loyal to the night elves. A Forsaken mage corrupted them. It is the largest lake in the forest, and its tainted elementals make it dangerous to anyone who comes to the shore.", "Mystral_Lake" },
  ["night run"] = { "A few broken pillars and pieces of ruined stonework shelter the Felmusk satyrs at Night Run, the smallest of the satyr holdings in Ashenvale. The ruins stand on a spur of land that juts out into the forest. Cliffs too steep to climb guard its sides.\n\nThe Felmusk are allied with the Cult of the Dark Strand.", "Night_Run" },
  ["nightsong woods"] = { "Most of the heavy fighting between the Horde and the Alliance happens in Nightsong Woods, the eastern part of Ashenvale, where the Warsong clan and the Silverwing Sentinels both hold ground. The Sentinels keep the west for the Alliance, and the Warsong clan keeps the east for the Horde.\n\nThe far east is the most contested ground of all. Satyrs, Warsong orcs and demons of the Burning Legion each hold a piece of it. In the south, the Warsong orcs have cut down much of the ancient forest and use the thick timber to build their settlements, leaving open, wounded ground where the old trees once stood unbroken.", "Nightsong_Woods" },
  ["raynewood retreat"] = { "At the top of the Great Tree Raynewood hides Ordanus, a keeper of the grove and one of the eldest sons of the forest god Cenarius.\n\nBelow him, Cenarion Protectors, Blink Dragons and the Laughing Sisters hold the Alliance outpost and watch the path that leads north into corrupted Felwood.", "Raynewood_Retreat" },
  ["ruins of ordilaran"] = { "Not much remains of the ancient city that once stood at Ordil'Aran. Among the broken stone grows a tree called the Heartswood.\n\nThe Cult of the Dark Strand has made the ruins its own. Its members wait among the stones, ready to strike at anyone who comes near.", "Ruins_of_Ordil'Aran" },
  ["ruins of stardust"] = { "The Ruins of Stardust stand on an island in a lake, and they were once held holy. The demonic rot of Felwood has since reached them. Water and stone carry the same stain, and large bog beasts, corrupted by the tainted lake, roam among the ruins.\n\nOnly the stardust shrubs scattered about have escaped the taint. They are known to hold powerful healing properties if drunk.", "Ruins_of_Stardust" },
  ["satyrnaar"] = { "Tents stand among the ruined buildings of Satyrnaar, where the Bleakheart satyrs shelter. Of the satyr ruins in the forest, it is the most intact. Their hellcallers summon imps and voidwalkers. They are allied with the Cult of the Dark Strand.\n\nThe ruins lie just off the main road from Splintertree toward Azshara, which makes them the most contested of the satyr holdings.", "Satyrnaar" },
  ["shady nook"] = { "A family of bears lives in the Shady Nook, a small valley in Ashenvale. A cave opens in the hillside, and the bears likely den there.", "Shady_Nook" },
  ["shrine of aessina"] = { "A shrine to Aessina, the spirit of the wilderness, stands in the forest west of Astranaar. The night elves have raised shrines to her across Kalimdor, though few mortals know much about her.", "Shrine_of_Aessina" },
  ["silverwind refuge"] = { "Sentinels guard Silverwind Refuge, and an army of water elementals serves beside them. The night elves count it with Astranaar as one of the major outposts of Ashenvale. It lies just north of Mystral Lake and is a welcoming place, with an inn more luxurious than most in these woods.", "Silverwind_Refuge" },
  ["silverwing grove"] = { "The Silverwing Sentinels keep their base in this grove in south-central Ashenvale, west of Fallen Sky Lake. Sentinel Farsong keeps watch here. Four years after the Third War, the night elves began a great push to win back the forests of Ashenvale and rid their land of the Warsong Outriders. The Silverwings answered the call and swore not to rest until every last orc was beaten and cast out of Warsong Gulch.", "Silverwing_Grove" },
  ["silverwing outpost"] = { "On the Horde side of the Falfarren River stands the eastern bastion of the Alliance's defense against the Horde in Ashenvale. The Sentinels posted at Silverwing Outpost fight off constant attacks from the orcs nearby, who are set on ridding the woods of the Silverwings once and for all.", "Silverwing_Outpost" },
  ["southfury river"] = { "The Southfury begins as a lake in Mount Hyjal. From there it drops in a run of spectacular waterfalls and comes down wide and swift, likely the most prominent river in Kalimdor. It marks the border between Ashenvale and Azshara, and farther south it divides the Barrens from Durotar.\n\nJust north of the western bridge to Orgrimmar, one spot on the water touches four lands at once. Ashenvale, Azshara, Durotar and the Barrens all meet there. At its mouth the river empties into the Great Sea in a last cascade, northeast of the goblin port of Ratchet.", "Southfury_River" },
  ["splintertree post"] = { "Grommash Hellscream and his Warsong clan raised Splintertree Post during the Third War, when the orcs first ventured into Ashenvale. It is one of the oldest Horde settlements on Kalimdor.\n\nThe night elves have not forgotten Grommash. Tricked by the Burning Legion, he killed the demigod Cenarius in these woods. With that and the strain on the peace between Horde and Alliance, Splintertree draws a great deal of retaliation, especially since raids on Astranaar are frequent.\n\nOrcs and tauren hold the post now, mostly scouts and guards.", "Splintertree_Post" },
  ["talondeep path"] = { "A winding tunnel runs through the rock between Ashenvale and the Stonetalon Mountains. It opens west of Mystral Lake on one side and in the northwest of Windshear Crag on the other. Small dead ends branch off it, and odd holes are worn into its walls. Nothing lives inside, and the remains of an old road can be found on the rock above.", "Talondeep_Path" },
  ["thistlefur village"] = { "Furbolgs of the Thistlefur tribe make their home in this village north of Astranaar. They are corrupted and hostile, and they attack anyone who wanders too close. Two ferocious warriors, Dal Bloodclaw and Rorgish Jowl, lead them.", "Thistlefur_Village" },
  ["warsong labor camp"] = { "Orc guards watch over loggers and harvesters at the Warsong Labor Camp in southeastern Ashenvale, east of Fallen Sky Lake. The camp sits far from help, so Alliance raiders now and then strike the guards without fear of retribution.", "Warsong_Labor_Camp" },
  ["warsong lumber camp"] = { "The forests of Ashenvale ring with axes at the Warsong Lumber Camp, where the Warsong clan strips the ancient trees for timber. Peons and deforesters do the cutting with a shredder beside them, while Horde grunts, scouts and shamans stand guard. The camp keeps a kennel, and its stronghold is Kargathia Keep, named for the orc hero Kargath Bladefist. The keep stands just west of the Southfury River, close enough for reinforcements to come from Orgrimmar.", "Warsong_Lumber_Camp" },
  ["xavian"] = { "Satyrs have made their home in the ruins of an ancient night elf temple in northeastern Ashenvale. Of the three satyr settlements in the forest, Xavian seems to be the center of their power, for Prince Raze and Geltharis both live here. Rogues, hellcallers, felsworn and betrayers of the sect fill the ruins.", "Xavian" },
  ["zoram strand"] = { "Before the Great Sundering, a night elf settlement named Zoram stood near this beach. The Sundering destroyed it, and the Veiled Sea took what was left.\n\nToday the naga hold the shore. Wrecked ships and stranded sea creatures lie along the sand, and the naga are many. The Horde keeps a small outpost here and fights the Wrathtail naga who contest the coast. Blackfathom Deeps lies on the strand as well.", "Zoram_Strand" },
  ["zoramgar outpost"] = { "Docks reach out over the water at Zoram'gar Outpost, a Horde port on the Zoram Strand. The Horde set it up here and fights the Wrathtail naga from it.", "Zoram'gar_Outpost" },
}
areas[1441] = {
  ["camp ethok"] = { "Galak centaur keep a camp here in northwestern Thousand Needles. It is the smaller of the tribe's two holdings, and most of the Galak live at Splithoof Crag. The camp lies close to the road and to the Horde's Whitereach Post, and from it the centaur send raids against caravans and traveling merchants. The Galak hold no alliance with any other centaur tribe.", "Camp_E'thok" },
  ["darkcloud pinnacle"] = { "Three small mesas here are home to the Grimtotem tauren, with several smaller empty ones around them. Rope bridges join the mesas. Only two ways lead up, a natural slope that climbs a fourth mesa and ends in a bridge, and a second bridge. Both are well guarded.\n\nArnak Grimtotem and Harb Foulmountain lead the Grimtotem here.", "Darkcloud_Pinnacle" },
  ["freewind post"] = { "Freewind Post stands on top of a mesa, out of reach of the Galak centaur war bands that camp below. Its people ride wooden lifts up from the canyon floor, and rope bridges climb from pinnacle to pinnacle until they reach the village. Screeching harpies are a lesser threat.\n\nThe tauren of Freewind grew wary of the Grimtotem on Darkcloud Pinnacle. Tauren were kidnapped, agents of dark and unknown origin came and went, and murder was laid at the Grimtotems' door, most of all at that of their chieftain, Arnak Grimtotem.", "Freewind_Post" },
  ["great lift"] = { "Built by the tauren, this elevator carries travelers between the Barrens and Thousand Needles. Grish Longrunner and Brave Moonhorn keep watch over it.", "Great_Lift" },
  ["highperch"] = { "On this elevated ground in the west, the proud Highperch wyverns keep their nests. Young tauren come from Freewind Post to study them and to take eggs from the ancestral nesting grounds, which are raised as mounts for the Horde. The wyverns are wild, and they attack anyone who comes among the nests.", "Highperch" },
  ["ironstone camp"] = { "A small band of the Horde camps at the border of the Shimmering Flats. They were sent to drive the dwarven diggers out of the Rustmaul Dig Site and got more than they bargained for, when silithid erupted from a nearby cave and attacked them. Moktar Krin leads them, and the silithid still infest the dig site.", "Ironstone_Camp" },
  ["mirage raceway"] = { "A goblin named Pozzik and a gnome named Fizzle Brassbolts each held that their own people were the better engineers. To prove it, they built a raceway and rocket cars to run on it, and enlisted teams to drive them.\n\nGoblin and gnome drag cars race here daily. Supporters from both the Horde and the Alliance come to cheer their favorites, and seedy types come to gamble on the winner. After each race the two teams scramble to find improvements that will give their side an edge.", "Mirage_Raceway" },
  ["razorfen downs"] = { "Legend says that where the demigod Agamaggan fell, his blood raised great masses of thorny vines. The quilboar revere the largest tangle of them as his resting place, and Razorfen Downs is their ancestral burial ground. The entrance is said to be Agamaggan's own maw, an arched gap in the outer dome so hidden that only someone who knows where to look will find it.\n\nThe Downs are the traditional capital of the quilboar. The high priests of the Death's Head tribe live in the thorny maze among a great army of loyal warriors. After the Third War, the lich Amnennar the Coldbringer and agents of the Scourge took control of the quilboar and made the maze a bastion of undead might. The quilboar are fighting to win their city back before he can spread his hold across the Barrens.", "Razorfen_Downs" },
  ["roguefeather den"] = { "Harpies crowd this small cavern in south central Thousand Needles. They screech at anyone who comes near, and at the back of the den they hoard the food they have gathered and guard it zealously.", "Roguefeather_Den" },
  ["rustmaul dig site"] = { "Dwarves of the Explorers' League were uncovering some old ruins here when a Horde war party led by Moktar Krin attacked and killed them all. The fighting stirred up a nest of silithid in a cave at the dig site. The swarm that poured out drove off the war party, which was already weakened. The dig site is abandoned now, and the silithid hold the ground.", "Rustmaul_Dig_Site" },
  ["screeching canyon"] = { "Screeching harpies infest this canyon in southern Thousand Needles, aggressive and loud. At its back lies Roguefeather Den.", "Screeching_Canyon" },
  ["shimmering flats"] = { "A sailing ship lies wrecked in the northern part of the Shimmering Flats, far from any water. It shows what this place once was, the bed of a lake that has dried away. Nothing grows on the flat salt here, at the southeastern end of Thousand Needles. Basilisks, scorpids, turtles and vultures live here.\n\nThe flatness drew goblins and gnomes, who race their machines here, and their inventions are often unstable. Craters pock the ground, and the wreckage of countless failed gizmos lies scattered across the salt.", "Shimmering_Flats" },
  ["splithoof crag"] = { "The Galak centaur keep their village against the northern wall of Thousand Needles, just east of Darkcloud Pinnacle. In a cavern in the mountainside at its center lies Splithoof Hold, their stronghold.", "Splithoof_Crag" },
  ["splithoof hold"] = { "This cave in Splithoof Crag is the stronghold of the Galak centaur. The Ancient Brazier stands inside, and the Sacred Flame upon it burns forever.", "Splithoof_Hold" },
  ["tahonda ruins"] = { "Long ago a tauren village here fell to ruin and sank beneath the sands of the Shimmering Flats. Little is known about it. Saltstone basilisks live among the ruins, and they may be the reason the village was abandoned.\n\nOther signs point to an attack by hostile forces. On one of the leather buildings are two sets of three parallel tears. The ruins lie in the southern reaches of the flats, near the path through the mountains to Tanaris.", "Tahonda_Ruins" },
  ["weathered nook"] = { "Dorn Plainstalker and Prate Cloudseer live in a small, well-hidden cave in the northern wall of Thousand Needles. Dorn teaches the ways of faith, endurance and strength.", "Weathered_Nook" },
  ["weazels crater"] = { "A large, deep crater opens in the northern part of the Shimmering Flats. Where it came from is a mystery. Its name suggests either who found it or who made it. Rubble and smaller craters lie scattered about, and goblins and gnomes race dangerous cars in these parts, so it may be the work of an unstable invention. Saltstone basilisks live in it now.", "Weazel's_Crater" },
  ["whitereach post"] = { "Along the road near Camp E'thok stands a small Horde camp. Kanati Greycloud and the goblin Wizlo Bearingshiner are among those who stay here.", "Whitereach_Post" },
  ["windbreak canyon"] = { "Wind serpents fill this canyon with their nests. It covers most of the ground between Freewind Post and the Shimmering Flats, on the north side of the road.", "Windbreak_Canyon" },
}
areas[1442] = {
  ["blackwolf river"] = { "The Blackwolf River runs out of Cragpool Lake and through the valley of Windshear Crag. It is a small river, and a foul one. The Venture Company's logging of the valley has poisoned its water.", "Blackwolf_River" },
  ["boulderslide cavern"] = { "Boulderslide kobolds nest in this cave at the end of the ravine. They mine without rest for Resonite, a rare purple crystal that glows and carries a trace of earthen magic, and they guard it with their lives.", "Boulderslide_Cavern" },
  ["boulderslide ravine"] = { "Rams graze the slopes of this small ravine, and the occasional cliff stormer wheels overhead. It leads in to the cavern where the Boulderslide kobolds nest.\n\nAcross from that cavern stands a small abandoned cave. Barrels sit inside, crude torches light it, and the walls carry tribal art that may be tauren work.", "Boulderslide_Ravine" },
  ["camp aparaje"] = { "Grimtotem forces from Grimtotem Post, led by Grundig Darkcloud, fell upon Camp Aparaje, a tauren village in the eastern Stonetalon Mountains. Makaba Flathoof made his home here.", "Camp_Aparaje" },
  ["charred vale"] = { "The Charred Vale was a green valley once. Bloodfury harpies called it home, and treants walked its forest as guardians.\n\nThen goblins of the Venture Company came and decimated it. The vale's spirits were enraged, and numerous elementals broke loose when the treants drove the goblins back. The goblins' weapons probably tainted the treants as the goblins left.\n\nThe valley now lies mostly in flames and lava, in the southwest of the Stonetalon Mountains. Blackened ancients and scorched basilisks live among the burning ground.", "Charred_Vale" },
  ["cragpool lake"] = { "An enormous structure built by the Venture Company runs above this small lake. Gerenzo Wrenchwhistle oversees the logging of Windshear Crag from its platform.", "Cragpool_Lake" },
  ["greatwood vale"] = { "Wooded foothills hold this valley, and sparse evergreens grow here even where the mountains further south are less forested. The Webwinder Path leads into it.\n\nThe Grimtotem who fell upon Camp Aparaje are in the vale now, their ruffians and mercenaries spread through the trees.", "Greatwood_Vale" },
  ["grimtotem post"] = { "Grimtotem warriors of many kinds, from brutes and ruffians to sorcerers and mercenaries, patrol the post and the slopes around it. It is the base of the Grimtotem tribe's operations in the Stonetalon Mountains. The fierce chieftain Grundig Darkcloud leads them.", "Grimtotem_Post" },
  ["malakajin"] = { "Darkspear trolls live in Malaka'jin, a small Horde village just south of Grimtotem Post. The hunters make a modest living selling their catches to Horde travelers who pass through.", "Malaka'jin" },
  ["mirkfallon lake"] = { "Mirkfallon Lake fills a basin at the center of the Stonetalon Mountains, with a hill rising out of the water in the middle. Wyverns nest around the lake, and slimes and thunder lizards roam its shores.", "Mirkfallon_Lake" },
  ["sishir canyon"] = { "Deepmoss spiders have taken Sishir Canyon entirely. Their eggs and webbing cover the ledges and hollows of the stone, and deep inside waits Besseleth, the most powerful of the brood.", "Sishir_Canyon" },
  ["stonetalon peak"] = { "At the far northwestern end of the range, in a green mountain valley, stands Stonetalon Peak. The mountain is held sacred, and magic runs through its stone.\n\nDuring the Third War, Jaina Proudmoore and her expedition made a base near the entrance to the caverns beneath the peak. Thrall and his Horde came later, searching for the Oracle, and in those caverns the Alliance and the Horde stood together against the Burning Legion for a time.\n\nNow the night elves and the Alliance keep the peak, and the druids of the Talon Den tend the valley.", "Stonetalon_Peak" },
  ["sun rock retreat"] = { "In ages past, tauren made pilgrimages to Sun Rock Retreat to perform the rites that cross into the spirit world, for the place has a strong bond with that realm. It is said that An'she himself once kissed the mountain where the retreat now stands.\n\nOrcs and tauren hold it together now, hidden among the peaks south of Mirkfallon Lake. It is the busiest settlement in the mountains, and its people work to repair what the Venture Company and other malignant forces have torn from the land.", "Sun_Rock_Retreat" },
  ["talon den"] = { "On Stonetalon Peak the earth opens into the Talon Den, a barrow den. Mirkfallon dryads and keepers make their home there, together with night elf Cenarion Caretakers.", "Talon_Den" },
  ["talondeep path"] = { "The Talondeep Path winds through the mountains from Ashenvale to Windshear Crag. Small dead ends and odd holes break its walls, and nothing lives inside.", "Talondeep_Path" },
  ["webwinder path"] = { "Spiders, rams and thunder lizards live along the Webwinder Path, the main road between Malaka'jin and Mirkfallon Lake.", "Webwinder_Path" },
  ["windshear crag"] = { "Windshear Crag was a massive old-growth forest, green and thick in its valley. Now it is stumps, fouled water, and machinery that creaks without end. The goblin-run Venture Company logged it, and the whole operation answers to a leper gnome, Gerenzo Wrenchwhistle. His flesh looks to be rotting away, and he has grafted metal and gears onto his body. He built the Super Reaper 6000, a machine that can turn the largest trees to sawdust, and he watches the crag from a platform above Cragpool Lake.", "Windshear_Crag" },
  ["windshear mine"] = { "Windshear kobolds occupy Windshear Mine, a tin mine at the end of the Blackwolf River in the southeast corner of Windshear Crag. They dig its tin for the Venture Company.", "Windshear_Mine" },
}
areas[1443] = {
  ["bolgans hole"] = { "Khan Shaka and the most prominent members of the Gelkis clan live in this cave. Blue crystals grow inside it, called the tears of Theradras. The clan's shaman hoard them and believe the stones carry the protection of Princess Theradras.", "Bolgan's_Hole" },
  ["ethel rethor"] = { "Far in the northwest of Desolace, a night elf ruin lies mostly underwater. Only a lone tower rises above the surface, joined to the shore by a small bridge that ends at a closed entrance. Naga, who inhabit many of the coastal elven ruins, have taken this one as well.\n\nAzore Aldamort, who speaks for the Argent Dawn, stays among the ruins.", "Ethel_Rethor" },
  ["gelkis village"] = { "The Gelkis clan keeps its principal village in a ring around a wide ravine in southwestern Desolace. Bridges join the cliffs and bluffs inside the ravine and lead to a cave in the southern mountain face, where the Gelkis khan lives.\n\nThe village sits hard against the main north-south road, which makes the road dangerous for anyone who travels it. Lately the centaur have spread, building huts on the east side of the road, near the lake.", "Gelkis_Village" },
  ["ghost walker post"] = { "Gurda Wildmane leads the tauren shaman who keep this small outpost beside the Kodo Graveyard. The shaman ease the nearby kodo into their next lives and sometimes speak with the spirits of the dead ones. A band of orcs sent by Warchief Thrall holds the post with them.\n\nThe outpost is exposed and bare. It has no inn and little beyond the necessities. From here the Horde wages war on the Burning Blade, its servants and the centaur tribes.", "Ghost_Walker_Post" },
  ["kodo graveyard"] = { "Here lies the final resting place of the kodo. Their bones are scattered across the ground, and many seem to come from beasts far larger than the kodo that roam the land now. Dying kodo still make their way here to end their days, and vultures called carrion horrors gather over the bones.", "Kodo_Graveyard" },
  ["kolkar village"] = { "The Kolkar clan holds the eastern margin of Desolace, where its ancestral grounds lie along the mountains. This village is the center of its power. Here the Kolkar are the smallest and least influential of the tribes, yet no other centaur clan has spread as far across Kalimdor. They keep villages and outposts in the Barrens and in Durotar.", "Kolkar_Village" },
  ["kormeks hut"] = { "A small hovel stands on the barren flats of north-central Desolace. Inside lives Willow, a friendly member of the Twilight's Hammer. In the pen outside, kodo bones lie scattered, and Bibbly F'utzbuckle dances among them, excited at the money the bones might fetch. No one knows where Kormek, the hut's namesake, has gone.", "Kormek's_Hut" },
  ["magram village"] = { "The Magram clan lives on the shores and islands of a chain of polluted yellow pools in southeastern Desolace. Crude bridges join the islands to each other and to the shore, and the clan's yurts and pavilions stand scattered among the pools. The Magram are at war with the Gelkis clan.", "Magram_Village" },
  ["mannoroc coven"] = { "The night elf ruins here take their name from Mannoroth, the dreaded pit lord. The earth was dead already, and the demons that live here have blasted it further with fel magic. It hangs thick in the air, and a deadly pall falls over anyone who crosses into the coven.\n\nBurning Blade cultists summoned these demons through a number of magical portals. Infernals, succubi, felhounds and felguards now stalk the ruins, and the orc warlock Jugkar Grim'rod stands here for the Burning Blade. It is the largest gathering of demons in central Kalimdor, and new portals keep opening.", "Mannoroc_Coven" },
  ["nijels point"] = { "The Alliance and the Cenarion Circle hold an outpost among night elf ruins in the northern mountains. No one there can say who Nijel was.\n\nKeeper Marandis and his dryad sisters use the outpost as a base, on a mission for the Cenarion Circle: to kill Princess Theradras and to bring the body of Zaetar out of Maraudon and home to the Stonetalon Mountains. The Stormwind Army keeps a guard here under Captain Pentigast, against the centaur clans. He is sure that whichever of the Gelkis and the Magram wins their war will grow bold enough to threaten the Alliance holdings, and he wants to learn how the centaur can be beaten.", "Nijel's_Point" },
  ["ranazjar isle"] = { "Naga live on this small, rocky island off the northwest coast of Desolace. Night elf ruins stand among the stones, and they seem to belong to the same ancient city as Ethel Rethor. A statue of Queen Azshara is there too.", "Ranazjar_Isle" },
  ["sargeron"] = { "Satyrs of the Hatefury have taken over the ruins of a night elf city. The city has lain broken since the War of the Ancients, and its first name is lost. The satyrs renamed it Sargeron, after their demon master Sargeras.", "Sargeron" },
  ["sartheris strand"] = { "The coast is the one part of Desolace with color. The interior is gray and gloomy, but here the grass is green and the sun comes out. Sea giants and naga crowd the water, and makrura share the beach. A small boat lies smashed on the sand below Ethel Rethor.", "Sar'theris_Strand" },
  ["scrabblescrews camp"] = { "Smeed Scrabblescrew lives in a small goblin camp not far from the Kodo Graveyard. He is an enterprising goblin who keeps a caravan of kodo.", "Scrabblescrew's_Camp" },
  ["shadowbreak ravine"] = { "The Burning Blade cultists have made a well-hidden haven of this ravine in the mountains. Huge mushrooms grow among its rocks. A summoning circle hung with giant chains lies in the middle of it.\n\nCaptured stallions are held here, and the cult's nightmares stand among them.", "Shadowbreak_Ravine" },
  ["shadowprey village"] = { "Darkspear trolls built this fishing village at the southern end of Desolace's coast, and they live by what the sea gives them. Their catches are always in demand, and they feed the tauren outpost inland.\n\nSelendra, a dryad of the Cenarion Circle, stays nearby, searching for the remains of Zaetar. The inn is airy, and guests wake to a view of the water.", "Shadowprey_Village" },
  ["tethris aran"] = { "North of Thunder Axe Fortress lie the ruins of a night elf settlement, presumably destroyed in the Great Sundering. Only the wild creatures of Desolace live among the broken stones.", "Tethris_Aran" },
  ["thunder axe fortress"] = { "The Burning Blade cult holds this stronghold in northwestern Desolace, and its reavers, felsworn, augurs, adepts and shadowmages crowd the walls. Inside them stand a barracks, a cave and several watch towers.", "Thunder_Axe_Fortress" },
  ["valley of bones"] = { "Skeletons crowd this crag on the southern border of Desolace, the way on foot into Shen'dralas, and centaur necromancers walk among them. Two enormous skeletons lie collapsed against the cliff walls, the Dead Goliaths, and no one knows where they came from.", "Valley_of_Bones" },
  ["valley of spears"] = { "The Maraudine clan of centaur lives in this valley in western Desolace. On foot it can be entered only through narrow mountain passes to the south and the east. At its southwestern end lies the entrance to Maraudon, the foul place where the centaur race was born.", "Valley_of_Spears" },
}
areas[1444] = {
  ["camp mojache"] = { "The tauren of Camp Mojache have built their village above Wildwind Lake, straddling a swift stream that runs down into it. They are known for their thick hides, and their braves guard travelers against the dangers of Feralas with great courage.\n\nWoodpaw gnolls raid the camp, and their attacks have grown more fevered of late.", "Camp_Mojache" },
  ["dire maul"] = { "Twelve thousand years ago a covert sect of night elf sorcerers raised Eldre'Thalas to guard Queen Azshara's most prized arcane secrets. Among its Highborne were the arcanists of the Shen'dralar.\n\nThe Great Sundering cut the Highborne off from the Well of Eternity. When Prince Tortheldrin set out to bind a demon, Immol'thar, the Highborne who warned that it would give the Burning Legion a foothold again died in a single bloody night. Other Shen'dralar rose in secret under a masked leader called the Shal'nan. After a last raid on Tortheldrin's sanctum, the Shal'nan led his people west out of the city, and they sailed into the unknown. Those who stayed lived on the demon's power for thousands of years, until holding him cost more than he gave. Tortheldrin then killed many of his own people so the rest could draw more from him, and the survivors drew back into the heart of the city, leaving the rest to whatever came.\n\nOgres, satyrs and ghosts hold the ruins now, and the stone still stands.", "Dire_Maul" },
  ["dream bough"] = { "The largest tree in Feralas stands on a small island in the middle of Jademir Lake. It is one of the four Great Trees, and its roots and branches hold a portal into the Emerald Dream. The green dragonflight guards it.\n\nDragons of the green flight corrupted by the Emerald Nightmare have come into Azeroth through the Great Trees, and they appear here at times.", "Dream_Bough" },
  ["feathermoon stronghold"] = { "General Shandris Feathermoon raised this fortress on Sardor Isle, off the western coast of Feralas, and it carries her name. For ten thousand years she has stood at the right hand of Tyrande Whisperwind among the Sentinels. The stronghold holds this stretch of Kalimdor's coast for the night elves still.\n\nThe naga trouble the island, and Shandris works to cleanse the land of the Hatecrest and their leader, Lord Shalzaru. Only the sea joins the island to the mainland, and a boat crosses to its dock from the coast.", "Feathermoon_Stronghold_(Classic)" },
  ["feral scar vale"] = { "The Feral Scar yetis hold this vale in the High Wilderness of western Feralas, and it carries their name.", "Feral_Scar_Vale" },
  ["forgotten coast"] = { "Water elementals rise out of the surf along the Forgotten Coast, the whole western edge of Feralas where the land meets the Veiled Sea, and sea giants roam the shore.", "Forgotten_Coast" },
  ["frayfeather highlands"] = { "The Frayfeather hippogryphs hold these highlands and give them their name. Deep among the crags their nests lie hidden, each with its eggs.", "Frayfeather_Highlands" },
  ["gordunni outpost"] = { "In the mountains north of Lariss Pavilion, the Gordunni ogres have taken the land, and the plant life that is green everywhere else in Feralas has withered to brown. Two caves open in the hills, a small one near the entrance and an ogre mound at the back. Gordunni brutes and ogre mages roam the slopes.", "Gordunni_Outpost" },
  ["grimtotem compound"] = { "East of Dire Maul, the Grimtotem tauren have built a large village in two parts. In the southern part a big cage holds sprite darters prisoner. East of the northern part, gnolls have set up a few camps of their own.", "Grimtotem_Compound" },
  ["high wilderness"] = { "Ancient trees fill the High Wilderness, a vast forest stretching across western Feralas from the Forgotten Coast to the Verdantis River. Bears, wolves and hippogryphs roam among them, and treants, yetis and ogres share the forest with them.", "High_Wilderness" },
  ["isle of dread"] = { "The Isle of Dread lies off the coast of Feralas, south of Sardor Isle. Its middle is a valley walled in by unyielding cliffs, and the only way in is from the southeast. The chimaerok live in that valley, among them arcane-touched beasts and devourers, led by Lord Lakmaeran.\n\nAt the northern end of the isle a cave sinks deep into the rock, unlike anything else there. The Hatecrest naga hold it under Lord Shalzaru.", "Isle_of_Dread" },
  ["jademir lake"] = { "Jademir Lake lies in northern Feralas, with a small island at its center. A portal into the Emerald Dream stands there, and Lethlas and a number of green dragonspawn guard it.", "Jademir_Lake" },
  ["lariss pavilion"] = { "Azj'Tordin, a Highborne who disagreed with Prince Tortheldrin, lives here. He left Dire Maul by a back way that comes out at the bottom of Lariss Pavilion.\n\nThe pavilion is a large open building in the night elf style, just north of Camp Mojache.", "Lariss_Pavilion" },
  ["lower wilds"] = { "The Lower Wilds is the eastern end of Feralas, where the forest leads on into Thousand Needles. Ironfur bears and Longtooth wolves roam among the trees, and now and then a treant wanders through.", "Lower_Wilds" },
  ["oneiros"] = { "The ruins of Oneiros lie south of Jademir Lake in northern Feralas. Many night elf ruins stand here, and green dragonspawn patrol them.", "Oneiros" },
  ["rage scar hold"] = { "The fiercest yetis in the area live in Rage Scar Hold, a cave dug into the ground. Books and strange symbols lie scattered inside. Either the yetis are studying magic, or the books belonged to some unlucky magus who met his end here.", "Rage_Scar_Hold" },
  ["ruins of isildien"] = { "Ten thousand years ago a great night elf city in southwestern Feralas was destroyed. The Gordunni ogres have made their home in its ruins, ruling over stones raised by hands far older than their own. A cave opens on the eastern side, and at the top of a stair stands a large round building that may once have been a temple of Elune, since it looks much like the Temple of the Moon in Darnassus.", "Ruins_of_Isildien" },
  ["ruins of ravenwind"] = { "Northern Feralas holds the broken stones of Ravenwind, raised long ago by the Highborne of the night elves, much as Dire Maul was raised elsewhere. Little of that glory remains standing now. Northspring harpies have taken the ruins for their own, nesting among the fallen columns and empty halls.", "Ruins_of_Ravenwind" },
  ["ruins of solarsal"] = { "Southwest of Feathermoon Stronghold, on Sardor Isle, lie old night elf ruins, long since abandoned. Hatecrest naga have moved in among the broken columns in such numbers that the night elves of the stronghold take it for the first move of an attack.", "Ruins_of_Solarsal" },
  ["sardor isle"] = { "Sardor Isle lies off the west coast of Feralas, north of the Isle of Dread. The night elves hold it as a small refuge, from Feathermoon Stronghold under General Shandris Feathermoon. Naga live along its shores and a pack of wolves ranges through what wild places remain, so the stronghold's watch is never idle.", "Sardor_Isle" },
  ["shalzarus lair"] = { "Lord Shalzaru, master of the Hatecrest, lives deep inside this twisting cave at the northern end of the Isle of Dread. It has a main cave and a smaller one joined by a waterway, and a hidden passage under the water leads to an inner chamber where Lady Szallah lives. Hatecrest sorceresses and serpent guards fill its length.", "Shalzaru's_Lair" },
  ["thalanaar"] = { "Where Feralas meets Thousand Needles stands Thalanaar, a tiny night elf camp held by the Alliance. Falfindel Waywarder is here, and he fights the Death's Head cult.", "Thalanaar" },
  ["twin colossals"] = { "Two sheer mountains, the Twin Colossals, rise on the western edge of Feralas, higher than anything else in the region. The road to Desolace runs through the gap between them, and the pair are among the most striking sights in the land.\n\nOn top of the eastern peak lies a small, deep pond. From the summit the whole region opens out below.", "Twin_Colossals" },
  ["verdantis river"] = { "The Verdantis River winds through central Feralas, southeast of the ruins of Dire Maul, cutting a quiet green thread through the tangled forest. Its waters hold Feralas ahi, found nowhere else in the world.", "Verdantis_River" },
  ["wildwind lake"] = { "Wildwind Lake lies beside Camp Mojache and is fed by the swift stream that runs through the tauren village.", "Wildwind_Lake" },
  ["woodpaw den"] = { "The Woodpaw Alphas, the leaders of the Woodpaw gnolls, crowd this den in Feralas, with mystics and reavers of the clan among them.", "Woodpaw_Den" },
  ["woodpaw hills"] = { "The gnolls of the Woodpaw clan make their home in these forested hills in southeastern Feralas. In the far southeast the ground has split open, in the place now called the Writhing Deep, and silithid have burst up from it.\n\nThe Woodpaw Alphas who lead the tribe walk the camps as well as the Woodpaw Den.", "Woodpaw_Hills" },
  ["writhing deep"] = { "Hive Zukk'ash lies in the southeastern corner of Feralas, where silithid swarm up out of the ground. Its workers, tunnelers, stingers and wasps fill the hive, and a silithid wasp called Qirot flies among them.", "Writhing_Deep" },
}
areas[1445] = {
  ["alcaz island"] = { "Tropical green covers Alcaz Island, off the northeast coast of Dustwallow Marsh, unlike anything on the mainland. The Strashaz naga hold its shores, myrmidons and sirens thick among them, and hydras prowl the water around it.\n\nA flying machine has landed on the island, and the minions of its mad inventor keep watch around it.", "Alcaz_Island" },
  ["beezils wreck"] = { "A goblin zeppelin bound south with supplies went down in the deepest reaches of Dustwallow Marsh, east of Tabetha's Farm. Its hull lies broken in the mud, cargo scattered among the wreckage.", "Beezil's_Wreck" },
  ["bloodfen burrow"] = { "Bloodfen raptors nest in great numbers in a cave in the south-west of Dustwallow Marsh. Outside the entrance lie the remains of some settlement, torn apart by the ravenous raptors.", "Bloodfen_Burrow" },
  ["bluefen"] = { "Bluefen is low marshland in the north of Dustwallow Marsh, near Brackenwall Village. Spiders, marsh birds, raptors and crocolisks overrun it, and Theramore infiltrators have been seen among them.", "Bluefen" },
  ["brackenwall village"] = { "The Stonemaul ogres came to Brackenwall as refugees. Black dragons fell on their old village in the south and ruined it, and Overlord Mok'Morokk led the survivors north to this cluster of a few huts and an ogre mound. No black dragon lives here, and Mok'Morokk is content to leave it that way.\n\nOthers will not. Tharg and Draz'Zilb refuse to call Brackenwall home and want the old village back.", "Brackenwall_Village" },
  ["darkmist cavern"] = { "A small mine in the northwest of Dustwallow Marsh has been given over to spiders grown terrifyingly large in the dark. The marsh folk call them the Darkmist Spiders, and the cavern Darkmist in turn, though none can say which name came first.\n\nRuins of small houses stand outside the mine, half-sunk in the wet ground. They suggest a settlement was here once.", "Darkmist_Cavern" },
  ["den of flame"] = { "The Den of Flame began as an ogre mound raised by the Stonemaul, and the stonework still bears their craft. It lies just west of the Stonemaul Ruins. Black dragonkin have overrun it, probably on the orders of Onyxia, who lives nearby, and hold it as a nest of the black dragonflight.", "Den_of_Flame" },
  ["dragonmurk"] = { "Dragon whelps and dragonspawn of the black dragonflight breed thickly in the Dragonmurk, the fetid ground of the southern marsh. The Den of Flame and the Stonemaul Ruins stand inside it, and black dragonspawn hold both.", "Dragonmurk" },
  ["dreadmurk shore"] = { "The Mirefin murlocs claim this coastline and the scattered islands along it, and they infest it. Sea turtles and other sea creatures share the water.", "Dreadmurk_Shore" },
  ["dustwallow bay"] = { "Dustwallow Bay is where the Great Sea reaches the eastern coast of the marsh, west of Theramore Isle. Salt water mixes with swamp water here and turns a wide band of the coast brackish. Makrura and turtles crowd its shallows, and young threshers swim the deeper water.", "Dustwallow_Bay" },
  ["emberstrifes den"] = { "A wide cave opens into the reeking Wyrmbog, home to the black drake Emberstrife. He puts young black dragonlings through vigorous tests, and only those he finds worthy go on to Onyxia's Lair.", "Emberstrife's_Den" },
  ["foothold citadel"] = { "At the doors of Foothold Citadel, the guards of Theramore drill against practice dummies and young recruits spar with each other. Priests watch close by, ready to call on the Light the instant anyone is hurt. The keep is the headquarters of the Theramore Guard.\n\nGrand Admiral Daelin Proudmoore died in battle inside it. He had taken Theramore, and Warchief Thrall asked Lady Jaina Proudmoore to let the Horde in so that they could kill her father. Thrall's forces stormed the city, led by Rexxar and his companions, and Rexxar faced the admiral himself in his keep.", "Foothold_Citadel" },
  ["lost point"] = { "Theramore built Lost Point as a scout tower in the west of the marsh. It is run-down and decrepit now, and no longer serves its builders. Theramore deserters live in it under their leader, Balos Jacken.", "Lost_Point" },
  ["north point tower"] = { "A sizable contingent of Theramore guards holds North Point Tower beside a well-traveled stretch of road in the north of the marsh. Since deserters took over Lost Point, it is the most distant of Theramore's outposts.", "North_Point_Tower" },
  ["onyxias lair"] = { "The entrance to Onyxia's Lair is carved to look like the maw of the broodmother herself. Inside, the path is lined with magma, and the ground is cracked and glowing with fire from below. The stone ceiling groans and shifts, and rock falls without end. Lava spills from the walls where the heat cannot be held.\n\nOnyxia built it, and her kin, the black dragonflight, guard her here. She is the daughter of Deathwing and the sister of Nefarian, lord of Blackrock Spire. Where her father rules by force and her brother breeds twisted abominations, she works through charm. She delights in meddling in the politics of mortals and bending them to her own ends.", "Onyxia's_Lair" },
  ["quagmire"] = { "The Quagmire fills the sodden heart of Dustwallow Marsh, reaching from Lost Point in the west to Dustwallow Bay in the east. Crocolisks lurk in its black water, spiders and oozes crawl through it, and Grimtotem tauren have made their home here.", "Quagmire" },
  ["sentry point"] = { "A scout tower built by Theramore stands just across the bridge from the city, watching the comings and goings on the road.", "Sentry_Point" },
  ["shady rest inn"] = { "The Shady Rest Inn, a human-built waystop near the border of Dustwallow Marsh and the Barrens, burned to the ground without explanation. James Hyal ran the inn, and his wife and child died in the blaze. Little of it still stands but a crumbling chimney, and the blackened ruin is still smoking.", "Shady_Rest_Inn" },
  ["stonemaul ruins"] = { "Kor'gall seized the Stonemaul clan with an ancient orcish axe, Serathil, and ruled its village by cruelty. Crol'ka, whose brothers Kor'gall had killed, barely escaped with his life. A Darkspear patrol found him, and Vol'jin and Rexxar agreed to help, hoping to win the Stonemaul for the Horde's war against Kul Tiras.\n\nBarred at the gates, Rexxar fought his way in. Kor'gall let him join the clan if he survived the Gauntlet, a valley full of deadly beasts, and Rexxar did. When Kor'gall then refused to lend the clan to the war, Rexxar challenged him for the leadership and killed him, and the Stonemaul went to war under Rexxar. Afterward he left the clan to Mok'Morokk, who took from his people instead of serving them.\n\nBlack dragons later raided the village and claimed it. Mok'Morokk led the survivors away, and Firemane dragonspawn infest the ruins now.", "Stonemaul_Ruins" },
  ["swamplight manor"] = { "\"Swamp Eye\" Jarl, a famous cook, lives in Swamplight Manor, a modest house on Witch Hill with a small garden outside. He favors good swamp cooking, fried green frog legs and frog leg stew, and he counts the eyes of Darkmist spiders a delicacy.", "Swamplight_Manor" },
  ["theramore isle"] = { "During the Third War, Lady Jaina Proudmoore led the survivors of Lordaeron across the sea to Kalimdor. After the Burning Legion's defeat at Mount Hyjal, they raised Theramore on a rocky island off Dustwallow Marsh, a walled port meant to safeguard the ragged remnants of the failing Alliance. A rectangular wall studded with cannon towers rings most of the isle. Only the harbor is open, and two scout towers guard it. At the center, behind an inner wall, rises Jaina's Tower, the tallest building in the city. Its many stories are linked by long winding stairs, and its walls are warded against attack. It holds her private rooms, her library and the small throne room.\n\nThe marsh gives poor timber, and the city's shipwrights call the local trees marshy garbage. They envy the orcs, who cut good oaks above Orgrimmar. Even so, humans trade fish to the orcs for surplus Durotar crops. Most people know each other, and crime is rare.", "Theramore_Isle" },
  ["tidefury cove"] = { "Treacherous water lies at the end of Dustwallow Bay, south of Theramore Isle. Small islands and hidden reefs dot the cove, and two ships lie broken on its shallows, given over now to roaming makrura. The famous fisherman Nat Pagle makes his home on one of the islets.", "Tidefury_Cove" },
  ["witch hill"] = { "Mirefin murloc camps stand in the mire of Witch Hill, and crocolisks, spiders and raptors hunt across it. Swamplight Manor sits near its center, and \"Swamp Eye\" Jarl lives there.", "Witch_Hill" },
  ["wyrmbog"] = { "South of the Dragonmurk lies the Wyrmbog. Black dragons make their home here, Onyxia, the daughter of Deathwing, and Emberstrife among them, and the entrances to Emberstrife's Den and Onyxia's Lair lie within the bog.", "Wyrmbog" },
}
areas[1446] = {
  ["abyssal sands"] = { "The sands here lie within Tanaris, a desert reaching in every direction.", "Abyssal_Sands" },
  ["broken pillar"] = { "The goblin scientist Marvon Rivetseeker has made his camp among a small group of troll ruins south of Gadgetzan. His home is in Ratchet, but after his zeppelin crashed here he decided to start the research he had come to do among these stones.", "Broken_Pillar" },
  ["caverns of time"] = { "The bronze dragonflight has guarded the Caverns of Time since the world was young. The titans gave them the task, and they keep it still. Beneath the eastern sands the caverns wind like a labyrinth, and the paths through them follow the shifting timeways. Mortals are kept out, because their meddling could upset the flow of time.\n\nNozdormu, the Aspect of Time, makes his home here. The dragon Anachronos sits outside the gate. It was here that he first learned how great a threat the silithid were, with their hives spreading through the remote parts of the desert.", "Caverns_of_Time" },
  ["dunemaul compound"] = { "The Dunemaul are nomadic ogres who have taken over a wide stretch of Tanaris, and this mound is their compound. Brutes and enforcers hold it alongside ogre mages and warlocks. Gor'marok the Ravager leads them.", "Dunemaul_Compound" },
  ["eastmoon ruins"] = { "Dunemaul ogres have taken over the Eastmoon Ruins, the crumbling stonework of an ancient troll settlement just west of the Gaping Chasm. The trolls are long gone from it.", "Eastmoon_Ruins" },
  ["gadgetzan"] = { "Profit above loyalty is the creed of Gadgetzan, capital of the Steamwheedle Cartel, the largest of the goblin cartels. That makes the city neutral ground in the quarrel between the Horde and the Alliance. Both governments recognize this, and goblin bruisers patrol the streets, ready to pound to a pulp anyone who brings war into a place built on trade. Wallets, not banners, decide who is welcome.\n\nSome of the best goblin engineers, miners and alchemists work here. In the auction house humans trade kittens to the Horde and tauren trade prairie dogs to the Alliance, and some say the pet trade may bring the two sides to terms.", "Gadgetzan" },
  ["gaping chasm"] = { "Hazzali silithid swarm through the Gaping Chasm, and several large hive complexes lie within it. It is one of the minor hives in southeastern Tanaris, just east of the Eastmoon Ruins. Workers and tunnelers, stingers, wasps, swarmers and sandreavers fill it, and a silithid reaver called Haarka the Ravenous hunts among them.\n\nSenior Surveyor Fizzledowser of the Gadgetzan Water Company once sent his junior, Scrimshank, down to survey it. Nothing has been heard from him since.", "Gaping_Chasm" },
  ["lands end beach"] = { "Land's End Beach lies at the southernmost point of Tanaris, in the far southeastern corner of Kalimdor, one of the remotest places in the world. Two islands lie off the shore. Surf gliders roam the eastern stretch of sand, and a mermaid named Meridith lives in the water off the eastern shore.", "Land's_End_Beach" },
  ["lost rigger cove"] = { "Mountains close in on three sides of Lost Rigger Cove, which hides the port of the Southsea Pirates on the Tanaris coast. A shipyard here is busy building a new vessel.", "Lost_Rigger_Cove" },
  ["noonshade ruins"] = { "Wastewander bandits have overrun the Noonshade Ruins, worn stone left behind by trolls long gone, east of Gadgetzan in northeastern Tanaris.", "Noonshade_Ruins" },
  ["noxious lair"] = { "The ground in southwestern Tanaris sinks a little here, overlooking Un'Goro Crater, as if something broad and shallow, a river or a lake, once lay across it. The Centipaar silithid hold it now, and they have drained the water pools that used to lie here. Large claws stand twitching in the sand, seemingly from huge silithid buried beneath, and the soil around each is drenched in silithid goo, the only moisture left in a place that used to hold a lake.\n\nBelow the surface the hive is humid, warm and eerie. Tunnels join its two entrances to the breeding rooms, and the walls are coated with silithid acid and hung with unborn insects.", "Noxious_Lair" },
  ["sandsorrow watch"] = { "From a chain of watchtowers called Sandsorrow Watch, the Sandfury trolls keep an eye on their holdings across a wide area west of Gadgetzan. They have had to give up most of Tanaris to outsiders over the years, and they guard what is left fiercely. The Sandfury need every troll to pitch in just to get by, so they learned to raise the dead with voodoo, and a troll can go on working even after death.", "Sandsorrow_Watch" },
  ["south seas"] = { "During the Second War the Stormreaver clan captured giant sea turtles in these waters for the Horde to use as submarines, and daring goblins of the Steamwheedle Cartel steered them against enemy ships. The South Seas are the stretch of the Great Sea that washes the southern shores of the Eastern Kingdoms and Kalimdor. Zandalar and Tel'Abim rise among its islands. The flesh-eating bloodwing bat rules the night sky over the misty jungle islands, and giant squids live in the deep.", "South_Seas" },
  ["southbreak shore"] = { "A flat stretch of beach runs along the southern edge of Tanaris, south of Lost Rigger Cove, quiet and almost deserted. Turtles and sea giants roam the surf. Just off the coast, on a small island, lives a lone gnome named Winkey.", "Southbreak_Shore" },
  ["southmoon ruins"] = { "Old troll ruins lie in southern Tanaris, just north of the Valley of the Watchers, their stones weathered and half-buried in the desert sand. Dunemaul ogres hold them.", "Southmoon_Ruins" },
  ["steamwheedle port"] = { "Steamwheedle Port sits on the eastern coast of Tanaris, with a road running west to Gadgetzan, its mother city. A dock reaches out toward the outer islands, and the goblins keep their ships to themselves. The port belongs to the Steamwheedle Cartel, who deal with anyone so long as the gold is good.\n\nAmong its residents is Yeh'kinya, who is trying to learn more about the essence of an ancient god.", "Steamwheedle_Port" },
  ["thistleshrub valley"] = { "Giant cacti stand thick in Thistleshrub Valley, strange for such barren desert to keep alive. Desert-dwelling thistleshrubs crowd the valley too.", "Thistleshrub_Valley" },
  ["uldum"] = { "The titans left a holding in the southern part of the desert, the ancient city of Uldum, rich in their lore. It is a research facility, where they work to improve the living world of Azeroth.", "Uldum" },
  ["valley of the watchers"] = { "A great gate stands in the Valley of the Watchers, impenetrable against Uldum behind it. Mountain giants called Dune Smashers roam the valley.", "Valley_of_the_Watchers" },
  ["waterspring field"] = { "Water towers rise across Waterspring Field, pumping water up from reserves deep underground. The goblins of Gadgetzan built them to supply their desert operations. Then the Wastewanders arrived, a nomadic gang of bandits, and claimed the field and the towers for themselves.\n\nThey squat on the land under their leader, Caliph Scorpidsting, hoping it will be kinder than the ogre camp they once lived in. They still shiver at the memory. The Gadgetzan Water Company wants its towers back, and it has put a high bounty on the Caliph's head for murdering its workers and seizing its property.", "Waterspring_Field" },
  ["wavestrider beach"] = { "Two ships burn off the coast of Wavestrider Beach, and they seem to be fighting each other. The beach runs along the eastern shore of Tanaris, near Steamwheedle Port. Steeljaw snappers crawl across the sand, and Wastewander bandits roam the beach.", "Wavestrider_Beach" },
  ["zalashjis den"] = { "A naga named Zalashji makes his home in this small cave in eastern Tanaris.", "Zalashji's_Den" },
  ["zulfarrak"] = { "Chief Ukorz Sandscalp rules Zul'Farrak, the Sandfury city in northwestern Tanaris. The Sandfury are known for their particular ruthlessness and dark mysticism. Ukorz was raised on stories of a time when trolls lorded over these lands with no outsiders in the way, and he wants that time back. The Sandfury are raising an army of undead trolls to win it.\n\nZul'Farrak was once the shining jewel of Tanaris, and its trolls have had to give up much of their territory since. Troll legends tell of Sul'thraze the Lasher, a sword that fills even the strongest foe with fear and weakness. It was split in half long ago, and rumor puts both halves inside these sun-blasted walls. Others say a demigod sleeps in a sacred pool at the city's heart.", "Zul'Farrak" },
}
areas[1447] = {
  ["bay of storms"] = { "The Bay of Storms is a stretch of the Great Sea off the eastern coast of Azshara, and travelers know it for the storms that gather over it. When the world broke, much of the night elf city of Eldarath sank into its water, and the drowned stones still lie below the surface.\n\nIslands in the bay have residents of their own. Loramus Thalipedes, a demon hunter, watches the horizon from his island refuge with his guardians beside him. On a small island far to the east, Duke Hydraxis of the Waterlords keeps his honor guard, and his war with the other elemental lords still rages.", "Bay_of_Storms" },
  ["bears head"] = { "Furbolg villages dot the land at Bear's Head, small outposts of the Timbermaw tribe that serve the larger Timbermaw settlements to the north. The place takes its name from them.\n\nOn a cliff above them stands the tower of Archmage Xylem, who lives there with several apprentices and associates.", "Bear's_Head" },
  ["bitter reaches"] = { "The northeast of Azshara is called the Bitter Reaches. Hippogryphs, oozes and chimaerae fill the land. Along its northern edge mountain giants have driven off most of the other wildlife and hold the ground for themselves.", "Bitter_Reaches" },
  ["forlorn ridge"] = { "A mountain called Forlorn Ridge rises over southwestern Azshara and commands the land around it. Both the Alliance and the Horde have built fortifications on its slopes.", "Forlorn_Ridge" },
  ["haldarr encampment"] = { "The Haldarr clan of satyrs has made its camp in western Azshara, northeast of Shadowsong Shrine and southwest of Valormok. The encampment is thick with vicious satyrs, a corrupted host of goat-legged fiends.", "Haldarr_Encampment" },
  ["hetaeras clutch"] = { "Beneath the waters of the Bay of Storms lies Hetaera's Clutch, a large temple of Elune now home to hydras, the children of Hetaera. Hetaera herself swims the sea just north of it, sometimes resting on a tiny island there, and calls herself the ruler of these waters. Sons of the sea giant Lord Arkkoroc patrol here too. She has killed many of them, and Arkkoroc names her a false god.", "Hetaera's_Clutch" },
  ["jagged reef"] = { "Jets of stone break the sandy beach of Azshara's northern coast again and again, and small inlets of the sea cut into the shore. Rocks and shallows hide beneath the surface, so travel along the coast is hazardous. Murlocs, hydras and makrura hold this shore, swimming the shallows or plodding across the sand in search of food.", "Jagged_Reef" },
  ["lake mennar"] = { "The waters of Lake Mennar lie still at their center, though dragonspawn hold the ground on every side. Anyone who crosses that ring finds the lake itself free of danger, a place of quiet and refreshment inside the guarded shores.\n\nFew places in the world hold so many of the blue dragonflight, and the only one like it is Mazthoril in Winterspring.", "Lake_Mennar" },
  ["legash encampment"] = { "Legashi satyrs hold this camp in northern Azshara, east of Ursolan and north of the Thalassian Base Camp. Their rogues and hellcallers crowd it under Master Feardred, and Thunderhead hippogryphs range over the land around them.", "Legash_Encampment" },
  ["ravencrest monument"] = { "Two broken legs stand on the cliffs at the southern tip of Azshara, all that is left of an ancient night elf colossus. The rest of the figure lies scattered on the stone around them, or has slipped beneath the waves below. Who it honored is not known.\n\nThe Spitelash naga hold the ruins now.", "Ravencrest_Monument" },
  ["rethress sanctum"] = { "Rethress Sanctum, a ruined shrine on the southern coast, stands broken among the Ruined Reaches. No one keeps it now. Murlocs and makrura wander through its fallen stones, the only ones who come here at all.", "Rethress_Sanctum" },
  ["ruined reaches"] = { "The Ruined Reaches run along the southern coast of Azshara, near the border with Durotar. Cliffs and beaches break into inlets, and ruins and shipwrecks lie scattered across them. Shallows just under the water make the coast treacherous, and murlocs, hydras and makrura hold the shore.\n\nOn a hill above the western beach stands a gnomish house where Blimo and Jubie Gadgetspring live and trade. Upstairs in their bedroom is a third bed, and no one knows who, if anyone, sleeps in it.", "Ruined_Reaches" },
  ["ruins of eldarath"] = { "Eldarath was a mighty stronghold of the Highborne before the world broke. Their greatest mages carved runes and tablets setting out the rules of the arcane and their insights into it, and left them in public places for anyone to ponder. Then the Great Sundering came, and most of the city crumbled into the Bay of Storms.\n\nWhat is left clings to the eastern cliffs, a cluster of ruins the night elves have long shunned. Fierce bands of naga have claimed it, and Warlord Krellian rules them from the Temple of Zin-Malor. Many of the old tablets still stand among the wreckage.\n\nThe blue dragonflight has taken it on itself to guard the magic left in these ruins, and the blue dragon Azuregos will fight to the death to defend Azshara's magical treasures. Some say his coming means that something of great worth, perhaps the fabled Vials of Eternity, lies hidden in this land.", "Ruins_of_Eldarath" },
  ["scalebeards cave"] = { "A cave on a tiny island in the western Bay of Storms is the den of Scalebeard, a rare turtle. He is seldom at home, since he prefers to hunt far out in the Great Sea.", "Scalebeard's_Cave" },
  ["shadowsong shrine"] = { "A hill covered in ruins in western Azshara bears the name Shadowsong. It shares that name with Jarod Shadowsong, who commanded the resistance in the War of the Ancients, and with his older sister Maiev, the warden who held Illidan Stormrage captive after his betrayal at Mount Hyjal. What either has to do with the hill, no one knows.\n\nThe ghosts of the Highborne haunt the stones and roam the land around, bent on snuffing out any life that comes near. Among them walks the ghost of Varo'then, who once led Queen Azshara's personal guard.", "Shadowsong_Shrine" },
  ["shattered strand"] = { "The north shore of the Bay of Storms is crowded. Spitelash naga, hydras, sea giants, makrura and murlocs each hold a stretch of the sand, and an old temple and a tower rise from the shore among them.", "Shattered_Strand" },
  ["southfury river"] = { "The Southfury rises in a lake on Mount Hyjal and drops into the lowlands in a set of spectacular waterfalls. From there it runs wide and swift, and it is the border between the forest of Ashenvale and Azshara, and farther south between the Barrens and Durotar. Near its mouth it tumbles over a last cascade into the Great Sea, northeast of the goblin port of Ratchet.", "Southfury_River" },
  ["southridge beach"] = { "Southridge Beach lines the southern edge of the Bay of Storms, its sands strewn with ruins of the once-great city of Eldarath. Naga now hold the shore, sharing it with makrura, hydras, murlocs, and the occasional sea giant risen from deeper water.", "Southridge_Beach" },
  ["storm cliffs"] = { "The Spitelash naga have made their home among the rock of the Storm Cliffs. At the far eastern edge of the cliffs stands the Ravencrest Monument.", "Storm_Cliffs" },
  ["talrendis point"] = { "Night elves keep a small encampment at Talrendis Point, in southwestern Azshara on the border with Ashenvale. It began as a watch kept at the edge of the forest.\n\nA tauren, Loh'atu, lives among them. The Sentinels let him stay so long as he makes no trouble, and he says he has come to look into disturbances to the land.", "Talrendis_Point" },
  ["temple of arkkoran"] = { "This temple on the northeastern shore of Azshara once belonged to the worship of Elune. Now Lord Arkkoroc, a sea giant revered as a demigod, lives at the top. Murlocs and makrura guard the temple and attack on sight, and his sons patrol around it. Hydras and fierce sea giants watch the shore beyond.", "Temple_of_Arkkoran" },
  ["temple of zin malor"] = { "Among the stones of Eldarath stands a temple to Elune, raised by night elves. Its design is strikingly close to the Temple of Arkkoran and to the Temple of the Moon in Darnassus. The Spitelash naga hold it now, and their cruel leader, Warlord Krellian, sits in the place of honor.", "Temple_of_Zin-Malor" },
  ["thalassian base camp"] = { "Blood elves hold the hill above the Legash camps, a scatter of small fortified camps on its slopes. Their expedition searches Azshara for Highborne relics under Magus Rimtori. She promised the Horde a share of the riches she found in return for warriors to guard her, then turned on them. Rimtori spends most of her time searching Azshara with her bodyguards, and Magister Hawkhelm watches over the camp.", "Thalassian_Base_Camp" },
  ["tower of eldara"] = { "The Tower of Eldara stands on the far northeastern tip of land above the Bay of Storms, its sandy shore patrolled by murlocs, makrura, and hydras. It once belonged to the night elf civilization that spread across this coast, but the tower stands closed and abandoned now.", "Tower_of_Eldara" },
  ["ursolan"] = { "The furbolgs revere Ursol, a bear demigod, and Ursolan takes its name from him. The area is now a large Timbermaw furbolg settlement, which runs south through Timbermaw Hold to Bear's Head.", "Ursolan" },
  ["valormok"] = { "Valormok, a Horde outpost of orc warriors in northwestern Azshara, stands bare and lightly guarded. The Horde took little interest in this hostile land at first. Its warriors endure hard times and keep their hope.\n\nBelgrom Rockmaul trusted the blood elf Magus Rimtori, who promised him riches and powerful relics from Azshara, and sent warriors to guard her. She killed them all. The camp was once a dozen warriors strong.\n\nAg'tor Bloodfist leads what is left and wants vengeance for his dead. He admits that the days here are not the Horde's best or most glorious, but his warriors are young, with blood still in their veins, and he says victory is only a matter of time.", "Valormok" },
}
areas[1448] = {
  ["bloodvenom falls"] = { "Poisoned water spills from the heights at Bloodvenom Falls and feeds the Bloodvenom River. Furbolgs are hardy enough to survive drinking it, but the water only deepens their madness.", "Bloodvenom_Falls" },
  ["bloodvenom post"] = { "A small Horde outpost stands on the high cliffs above Darkshore, near the Bloodvenom River where it cuts through Felwood. Its old architecture and the tauren who guard it suggest that tauren founded it.", "Bloodvenom_Post" },
  ["bloodvenom river"] = { "The Bloodvenom River runs through the middle of Felwood, and its banks crawl with noxious oozes and corrupted beasts. The water is not fit to drink or to swim in. Its taint does not stop at the edge of the forest. Farther on, the river comes down the mountains and becomes the sickened Wildbend River in Darkshore.", "Bloodvenom_River" },
  ["deadwood village"] = { "The furbolgs of the Deadwood tribe hold this village in southern Felwood. Like most of their kin they have been corrupted by the forest's taint, and they attack outsiders on sight.", "Deadwood_Village" },
  ["emerald sanctuary"] = { "Druids of the Emerald Circle keep a camp in southeastern Felwood. They work to snuff out the demonic corruption that spreads from Jaedenar. The tauren Greta Mosshoof is among them, and Grazle, a furbolg of the uncorrupted Timbermaw tribe, stays here with the druids.", "Emerald_Sanctuary" },
  ["felpaw village"] = { "Felpaw Village stands at the far north of Felwood, near the entrance to Timbermaw Hold. It belongs to the Deadwood, furbolgs whose corruption has turned them from their kin, and their taint still marks the ground they hold.\n\nChieftain Bloodmaw lives here among his shamans, avengers and den watchers.", "Felpaw_Village" },
  ["irontree cavern"] = { "Warpwood Shredders and Warpwood Moss Flayers stalk the dark of this deep cave.", "Irontree_Cavern" },
  ["irontree woods"] = { "The Irontree Woods once stood as a peaceful glade in northern Felwood, before the corruption of the Burning Legion twisted it into something else. Crazed treants, twisted bog beasts, ferocious wildlife and corrupted water elementals roam the woods and attack anyone who comes near.", "Irontree_Woods" },
  ["jadefire glen"] = { "In the corrupted woods of southern Felwood, Jadefire Glen shelters a camp of Jadefire satyrs, demonic creatures who have made the tainted hollow their own, thriving amid its rot and spreading the corruption further through the trees around them.", "Jadefire_Glen" },
  ["jadefire run"] = { "The most powerful satyrs in Felwood make their home in this encampment in the north of the forest.", "Jadefire_Run" },
  ["jaedenar"] = { "Jaedenar rises out of ground that once belonged to the night elves, its ruins older than the corruption that now claims them. Beneath it lie two druidic barrow dens, sacred once, and its name was given in worship of Kil'jaeden, the Deceiver.\n\nAfter the Third War the ruins fell to the Shadow Council, led by Lord Banehollow. Its cultists are mostly orc and Forsaken warlocks, and demons serve beside them, among them succubi, felhunters, felguards and satyrs. At its heart stands Shadow Hold, and beside it the Shrine of the Deceiver.", "Jaedenar" },
  ["morlosaran"] = { "Morlos'Aran was once a great night elf settlement, raised long before the Great Sundering. Now it lies in ruin at the southern edge of Felwood.\n\nNight elf ruins of the same kind lie across Darkshore, Ashenvale and Desolace. Once thriving cities, they are now only stone and a reminder of a golden age lost long ago.", "Morlos'Aran" },
  ["ruins of constellas"] = { "Broken columns mark what appears to have been one of the night elf settlements near Mount Hyjal. A moonwell still rests among them, but it no longer holds clean light. Fel energy has soaked into its waters, and Jadefire satyrs hold the ruins. A particularly cruel satyr named Xavathras leads them, and they spread the corruption to any creature they meet.", "Ruins_of_Constellas" },
  ["shadow hold"] = { "A gigantic labyrinth that was once a night elf barrow den lies within Jaedenar, in western Felwood. It is now the Shadow Council's stronghold on Azeroth, and warlocks and their demonic familiars roam its tunnels. The satyr Prince Xavalis and the dreadlord Lord Banehollow both dwell here.", "Shadow_Hold" },
  ["shatter scar vale"] = { "Many infernals fell here when the Burning Legion invaded during the Third War. The craters they left still smolder, and demons still wander the scarred ground.", "Shatter_Scar_Vale" },
  ["shrine of the deceiver"] = { "Warlocks of the Shadow Council fill this corrupted barrow den at Jaedenar, in Felwood. It takes its name from Kil'jaeden, the Deceiver, lord of the Burning Legion.", "Shrine_of_the_Deceiver" },
  ["talonbranch glade"] = { "This small night elf post in northern Felwood is the Alliance's only outpost in the forest. It lies south of Timbermaw Hold, along the path that leads on to Moonglade and Winterspring. It is a night elf house, a moonwell and a few other pieces of elven building.", "Talonbranch_Glade" },
  ["timbermaw hold"] = { "When the corruption of the Burning Legion crept into the sacred forests of Kalimdor, most furbolgs fell under its influence and were twisted into madness. The Timbermaw tribe escaped it. They sheltered in their hold in the northeast of Felwood, and they watched the forest they loved turn into a festering ruin of itself. Even after the demons' leader fell, the taint on the woods remained. Its tunnels open a passage through to Moonglade and Winterspring.\n\nThe Timbermaw are one of the last two tribes of pure furbolgs. They are wary of strangers, fearing they will be mistaken for their corrupted kin and attacked, but they value friends above all else. Their wish is to end the suffering of their corrupted brethren and to find a cure for the wound that Felwood has become, so that the ancient forest might ring again with peaceful chants and soft laughter.", "Timbermaw_Hold" },
}
areas[1449] = {
  ["fire plume ridge"] = { "Fire Plume Ridge rises at the heart of Un'Goro Crater, a mountain of scorched rock where lava creeps down the slopes and fire elementals stir in the heat. Here the jungle ends, scorched away by the lava. The strongest of the fire elementals, Blazerunner, lives in a cave at the peak, master of the mountain's burning heart.\n\nShallow veins of magma run beneath the floor of the crater and heat the steam pools and geysers that hiss throughout Un'Goro, so that the whole basin seems to breathe from the mountain's fire.", "Fire_Plume_Ridge" },
  ["fungal rock"] = { "A large cavern opens in the northeastern wall of Un'Goro Crater. Gorillas have made their home inside, among the ghost mushrooms. Outside the entrance is a small pool with a waterfall, and there stands A-Me 01, the mechanical ape of Karna Remtravel.", "Fungal_Rock" },
  ["golakka hot springs"] = { "Steam rises constantly from Golakka Hot Springs, a stretch of the western crater filled with pools and geysers heated by the nearby volcano. Elder diemetradons and frenzied pterrordax roam around the pools.\n\nA goblin named Krakle has made camp by the springs to study how they are tied to the fires of Fire Plume Ridge.", "Golakka_Hot_Springs" },
  ["ironstone plateau"] = { "Bare rock breaks through the green of Un'Goro in several stretches along the northeastern edge of the crater, tucked against the crater wall. Ironstone Plateau lies quiet compared to the rest of the crater, and no monsters hunt here. Fledgling pterrordax live on it, and its stillness sets it apart from Un'Goro's restless growth.", "Ironstone_Plateau" },
  ["lakkari tar pits"] = { "Lakes of thick, foul-smelling black tar lie in the northern part of Un'Goro Crater. The tar traps creatures in its grip, and the bones of hundreds of beasts jut from its surface. Scavengers loiter nearby looking for easy prey, and tar beasts live in the pits.\n\nRare tarblossom flowers grow at the bottom. They are prized for their beauty and durability, but they lie deep in the tar and are hard to harvest.", "Lakkari_Tar_Pits" },
  ["marshals refuge"] = { "Marshal's Refuge sits at the northern edge of Un'Goro Crater, raised by the Marshal Expeditions under Williden Marshal and Hol'anyee Marshal. Their venture into the crater was not ready for what it found. Un'Goro's beasts proved too savage, and the expedition's base camps were overrun one by one. The survivors fled to higher ground and made camp here, at the crater's edge.\n\nThe refuge is what came of that retreat, a foothold barely kept. It is neutral ground.", "Marshal's_Refuge" },
  ["marshlands"] = { "Standing water and rank growth sprawl across much of the southeastern crater in a vast marsh. Raptors move through the reeds in large numbers, and some of them are poisonous. Blue power crystals are found here and nowhere else in the crater.\n\nOn a large mound stands Torwa Pathfinder, who seems to have come here by canoe from elsewhere in the crater.", "Marshlands" },
  ["slithering scar"] = { "The Gorishi, a sect of the silithid, hold most of the southern part of Un'Goro Crater with their hive. Wasps and workers swarm through it, guarded by reavers, stingers and tunnelers. One of the reavers is known as Clutchmother Zavas.", "Slithering_Scar" },
  ["terror run"] = { "In the southwest of Un'Goro Crater, just west of the Slithering Scar, stegodons, diemetradons, pterrordax and devilsaurs gather in great numbers, and all of them are hostile. Red crystals grow here and nowhere else in the crater.\n\nAlong the eastern edge lies an abandoned research camp that the Marshal Expeditions left behind.", "Terror_Run" },
}
areas[1450] = {
  ["lake eluneara"] = { "Crystal water fills the heart of Moonglade, still as glass beneath the trees. The lake is named for Elune herself.\n\nCenturies ago heroes gathered in Nighthaven to face the maddened demigod Omen. They blinded him and drove him into the lake, and he rests in its depths still, stirring each year around the time of the Lunar Festival.", "Lake_Elune'ara" },
  ["nighthaven"] = { "On the shore of Lake Elune'ara stands Nighthaven, a village of night elf stonework and a pair of moonwells, home to the druids of the Cenarion Circle. It is a quiet, remote place, usually closed to outsiders, its people given over to druidism and the tending of the wild.", "Nighthaven" },
  ["shrine of remulos"] = { "Western Moonglade holds a shrine dedicated to Keeper Remulos, son of the demigod Cenarius and guardian of the valley. Remulos himself keeps his place at the shrine, and the night elf Tajarri lives within.", "Shrine_of_Remulos" },
  ["stormrage barrow dens"] = { "Beneath the quiet turf of Moonglade lie the barrow dens, sunk into earth that many night elf druids never truly leave, their bodies resting while their spirits wander the Emerald Dream.\n\nIt was here that Malfurion Stormrage once slept, and the dens bear his name for it. When Tichondrius and his Dreadlords came in the Third War, they struck at Malfurion where he lay, nearly tearing his den apart. It was Tyrande Whisperwind who woke him to stand against Archimonde and the Burning Legion.\n\nAfterward, when the Cenarion Circle spoke of raising a second World Tree, Malfurion returned to the barrow dens to meditate. Tyrande later came seeking him and found his body fallen still, as though asleep, with no waking it. The Circle judged it wiser not to move him, because if his spirit still wandered the Dream searching for its way home, it would need to find the body where it had left it. So he remains there, watched over by the Cenarion Circle and the Sisterhood of Elune.", "Stormrage_Barrow_Dens" },
  ["timbermaw hold"] = { "The Timbermaw tribe of furbolgs keeps its capital in northeastern Felwood, and a tunnel through the hold leads out to Moonglade and Winterspring. When the corruption of the Burning Legion crept into the sacred forests of Kalimdor, most furbolgs fell under its influence. The Timbermaw escaped it and sheltered in the hold, and they have watched the forest they loved twisted into a festering ruin of itself. Even after the source of that corruption was destroyed, the taint on the woods remained.\n\nThe Timbermaw want an end to the suffering of their corrupted kin, and a cure for the land, so that the ancient forest might one day ring again with chants and laughter.", "Timbermaw_Hold" },
}
areas[1451] = {
  ["bones of grakkarond"] = { "The huge bones of the bronze dragon Grakkarond lie in central Silithus. Ossirian the Unscarred slew Grakkarond in the War of the Shifting Sands. The Anubisath hold the bones sacred.", "Bones_of_Grakkarond" },
  ["bronzebeard encampment"] = { "Rutgar Glyphshaper works at a camp in southern Silithus, east of the Scarab Wall, and asks after the whereabouts of the famous explorer Brann Bronzebeard.", "Bronzebeard_Encampment" },
  ["cenarion hold"] = { "Cenarion Hold stands in Silithus on the ground of an ancient night elf settlement. It is one of the biggest druid strongholds in Kalimdor, almost as large as Moonglade itself. After the War of the Shifting Sands the Cenarion Circle took on the duty of defending it against the silithid and the other forces that grow more hostile in this region, and of guarding the imprisoned Old God C'Thun and its armies.\n\nIt is a hard, embattled place to live, and the silithid press constantly against its walls.", "Cenarion_Hold" },
  ["crystal vale"] = { "In the far northwestern corner of Silithus lies the Crystal Vale, a valley held by earth and air elementals, desert rumblers and dust stormers. Thorium lies thick in its stone. An air elemental called Huricanian patrols the mountain at its southeastern edge.", "Crystal_Vale" },
  ["hiveashi"] = { "A silithid hive lies in northern Silithus, and its brood has spread out to infest the Swarming Pillar. Defenders, sandstalkers, stingers, swarmers and workers fill it, and a scout of the Cenarion Circle watches it from nearby.", "Hive'Ashi" },
  ["hiveregal"] = { "In southern Silithus, the mounds of Hive'Regal rise from the sand, tunnels and chitin thick amid the dust. Ambushers, burrowers, slavemakers, spitfires and hive lords crowd the nest.\n\nBefore the hive camps the Orgrimmar Legion, a battalion of orcs and jungle trolls sent from Orgrimmar to aid Cenarion Hold against the silithid. Krug Skullsplit leads it.", "Hive'Regal" },
  ["hivezora"] = { "Out in western Silithus, a silithid nest lies burrowed into the sand, swarming with wasps, reavers, hive sisters, tunnelers and waywatchers.\n\nThe Ironforge Brigade guards against the hive from an outpost nearby. It is an Alliance force, mostly dwarves of Ironforge with gnomes and humans in its ranks, sent to support Cenarion Hold, and Captain Blackanvil leads it.", "Hive'Zora" },
  ["ortells hideout"] = { "Ortell, a hermit, makes his home in a small cave in southeastern Silithus.", "Ortell's_Hideout" },
  ["ravaged twilight camp"] = { "Highlord Demitrian makes his home in the Ravaged Twilight Camp, set in the Crystal Vale in northwestern Silithus.", "Ravaged_Twilight_Camp" },
  ["scarab dais"] = { "The Scarab Gong stands on a dais in southern Silithus, northwest of the Scarab Wall, outside Ahn'Qiraj.", "Scarab_Dais" },
  ["scarab wall"] = { "Along the southern edge of Silithus, between the silithid-held desert and the ruined city of Ahn'Qiraj, runs the Scarab Wall. It was molded from the sand itself at the end of the War of the Shifting Sands, and its sandstone bulk climbs high above the desert. Where the stone could rise no further, the magic of the bronze dragonflight took over and formed a barrier many times taller still.\n\nThe night elves and bronze dragons who made it named their creation Irana'ethil, Shield against the Shadow. It keeps C'Thun and its crawling minions sealed within.", "Scarab_Wall" },
  ["southwind village"] = { "Southwind Village was a night elf base until the Qiraji swarmed over it in the War of the Shifting Sands. Valstann Staghelm, son of Archdruid Fandral Staghelm, led a detachment of the night elf army to defend it. The Qiraji slaughtered his troops, and General Rajaxx took Valstann captive while the village was torn apart behind him.\n\nThe silithid have since overrun the ruins. The spirits of druids and Sentinels who fell in the slaughter linger among the wreckage, wandering without purpose, bound to the place of their deaths.", "Southwind_Village" },
  ["staghelm point"] = { "A night elf tower crowns a peak in northeastern Silithus. Ralo'shan the Eternal Watcher keeps the post, and a bronze drake of Nozdormu's brood circles the sky above.", "Staghelm_Point" },
  ["swarming pillar"] = { "A large peak in the middle of Silithus, just south of Cenarion Hold, is riddled through with silithid tunnels and filled by a hive. A haze of small silithid circles the stone at all hours, a living cloud that gave the pillar its name. Of the many hives built beneath the sands of Silithus, this is the largest.", "Swarming_Pillar" },
  ["twilight base camp"] = { "The Twilight's Hammer cult keeps four camps across the wastes of Silithus, and this is one of them, a foothold amid the dust and ruin of that scoured land. Twilight Keeper Havunth is stationed here, and wind stones stand in the camp.", "Twilight_Base_Camp" },
  ["twilight outpost"] = { "Twilight Keeper Exeter is stationed at an outpost of the Twilight's Hammer cult in the far southwestern corner of Silithus, just northwest of the Scarab Wall and west of the Bronzebeard Encampment. Wind stones stand in the camp.", "Twilight_Outpost" },
  ["twilight post"] = { "Wind stones stand in a camp the Twilight's Hammer cult holds in the northwest of Silithus, far west of Cenarion Hold and northwest of Twilight Base Camp. Twilight Keeper Mayna is stationed here.", "Twilight_Post" },
  ["twilights run"] = { "Twilight's Run lies hidden in the rock at the base of Staghelm Point, in northern Silithus. A cave there is filled with members of the Twilight's Hammer cult, who have made the hollow in the mountain their own while the desert wind scours the stone above them.", "Twilight's_Run" },
  ["valors rest"] = { "Alliance and Horde alike shelter at Valor's Rest, a small camp at the edge of Silithus where the path out of Un'Goro Crater reaches the desert. Members of the Cenarion Circle tend it, and graves stand quiet at its edge.", "Valor's_Rest" },
}
areas[1452] = {
  ["banthallow barrow den"] = { "The Ban'Thallow Barrow Den lies in the hillside behind Starfall Village. It stands unused and empty.", "Ban'Thallow_Barrow_Den" },
  ["darkwhisper gorge"] = { "When Archimonde fell at the Battle of Mount Hyjal, the demons of the Burning Legion who survived did not vanish. They fled into Darkwhisper Gorge, a network of tunnels and chasms in the far south of Winterspring, and took refuge there. Hederine demons hold it now, seemingly led by Lady Hederine.", "Darkwhisper_Gorge" },
  ["dun mandarr"] = { "Dun Mandarr is a small camp beside an icy cavern in southeastern Winterspring. Jaron Stoneshaper, a dwarf of the Explorers' League, set it up while he hunted for artifacts in the region, until bloodthirsty moonkin chased him out and wrecked it. Ferocious moonkin overrun it now, alongside Winterspring screechers.", "Dun_Mandarr" },
  ["everlook"] = { "Goblins of the Steamwheedle Cartel run Everlook, a trading post where Winterspring's main trade routes cross. It has a goblin laboratory among its buildings, and Everlook Bruisers keep the peace. The Argent Dawn sent Gregor Greystone here to speak for their interests to the Cartel's local governance.\n\nMalyfous Darkhammer of the Thorium Brotherhood, the Dark Iron smiths of Thorium Point, also keeps his trade in the town.", "Everlook" },
  ["frostfire hot springs"] = { "A chain of steaming pools lies at the far western end of Winterspring, warm water in a land of ice. No one has explained why the springs burn hot beneath the snow and ice. Donova Snowden has made camp nearby. She wants to know why the springs appeared and what power they hold, and she suspects demonic interference reaching them from Darkwhisper Gorge in the far south. Furbolgs of the Winterfall tribe live around the pools.", "Frostfire_Hot_Springs" },
  ["frostsaber rock"] = { "At the very top of a large tor in the north of Winterspring stands Rivern Frostwind of the Wintersaber Trainers, keeper of the bond between the frostsabers and those who would ride them. He works with the Alliance, and the beautiful white cats prowl the icy stone below.", "Frostsaber_Rock" },
  ["frostwhisper gorge"] = { "Night elf ruins lie scattered across the floor and ridges of Frostwhisper Gorge, a canyon in southern Winterspring. A great bridge spans the chasm toward Darkwhisper Gorge, just to the south. Huge Frostmaul giants and their Preservers live among the old stones.", "Frostwhisper_Gorge" },
  ["hidden grove"] = { "The Hidden Grove lies sunk in a forested crater in northeastern Winterspring, walled in on every side by high cliffs. A single cave cut into the southeastern ridge is the one way in. A drop from the heights above is possible in places, and very dangerous.\n\nMoonkin hold the grove, berserk and crazed, and they are hostile to any who enter.", "Hidden_Grove" },
  ["ice thistle hills"] = { "The Ice Thistle Hills rise southeast of Everlook, home to the Ice Thistle yeti, who live in and around a cave high on the slopes. Icecap grows across the open slopes outside, and veins of thorium run through the rock inside.", "Ice_Thistle_Hills" },
  ["lake keltheril"] = { "The frozen waters of Lake Kel'Theril lie still in the middle of Winterspring, ringed by old Highborne ruins that carry its name. The tormented spirits that haunt the ruins drift out across the ice as well.", "Lake_Kel'Theril" },
  ["mazthoril"] = { "Deep in the ice of Winterspring, near the middle of the valley, the cavern of Mazthoril opens beneath the earth, vast and thick with magic. Here the blue dragons, sworn to Malygos, keep their lair, and here their matriarch Haleh keeps her solitary watch. She shows herself only as a high elf, at the very top of the cavern, and a portal rune in the floor far below is the way up.\n\nFour powerful drakes guard the lair. Spellmaw patrols a slow path around a third of the lower valley, while Manaclaw and Scryer watch over the runes at the back of the cavern. Azurous ranges through the nearby lands for threats to the forces of Malygos, and a sizable army of sorcerous spawn and young whelps stands behind them.", "Mazthoril" },
  ["moon horror den"] = { "Raging owlbeasts live in the Moon Horror Den, a small cave a short walk northeast of Everlook's gates. Winterfall Village lies close by, but a mountain stands between the two.", "Moon_Horror_Den" },
  ["owl wing thicket"] = { "In the south of Winterspring, a series of caves cuts through the rock, home to owlbeasts gone berserk, moontouched and crazed. Ranshalla stands at the entrance.", "Owl_Wing_Thicket" },
  ["ruins of keltheril"] = { "The Ruins of Kel'Theril lie broken on the shore of the lake that bears their name. Ten thousand years ago a great city of the Highborne stood here. The Great Sundering destroyed it, and it has never been rebuilt.\n\nThe Highborne who died there did not pass on. Their tormented spirits still wander among the fallen columns and stones.", "Ruins_of_Kel'Theril" },
  ["starfall village"] = { "The Alliance holds one settlement in Winterspring, Starfall Village, which stands beside the main road. A night elf lodge and a smaller house make up the village. Jaron Stoneshaper, a dwarf of the Explorers' League, stays here while he hunts the region for artifacts. Winterspring holds powerful relics, he says, most of them heavily guarded.", "Starfall_Village" },
  ["timbermaw hold"] = { "Timbermaw Hold, the capital of the Timbermaw furbolgs, lies in northeastern Felwood, and its tunnel comes out here in Winterspring as well as in Moonglade.\n\nThe furbolgs are wary of all strangers, fearing they will be mistaken for their corrupted brethren and attacked. A stranger who proves a friend finds that they value friends above all else. They own no fine jewels or worldly riches, but their shamanistic tradition is strong, and they know much about crafting armor from animal hides.", "Timbermaw_Hold" },
  ["timbermaw post"] = { "The name says Timbermaw, but the small furbolg camp at Timbermaw Post belongs to the corrupted Winterfall tribe, who hold it completely. It stands southeast across the road from the Frostfire Hot Springs and west of Lake Kel'Theril.", "Timbermaw_Post" },
  ["winterfall village"] = { "High Chief Winterfall rules the corrupted furbolgs of Winterfall Village from a small cave at the top of the settlement. The village climbs the slopes of far eastern Winterspring, near Everlook.", "Winterfall_Village" },
}
areas[1453] = {
  ["champions hall"] = { "In the south of Old Town, the officers of the Alliance keep their barracks in the Champions' Hall. At the back they gather around a table spread with maps of Azeroth, among them Archmage Gaiman of Stormwind and Sergeant Major Skyshadow of Darnassus.", "Champions'_Hall" },
  ["deeprun tram"] = { "After the Second War the dwarves of Ironforge hurried to help their human allies rebuild Stormwind, but King Magni Bronzebeard thought the aid came too slowly. He also wanted a way to send dwarven soldiers to the humans quickly if war returned. He turned to High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque, the finest engineer on Azeroth, who drew up a railway under the earth to link the two capitals.\n\nThe builders ran into a subterranean lake and then a plague of rats. The tram was finished anyway. Gnomes work on the line every day, and the rats have never quite gone.", "Deeprun_Tram" },
  ["valley of heroes"] = { "A stone bridge crosses the moat before Stormwind's gate and leads into a lush green valley. Five great statues stand along the road. Stormwind raised them after the Second War, as the city was rebuilt, for the leaders of the Alliance expedition that passed through the Dark Portal to seal it from the far side.\n\nKurdran Wildhammer, Thane of Aerie Peak, and the Archmage Khadgar of the Kirin Tor stand on the left. Force Commander Danath Trollbane and Ranger-Captain Alleria Windrunner stand on the right. At the end of the road, where it splits to enter the city, is General Turalyon, who led them all. None of the five came back.\n\nGeneral Marcus Jonathan, High Commander of Stormwind's defense, keeps his post in the valley.", "Valley_of_Heroes" },
}
areas[1454] = {
  ["hall of legends"] = { "The Horde's officers keep their barracks in the Hall of Legends. Around a table in the middle, spread with maps of Azeroth, stand Chieftain Earthbind of Thunder Bluff, Advisor Willington of the Undercity and Blood Guard Hini'wana of the Darkspear trolls.", "Hall_of_Legends" },
  ["ragefire chasm"] = { "Beneath Orgrimmar the earth opens into a chasm of lava and smoke. Troggs have begun to climb out of the deep tunnels, a clan the Horde calls the Ragefire troggs. Magatha the Seer, of the tauren, tried to make peace with them and met only hostility. These creatures have no interest in talking, and left alone they could overwhelm the whole Horde.\n\nA sect of the Shadow Council, the Searing Blade, has also taken refuge in the chasm. Taragaman the Hungerer, a felguard, leads it alongside the warlock Jergosh the Invoker and the satyr Bazzalan. From their hiding place they plot to overthrow all that the Horde has built in these lands.", "Ragefire_Chasm" },
}
areas[1455] = {
  ["deeprun tram"] = { "Two sets of three wagons run on double tracks between Ironforge and Stormwind, passing partly under the water of a subterranean lake. High Tinker Gelbin Mekkatorque, the most esteemed engineer in Azeroth, drew up the line for King Magni Bronzebeard, who wanted a way to send dwarven soldiers to the humans if they were ever needed. Rats overran the tunnels while it was dug, and gnomes work on it every day to keep it safe.", "Deeprun_Tram" },
  ["gates of ironforge"] = { "The Gates of Ironforge rise where the great ramp of central Dun Morogh climbs to meet the mountain, a grand sight visible for miles across the snow. They stand as the threshold between the open dwarven lands and Ironforge proper.\n\nInside stands a huge statue of High King Modimus Anvilmar, set to guard the entrance so that none enter the city without passing beneath his gaze.", "Gates_of_Ironforge" },
}
areas[1456] = {
  ["elder rise"] = { "The Council of Elders governs from Elder Rise, which lies directly east of the central bluff, and the Cenarion Circle makes its home here among the tauren druids and priests. Arch Druid Hamuul Runetotem lives on the rise, a childhood friend of Cairne Bloodhoof. The hut between the two bridges belongs to Magatha Grimtotem, who leads the Grimtotem tribe.", "Elder_Rise" },
  ["hunter rise"] = { "The southern rise of Thunder Bluff belongs to those who hunt and fight. Trappers, trackers and tanners work beside the renowned Hunter's Hall, where the tauren train their warriors and hunters, and training dummies stand outside for warriors to practice on.", "Hunter_Rise" },
  ["pools of vision"] = { "In a tunnel system just below the cap of Spirit Rise, the Forsaken keep a foothold deep in tauren country. Their mages and priests gather around the Pools of Vision, which steam beneath the mesa.", "Pools_of_Vision" },
  ["spirit rise"] = { "Shamans study on Spirit Rise, northwest of the central bluff, and the Skychaser tribe holds great influence here. The steamy Pools of Vision lie below the surface of the mesa.", "Spirit_Rise" },
}
areas[1457] = {
  ["cenarion enclave"] = { "In the northwest of Darnassus, the Cenarion Enclave rings a grove where oaks and ashes stand taller than the halls of stone elsewhere in the city. Every tree bears mystic runes shaped from its own bark. Within the circle, the druids gather in structures molded from living wood and carefully shaped stone, and the largest, at the heart of the grove, is the home of Archdruid Fandral Staghelm. The Enclave takes its name from Cenarius, the ancient demigod, and treants called Darnassian Protectors guard it in place of Sentinels.\n\nThis is the seat of the city's druids, just as the Temple of the Moon is the seat of its priests. Druids learn the old ways here, hunters train in its easternmost tree, and a secretive guild of rogues keeps its quarters below ground, known only to those who already know where to look.", "Cenarion_Enclave" },
  ["craftsmens terrace"] = { "The Craftsmen's Terrace lies in the north of Darnassus. Most of the city's craftspeople keep their halls and workshops here, alchemists and tailors, leatherworkers and enchanters, cooks and healers, and travelers find a room at Innkeeper Saelienne's inn, one of the city's main inns. An embassy of the Argent Dawn also stands here, a foreign presence within the elven city.\n\nTelonis, the master leatherworker, says the grass is soft and the air full of light and song, and that everything he has seen tells him Teldrassil is a dream come true. He cannot say why his heart is full of foreboding.", "Craftsmen's_Terrace" },
  ["temple gardens"] = { "At the heart of Darnassus lie the Temple Gardens, an expanse of water, walkways and small islands named for the Temple of the Moon that rises just to the south. Bridges lead out in every direction, so that all roads through the city seem to gather here. The islets hold some of the finest plants in the city, and the night elves tend the waters to keep them so. Tyrande Whisperwind has meditated here.\n\nIn the far west of the gardens a portal leads down to Rut'theran Village at the foot of the World Tree.", "Temple_Gardens" },
  ["tradesmens terrace"] = { "Most of the shopkeepers and merchants of Darnassus keep to the Tradesmen's Terrace in the south of the city. It is the center of commerce for the night elves, and the city's auction house is here. Its buildings are grown of wood, in the manner of the night elves, light and graceful things.", "Tradesmen's_Terrace" },
  ["warriors terrace"] = { "The eastern gate of Darnassus, where the city opens onto the wilds of Teldrassil, stands on the Warrior's Terrace, also called the Hall of Justice. The Sentinels keep their ground here, and patrols on nightsabers guard the gates. Night elves of military rank train on its target ranges and dueling grounds, and the terrace is the city's shield against whatever comes up the road from the forest.", "Warrior's_Terrace" },
}
areas[1458] = {
  ["ruins of lordaeron"] = { "Above the buried city the palace of Lordaeron still stands, its halls open to the sky and its gardens gone to ruin. Prince Arthas Menethil came home to it at the head of an army, welcomed through the gates by bells and cheering before anyone understood what he had become. In the throne room he killed his father, King Terenas, and the Scourge destroyed the capital. The kingdom fell.\n\nThe ruins have kept the sound of that day. Bells clang in the entryway, a faint cheer welcomes the prince, and voices murmur in the throne room, where Arthas is heard declaring that the kingdom will fall and a new order will rise from its ashes. The ghosts of Lordaeron's citizens still haunt the courtyard, and darkhounds and duskbats prowl beyond the walls.", "Ruins_of_Lordaeron" },
}

